{"id": "gdromcondavail", "state": "open", "commits": "", "hold": "", "witness": "none -- a state-consistency defect; its oracle is what the device ADVERTISES", "title": "gdromcondavail  COND_DATA_AVAIL permanently lies after an overread -- the device advertises data while returning 0xffff (split out of gdromcondlies)", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "entries": [{"seat": "grok", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-24", "note": "SPLIT OUT of gdromcondlies, which named two defects at once. Original record at OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:7575-7587, a residual of #460: once a guest reads past the buffer, COND_DATA_AVAIL stays SET while every subsequent read returns 0xffff, announced once. Pre-fix that inconsistency was unmissable because the same read ended the host -- so the de-escalation made the state defect QUIETER without removing it, which is the class de-escalation always creates. Clearing COND_DATA_AVAIL at exhaustion would repair it, and was deliberately out of scope for #460 because changing what a device ADVERTISES is a behaviour change with its own oracle. Does NOT join the BADVAL round: different class, different oracle, different fix design."}]}
{"id": "asicindexcase", "title": "asicindexcase  the deleted guard was ALSO a bounds check, so an INDEXING case added at an r==3 offset is now a SILENT out-of-bounds access", "state": "open", "commits": "", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "hold": "#463b FINAL FORM, ADJUDICATED AT 9/9 WITH NO MARKERS. Ships a SILENT FALL-THROUGH decline. The LATCH is REFUTED, not deferred. Budget: the guarded arm, the r==3 row value upgrade, one contract sentence. Not run.", "witness": "none -- LATENT; the invariant is verified TODAY (max r = 2 over 12 labels)", "state_note": "*** THE THREE ASIC ROWS REST ON ONE INVARIANT, AND TWO OF THEM MAY BE ONE FIX. Scoped 2026-08-26 by reading, no run. ***\n\n**THE SHARED INVARIANT: `r == 3` is unreachable by any implemented case.** VERIFIED by reading the switch labels -- they are three CONSECUTIVE words at group bases `0x00/0x10/0x20/0x30`, so `r = (relative_addr / 4) & 3` yields 0, 1, 2 and never 3. All three rows are that one invariant, differing only in **which edit would break it**:\n\n    asiceventaddr    a #define in the vendored HEADER (event >= 96 -> offset 0x0c)\n    asicindexcase    an indexing `case` added at an r==3 offset in the DEVICE FILE\n    asicoffsetkill   an adversarial MUTANT at an r==3 offset no detector row touches\n\n**THE THIRD IS DIFFERENT IN KIND and should not be folded with the other two:** it is a DETECTOR-COVERAGE question, not a product invariant. `kill far` (+0xfc) was added to defeat a GROUP-NARROWING mutant and cannot defeat a SINGLE-OFFSET one; the seat that raised it said plainly it did not build it.\n\n*** THE FINDING THAT MAY FOLD THE OTHER TWO, and it contradicts `asiceventaddr`'s own stated reason for being a separate row: *** that row argues a device-file comment \"cannot govern a `#define` added to a header\". **True of a COMMENT. Not true of a GUARD.** An event `>= 96` does not stay in the header -- it becomes an ADDRESS the driver accesses, arriving at the device as `relative_addr == 0x0c` and decoding to `r == 3`. **So a guard on `r` in the device file would catch the header entry point too**, because the header case MANIFESTS as a device-file access.\n\nIf that holds, one guard covers both rows and the vendored-header edit -- the expensive part, on a file this fork has touched once -- may be unnecessary. **Stated as a question rather than a conclusion because I have not built it**, and the adjudicator was asked the licence question on the header assumption; this finding is being sent to it as a correction rather than acted on unilaterally.\n\nSTOP CONDITION: if the guard-covers-both reading holds, fold `asiceventaddr` into `asicindexcase` as one fix with two entry points and close it as a duplicate; if it does not, the rows stay separate and the header licence question stands.\n\nNAMED BY THE FABLE ADJUDICATION AND BY minimax, INDEPENDENTLY (_scratchpad/panel_20260825_020219). `r == 3` is out of range for `uint32_t[3]`, so `if (r == 3) exit(1)` was doing TWO jobs and #463 reasoned about only one. Before #463 the switch had NEVER ONCE executed with r == 3. It is safe today only because r == 3 lands on `default:`, whose body indexes nothing -- ENUMERATED, not asserted: all twelve labels give max r = 2. **But #463 also deleted the tripwire that made this self-announcing**, so a future edit adding an indexing case at a +0x0c-class offset is a silent OOB that no detector row sees. Deliberately NOT fixed in-round: it is outside the DEPENDS-ON enumeration (it adds a predicate rather than weakening one the fix depends on), so fixing it in-round would be the runaway chain. Closing options: extend the arrays to [4] (see `asicarray4`), or ship `if (r == 3) return;` as a bounds-safe decline.  *** ENRICHED 2026-08-25 FROM #463'S RESEARCH STAGE, which was fired to close a panel gap AFTER the round had committed -- the owner was offered an exemption and DECLINED it. *** (a) THE SURVIVING ARGUMENT, credited to the kimi seat and the only one that survives contact with the file: the fix insured brand-new state with a COMMENT where one line would insure it with CONTROL FLOW. It is an ADDITION, not a reversal -- 'keep the deletion; add the line'. (b) THE CORRECTED FORM, and it matters because the obvious form is WRONG: a bare `if (r == 3) return;` SKIPS the function's common tail (`if (writeflag == MEM_READ) memory_writemax64(cpu, data, len, odata); return 1;`), handing a guest read an untouched buffer. **FOUR readers independently proposed that broken line -- deepseek, minimax, kimi AND THE ADJUDICATOR ITSELF** -- from packets that showed the guard and the switch but not the tail. The correct form declines while leaving `odata` at 0 and reaching the common tail. (c) IN-TREE PRECEDENT nobody had cited: `memory_rw.c:495-523`, in the file that DISPATCHES to this device, bounds a guest-drivable diagnostic to 8 emissions (`n_trunc_msgs`) because 'probing at the end of a device's mapped range is common and benign'. The fork's own answer to this phenomenon is BOUND THE COUNT, KEEP THE MESSAGE -- so a latched variant has precedent the round never weighed. (d) THE ANCHOR PROPERTY, verified arithmetically: an explicit decline pins the DECLINE COLUMN at `(addr & 0x0c) == 0x0c`, so a translation MOVES it onto a probed offset -- `+1` reddens `safe 8`, `+2` reddens `safe 4` (the residual that escapes even `mask9 rt`), `-1` reddens `asic ok`. The whole translation class would close with one line and rows already shipped. (e) PRECONDITION for shipping it: a 13-mutant x 13-row table re-measured byte-identical with the line in, and `mask9 rt` KEPT -- it is the only anchor surviving if the line is later deleted, and the two catch the class through different channels. *** AND A CORRECTION TO THE ADJUDICATION THAT ORDERED THIS ENRICHMENT. *** It counted five layers protecting this hazard, one being 'four measured kill rows asserting survive-plus-DECLINE-VALUE at r==3 offsets', concluding a future `case 0x0c:` assigning visible data would redden the detector. **MEASURED FALSE BY READING THE PROBE**: `kill word`, `kill byte`, `kill store` and `kill far` all compare `'survived' if alive else 'died'` against `'survived'` -- SURVIVE-ONLY, no value check. Such a case would keep them GREEN. That layer does not exist; the other four (verified enumeration, the in-code comment, this filed item, the contract's disclosure) stand. The adjudicator wrote 'the dissent read the diff, it did not read the detector' in the same answer -- it had descriptions rather than the source, and the opus5 seat, which DID read it, had already reported the rows as survive-only. **This is `asickillsurvive`, and it has now caused a wrong claim by an adjudicator, which is a sharper argument for fixing it than the original filing made.**  *** #463b FULLY SPECIFIED 2026-08-25 BY ADJUDICATION. Ships decline+latch, not a bare decline. *** THE FORM: `if (r == 3)` declines with a ONE-SHOT LATCHED `fatal()` and an explicit `memory_writemax64` so the read path writes the defined 0 -- early-return, which makes a future `+0x0c`-class label DEAD CODE BY CONSTRUCTION and CLOSES THIS ROW. Per-instance struct field, not a `static`, because the device is multi-instance. WHY LATCHED RATHER THAN BARE: the loss is MEASURED, not aesthetic -- #463's finding 5 established the deleted `fatal()` printed unconditionally while the surviving `debug()` is silenced by `-q` and by free-running at verbose 0. **Code that does not make the trade beats prose confessing it.** And the latch rebuilds exactly the property the de-escalation clause mourned: under a future `+1`-style decode defect every legal `+0x?8` access lands in the decline column, and the first 8 announce -- field-time announcement complementing the gate-time detector, with no host kill and no flood. THE ROW BUDGET, and it is bounded ON PRINCIPLE rather than by restraint: (1) **UPGRADE the four r==3 rows' oracle** to assert the returned value is the defined 0. They are survive-only today; they already READ, so this only adds the assertion. This discharges an adjudicator condition open since the FIRST adjudication ('confirm the kill rows check the decline VALUE, not survival alone'), never discharged, and now load-bearing because the candidates differ exactly in decline-column semantics. (2) **ONE BOUND ROW**: the fix now ADDS a predicate (`n < 8`), so clause (d) / accept-failability owes a row that reddens when the bound is REMOVED -- count <= 8 occurrences across the probe's 64 r==3 accesses. **Pin the COUNT, not the message text.** (3) One contract sentence. EXPECTED EQUIVALENCE, recorded in advance so it is not mistaken for an escape: the decline-nulled mutant (`if (r == 3)` kept, fall-through restored) will likely measure EQUIVALENT at base, because `default:` declines identically -- the #449 pattern, an equivalence RESULT and not a failure. MEASUREMENT DISCIPLINE: base is an INVARIANCE CONTROL, never a discriminator -- all candidates are guest-identical at base and a detector should pin CONTRACT, not implementation. The evidence is entirely in the mutant deltas, and the translation mutants must go STRICTLY REDDER (`+1` -> safe 8, `+2` -> safe 4, `-1` -> asic ok; arithmetic verified). **Base must be RE-RUN on the latched build, not inherited** -- 8 new `fatal()` lines enter the pty logs, and `gate_hygiene` GRADES pty logs, which is a known trap class here: a new diagnostic entering graded logs must be shown green, not assumed. CLOSE-OUT: full 13-mutant table on the latched build, pass 2 on the real diff, targeted gate, one commit, and then #463 and #463b are OVER -- the next dissent on this device buys a new round or a filed row, never a reopening.  *** FINAL FORM 2026-08-25: A SILENT FALL-THROUGH DECLINE. THE LATCH IS DEAD. *** `if (r == 3) { /* comment */ } else switch (...)` -- a guarded arm joining the EXISTING epilogue, NOT an early return. That form uses the tail's own `memory_writemax64` so nothing is duplicated, and it does not lean on the dispatcher pre-zeroing read buffers (`memory_rw.c:485`) -- codex's objection answered STRUCTURALLY rather than by trusting a non-local guarantee. *** WHY THE LATCH DIED, on two independent closures either of which is fatal. *** (1) THE PREDICATE IS EMPTY WHERE IT MATTERS: implemented registers occupy words 0-2 of all four groups (documentarily grounded -- Linux mach-dreamcast and KallistiOS both show three words per group, no fourth), so every r==3 lane in modelled space sits directly above an implemented word-2 register and IS the second lane of a legal 8-byte access. 'Latch only on offsets that are not a lane of a legal multi-word access' selects the EMPTY SET -- a dead guard, and this round has refused three of those already. (2) THE INFORMATION IS ABSENT, NOT MERELY AWKWARD: the decoder issues two independent 32-bit accesses, so at the handler **the second half of a legal fmov.d and a direct probe of a reserved word are BYTE-IDENTICAL CALLS.** No predicate written inside this function separates them at any complexity. A dispatcher-layer latch, where transaction context exists, is a different change to a different file -- out of scope. *** AND IT REWRITES THIS ROUND'S FINDING 5: the deleted `fatal()` did not merely print unconditionally, it printed ON LEGAL TRAFFIC -- the second half of every 8-byte access to an implemented register. The 'self-announcing property' the latch was meant to restore was PARTLY A FALSE-ALARM PROPERTY ALL ALONG, so the trade the deletion made was SMALLER than finding 5 claimed and smaller than the latch ruling claimed on top of it. *** THE COMMENT MUST CARRY THE INOCULATION, one line: *silent by design -- r==3 lanes occur inside legitimate split accesses (fmov.d, SQ), so any diagnostic here false-alarms.* That sentence is what stops the next reader re-deriving the latch. WARRANT, THREE INDEPENDENT LEGS: the measured anchor (`+1`->safe 8, `+2`->safe 4 closing the one mutant that escaped everything, `-1`->asic ok); enforcement by control flow instead of enumeration; and the UB upgrade -- a broken invariant buys UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR a compiler may exploit, not a defined overlap, which makes the decline the only C-meaningful statement of the bound in executable form. DETECTOR DELTA: **the bound-count row DIES WITH THE LATCH** -- it was the latch's own accept-failability obligation, and no predicate means no row. The r==3 value upgrade stays."}
{"id": "gdrompiooddlen", "title": "gdrompiooddlen  the GDROM PIO read guards ONE byte and then reads TWO -- LATENT, because no path can produce an odd length today", "state": "open", "commits": "", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "hold": "", "witness": "none, and none is possible today -- the OWED reachability measurement was done BY READING and came back NEGATIVE. The code asymmetry is real; the route to it is not. See the correction below.", "state_note": "*** FOUND 2026-08-25 WHILE VERIFYING A PREMISE IN THE `gdrompionull` PASS-1 BRIEF, NOT WHILE LOOKING FOR IT. *** The brief's Q4 asked, almost as an afterthought, whether `d->cur_data_offset` is reset anywhere -- because the round had reasoned about `data_len` throughout and barely looked at the guard's OTHER operand. It is reset (`:327`, beside the NULL). But reading the guard to check that showed something else. *** THE GUARD CHECKS ONE BYTE AND THE BODY READS TWO. *** Verified by reading, three lines:   (1) `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:611` -- `if (d->cur_data_offset < d->data_len) {`   (2) `:612-613` -- `odata = d->data[d->cur_data_offset ++];` then `odata |= (d->data[d->cur_data_offset ++] << 8);` -- **two increments, one bounds test**   (3) `alloc_data` `:309` -- `malloc(d->data_len)`, **exactly `data_len` bytes, no padding, no rounding**. So at `cur_data_offset == data_len - 1` the guard PASSES, the first read is the last valid byte, and **the second read is `d->data[data_len]` -- one byte past the allocation**. *** REACHABILITY, STATED AS WHAT IS READ AND WHAT IS OWED. *** READ: `data_len = d->cnt` (`:307`), and `d->cnt` is GUEST-WRITABLE A BYTE AT A TIME -- `:704` sets the low byte from a CNTLO store and `:712` the high byte from CNTHI, so an ODD count is expressible. The two subsequent adjustments do not remove oddness: `:409-410` only replaces `cnt` when it is ZERO, and `:486-487` only rewrites the single value 65536. **OWED: a measurement that an odd `cnt` actually survives to `alloc_data` on a path a guest can drive.** The existing `gdrom_pio_null_probe.py` already stages commands through these registers and could answer it with two extra stores; I have NOT run it, and this row does not claim the defect is reachable until that runs. *** WHY THIS IS A DIFFERENT DEFECT FROM `gdrompionull` AND MUST NOT BE FOLDED INTO IT. *** That row is `data` NULL while `data_len` lies -- a state-invariant violation whose fix is one assignment in the tail. This is an OFF-BY-ONE in a bounds test, on a path where `data` is a perfectly valid pointer and `data_len` is honest. Different fix design (`< data_len` becomes `+ 1 < data_len`, or the read becomes width-aware), different oracle (a heap over-read, not a SIGSEGV), so under the reopening rule they are separate rounds. **Note the two interact in the safe direction: `gdrompionull`'s fix sets `data_len = 0`, under which this guard is false and the over-read cannot occur on THAT path.** It does nothing for the odd-count path. *** AND UNLIKE THIS DEVICE'S OTHER OUT-OF-BOUNDS ISSUES, ASan SHOULD SEE THIS ONE. *** The file's own comment at `:272` records that ASan cannot detect its member-to-member overrun because that is intra-object inside one malloc. **This is a read PAST a malloc'd buffer's end**, which is the case ASan is built for -- so `build-asan/` is a real second oracle here, where for the aliasing family it was not. That is worth testing early because it is cheap and because a positive ASan result would settle reachability and severity in one run. SEVERITY, stated honestly and NOT inflated: a ONE-BYTE heap over-READ. It does not write, and the byte it returns goes to the guest as the high half of a 16-bit read. That is an information leak of one adjacent heap byte per odd-length transfer and a potential crash only if the allocation ends exactly on a page boundary. It is materially less severe than the SIGSEGV in `gdrompionull` and should rank below it.  *** REACHABILITY SETTLED 2026-08-25, THE SAME DAY THIS ROW WAS FILED, AND IT SETTLED AGAINST THE ROW. *** The filing said the odd-count route was OWED and explicitly did NOT claim the defect was reachable. That was the right caution, and the answer is NO. *** `data_len` HAS EXACTLY ONE WRITER, AND BOTH ROUTES TO IT FORCE AN EVEN VALUE. *** Verified by grepping every assignment: `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:307` in `alloc_data` (`d->data_len = d->cnt;`) is the only one -- the apparent second hit at `:413` is a printf FORMAT STRING, not an assignment, which is the kind of match that would have made this look open if counted rather than read. `alloc_data` has exactly two call sites and each is guarded:   * `:342`, the Read-TOC path, is preceded by `:338-341` -- `if (d->cnt != 408) { fatal(...); exit(1); }`. **408 is even**, and nothing else reaches this call.   * `:418`, the read-sectors path, is preceded by `:412-415` -- `if (sector_count * 2048 != d->cnt) { fatal(...); break; }`. **Any multiple of 2048 is even.** Note `:409-410` only substitutes a count when `cnt == 0`, so a guest-set odd count survives THAT line -- and is then rejected by the equality check two lines later. **So the guest CAN express an odd `cnt` at the CNTLO/CNTHI registers (:704, :712), and that half of the filing was right; what it cannot do is get that value past either guard into `alloc_data`.** *** DISPOSITION: LATENT, NOT LIVE. SEVERITY DROPS FROM A ONE-BYTE HEAP OVER-READ TO A STRUCTURAL HAZARD WITH NO ROUTE. *** The asymmetry itself is untouched and still worth recording: `:611` guards `cur_data_offset < data_len` and `:612-613` reads TWO bytes, while `alloc_data` mallocs exactly `data_len` with no padding. **Any future edit that admits an odd length -- a new command arm, a relaxed guard, a different transfer size -- makes this live immediately, and the code carries nothing that would stop it.** That is a real thing to know and a cheap thing to fix (`+ 1 < data_len`, or make the read width-aware), but it is NOT a defect a guest can reach today and this row may not be ranked as one. *** AND THE ASan NOTE IN THE FILING IS NOW MOOT AND IS WITHDRAWN. *** It said `build-asan/` would be a real second oracle here, unlike the intra-object aliasing family. True in principle and irrelevant in fact: ASan cannot observe an access no execution reaches. *Process note worth keeping: this row was filed with reachability marked OWED rather than asserted, and the owed check refuted it within the hour -- at the cost of two greps. Had it been filed as a live heap over-read it would have been a WRONG RECORD ranked above real work, and the correction would have arrived later and cost more.*"}
{"id": "asickillsurvive", "title": "asickillsurvive  #463's four kill rows are SURVIVE-ONLY, so an OOB read returning garbage would score green", "state": "open", "commits": "", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "hold": "", "witness": "none -- the rows exist and pass; this is about what they OBSERVE", "state_note": "RAISED BY THE FABLE ADJUDICATION (_scratchpad/panel_20260825_020219). `kill word`, `kill byte`, `kill store` and `kill far` compare 'survived' against 'survived' -- they prove LIVENESS but do not check the value returned. A mutant that made an r==3 read return garbage WITHOUT crashing would keep them green. **A disagreement worth recording rather than silently resolving:** the seat called this a witness-ladder violation. The ladder's clause (ii) says *the witness* must carry a liveness control AND a device-signature control, and it does -- `live` and `asic ok` are separate rows doing exactly that. So this is coverage debt, not a ladder breach, and it is filed on that reading. Cheap to close: the rows already issue `print r2`, so the value is captured and unused -- the oracle can become 'survived 0x0' at the cost of re-measuring the table.  *** PROMOTED 2026-08-25: this is not a bare residual, it is WHY A FULL RESEARCH PANEL COULD NOT BE REPLACED BY RE-RUNNING THE GATE. *** The opus5 research seat established the consequence: all four rows touching r==3 offsets (`kill word` +0x0c, `kill byte` +0x0d, `kill store`, `kill far` +0xfc) are SURVIVE-ONLY, and every value-checking row touches only r in {0,1,2}. **Therefore the 13-row detector cannot distinguish the shipped fix from a bare decline, from a decline-plus-latch, or from a latched diagnostic -- all four leave it 13/13.** So 'the detector is green' was NO EVIDENCE AT ALL for the disposition the research stage was asked to review. The detector pins the kill's absence and register distinctness; it pins nothing whatever about reserved-space semantics."}
{"id": "gdromdmawidth", "title": "gdromdmawidth  the DMA tail store ignores `len`: an ALIGNED byte or 16-bit write to an implemented offset clobbers all 32 bits of the shadow register", "state": "open", "commits": "029a137", "hold": "", "witness": "none committed -- MEASURED 2026-08-23 at rung 3 via a scratchpad probe (reported, not pinned): the row is DELIBERATELY not shipped, because pinning 0x000000ff would enshrine this model's own behaviour as an oracle", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "entries": [{"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "Pre-existing upstream, untouched by #461, found by the pass-2 measuring seat while building mutants against the alias fix. NOT joined: #461's semantic class is 'unimplemented offset'; this is 'implemented offset, wrong width' -- a different fix design (read-modify-write honouring len, which raises an endianness question the default arm never has to answer) and a different oracle, so the round-scope rule makes it a separate round. *** IT IS THE SAME FAMILY AS THE ALIAS #461 DID FIX, AND #461's NEW RULE DOES NOT COVER IT: *** 'alignment alone separates unimplemented word from misaligned access to an implemented one' is true and says nothing about an ALIGNED NARROW write to an implemented offset. A future reader applying that rule here would conclude it is safe. Related to [hlen], but the OPPOSITE direction: hlen is a wide access spanning registers, this is a narrow access clobbering one. `dma seed` is one store away from being a witness."}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "*** WITNESS CONSTRUCTIBLE AT RUNG 3, contrary to `witness: none`. Verified by READING the arm; flagged as a reading, since a probe run would have contended with a live gate. *** The carrier's rule is that 'no witness of either kind' is a CHECKABLE claim and must be checked rather than asserted. Checked: `len` appears exactly THREE times in DEVICE_ACCESS(dreamcast_gdrom_dma), and all three are byte marshalling -- memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len) on the way in and two memory_writemax64(..., len, ...) on the way out. The store itself, at dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:889 (re-derived 2026-08-23), is `d->dma_reg[relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)] = idata;` -- a full 32-bit assignment with no width discrimination anywhere. THE WITNESS IS `dma seed` PLUS ONE STORE, exactly as the filing predicted: 32-bit store 0x11223344 at DMA+0x04, then a BYTE store of 0xff at DMA+0x04 (ALIGNED, so it matches `case 0x04` and is NOT the #461 alias), then read +0x04 back. Real hardware leaves the upper 24 bits; this model yields 0x000000ff. Ordinary guest instructions through real address decode on a committed machine -- rung 3, no new device and no machine edit, so no laundering. NO BUILD NEEDED EITHER: the defect is pre-existing upstream and untouched by #461, so it is in the current binary."}, {"seat": "grok", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] *** FOUND THE DECISIVE PRECEDENT, AND IT IS IN THIS VERY FILE FROM THE PREVIOUS ROUND. *** dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:515-522, written by #460: 'DO NOT \"EMULATE\" NARROW ACCESS INSTEAD. A pass-1 panel split on that, and it was MEASURED ACTIVELY HARMFUL: letting cmd_count step by 1 makes eleven byte writes plus one word write go 11 -> 13, SKIPPING the == 12 equality, so busy&0x08 never clears and the next write reaches the \"Too much GDROM_DATA\" exit -- otherwise unreachable. It also writes cmd[12], one past the array.' VERIFIED BY READING. Option A is exactly that shape, ruled out by the file's own measured history one round ago. Its reachability line is the right distinction too: 'Reachable as a guest instruction; unobserved as a driver path. That is not Option C.' -- rung-3 reachability is established, driver reachability is not, and the ladder says a witness makes an item ELIGIBLE while severity orders it."}, {"seat": "agy", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] Found the OTHER in-tree precedent, independently: sh4_bsc16_report() at dev_sh4.c:1646, the tree's existing handling for a narrow access to a wide register. Its conclusion: 'in-tree precedent unequivocally supports Option B (decline partial/narrow writes with a latched warn-once diagnostic)'. Two seats finding two different precedents that agree is the strongest form this panel produces -- the answer was already in the tree twice and the brief invented a three-way choice that the tree had effectively made."}, {"seat": "codex", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] Supplied the discriminating row and the comment amendment, both adopted. The row -- W32 +0x04 = 0x11223344, then W8 +0x04 = 0xff, then R32 +0x04 -- separates all three options: A gives 0x112233ff little-endian (0xff223344 big-endian), B gives 0x11223344 preserved, C gives 0x000000ff. Its amendment text is the one to use: it SEPARATES ADDRESS DECODING FROM WIDTH VALIDATION, so #461's alignment rule cannot be misread as proof that implemented cases are width-safe."}, {"seat": "deepseek", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] Reached codex's discriminating row independently, with the same three expected values including the endianness split on Option A. Its amendment wording is the crisper of the two: 'An aligned offset reaching the default arm is genuinely an unimplemented word. This does not imply that an aligned narrow access to an IMPLEMENTED offset is safe; narrow accesses to implemented registers are a separate width question and are currently not width-discriminated.'"}, {"seat": "glm", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] Option B with the clearest cost/benefit statement: one branch on `len != 4` before the tail store; consistent with #461's decline-rather-than-corrupt philosophy; establishes the missing in-tree precedent for the WIDTH axis without committing to Option A's endianness-dependent merge; and it 'turns an unreachable silent-clobber into an unreachable LOUD no-op'. Explicitly ranked B over C: 'B is C plus a guard for the same engineering cost.'"}, {"seat": "minimax", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] *** THE ONLY SEAT THAT REFUSED TO PRETEND THE REACHABILITY QUESTION WAS SETTLED, and it is right. *** 'Probably not in well-behaved guest code, but the brief does not let us prove a negative.' It also caught a trap in the brief's own framing: adding the witness row makes the defect reachable IN CI, which 'is not the same as reachable' by a guest driver -- a distinction this project's witness ladder makes and the brief blurred. Its answer is correctly CONDITIONAL rather than confident."}, {"seat": "kimi", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] Went furthest on the endianness question the brief called the real technical risk, working out what memory_readmax64 does to a narrow value and where a byte write at +0x04 lands in dma_reg[1]. Its answer is the reason Option A is unsafe to implement from the brief alone: the fact is derivable but was NOT established in the tree, and SH-4 runs either endianness."}, {"seat": "fable5", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "*** VERDICT: OPTION C, OVERTURNING A UNANIMOUS 7-OF-7 PANEL FROM SOURCE. *** The panel converged on Option B (decline narrow writes). This seat fetched mainline Linux drivers/cdrom/gdrom.c and read it line by line: :622 `__raw_writeb(1, GDROM_DMA_STATUS_REG)` is the DMA TRIGGER -- a BYTE WRITE to this window's +0x18 on every transfer -- and :618 byte-READS the same offset to poll for completion, while the four value registers at :585-589 are always __raw_writel. *** SO OPTION B WOULD BREAK A BOOTING LINUX, *** and the width-blind code delivers exactly the semantics the one real narrow user needs. The defect's actual content -- clobbering a value register's upper bits -- sits in cells no known guest reaches. It also checked NetBSD (never touches the DMA window) and promemul/dreamcast.c (zero references), and was explicit that KallistiOS widths are BELIEVED only. *** AND IT REFUTED OPTION A ON A DIFFERENT AXIS THAN THE BRIEF EXPECTED. *** The brief called endianness the real technical risk; the seat showed it is DERIVABLE (cpu_sh.c:96 sets EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN UNCONDITIONALLY, so there is no big-endian SH in this tree and the merge is always the low lane -- the brief's 'SH-4 can run either endianness' is true of silicon and FALSE of this tree, verified independently). The real objection is an ABSENT ORACLE: 0x005f74xx is Sega's Holly ASIC, outside the SH7750 manual, and this project holds NO documentation for it -- so the filing's own 'real hardware leaves the upper 24 bits' was an UNSOURCED CLAIM, the #357/#362 shape. A would ship an invented behaviour and pin the invention with a detector row. ON PRECEDENT it corrected the panel again: the BSC16 idiom (dev_sh4.c:1620-1680) is the direct shape-match, but *** IT TRANSFERS BY ITS WARRANT, NOT ITS SHAPE *** -- its warrant is 'a real OpenBSD/landisk boot makes ZERO non-16-bit BSC accesses (measured), so the latch costs a correct guest nothing', and that warrant FAILS here. Decided design recorded as B': decline at the four VALUE registers only, keep +0x18 byte-capable. It also flagged that the row pinning the REAL contract is currently UNBUILDABLE -- any nonzero store at +0x18 hits one of sh4gdromexit's filed exit(1) kills -- so this item is gated behind that round by probe-buildability rather than by ranking."}], "state_note": "*** A FARMED agy JOB RE-DERIVED THIS ROW INDEPENDENTLY 2026-08-26, AND ITS RECOMMENDATION WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A DEFECT. Recorded because the failure mode is instructive, not because the seat was careless. ***\n\n**What it got RIGHT, and it is a clean confirmation:** the tail store is `d->dma_reg[relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)] = idata;` with **no `len`**, `idata` arrives zero-extended from `memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)`, an aligned narrow write to an IMPLEMENTED offset therefore widens to 32 bits, and it is directly guest-reachable. It gave a correct three-way oracle table (defect `0x000000ff` / merged `0x112233ff` / declined `0x11223344`) and named both controls.\n\n**What it MISSED is what the source already says at the store itself, and it is decisive:**\n  1. **THE ORACLE IS ABSENT.** `0x005f74xx` is Sega's Holly ASIC, outside the SH7750 manual, and this project holds NO documentation for it. *'Real hardware preserves the upper bits'* would be an ORACLE FROM PLAUSIBILITY -- the `#357`/`#362` shape this tree has already been burned by once.\n  2. *** MAINLINE LINUX CONSTRAINS ANY FIX, and the comment records that this is the finding which KILLED THE PANEL'S UNANIMOUS ANSWER. *** `drivers/cdrom/gdrom.c` BYTE-WRITES `+0x18` to trigger every DMA transfer and BYTE-READS it to poll for completion. **So the width-blind code delivers exactly what the one real narrow user needs, and a blanket 'decline narrow writes' would BREAK A BOOTING LINUX.** The agy answer cited that same driver as EVIDENCE FOR the defect while missing that it is the CONSTRAINT AGAINST the obvious fix.\n  3. **The detector has a trap already named:** the discriminating row must NOT ship pinning `0x000000ff`, because that would enshrine this model's own behaviour as an oracle.\n\n*** AND THE PROVENANCE CLAIM WAS WRONG IN A WAY WORTH RECORDING: the seat labelled its oracle table MEASURED. *** Its numbers -- `0x000000ff`, liveness `0x12345678` -- are the EXACT values recorded in the source comment, which documents a real earlier measurement. **The numbers are true; the seat did not produce them.** That is the reading-vs-measurement distinction this project draws, and it matters because a seat quoting the tree's own measurement back looks exactly like independent confirmation.\n\n**DISPOSITION UNCHANGED: assessed-not-changed.** The decided design, if a fix is ever warranted, is already written at the store: decline with a latched diagnostic at the four VALUE registers ONLY, keeping `+0x18` byte-capable. The farmed job confirmed the reading and did not advance the row.\n\n*Worth carrying about the farm generally: a seat given a row whose analysis is already COMPLETE IN THE SOURCE will re-derive it and can reach a WORSE conclusion, because the constraints live in the comment rather than in the code it reads. Brief a seat with the existing record, or ask it a question the record does not already answer.*\n\nASSESSED-NOT-CHANGED under Option C. Code ships under this row's name at ba30e64's successor -- a documenting comment at the tail store -- but the defect is deliberately unfixed, so the row stays open. Gated behind sh4gdromexit by PROBE-BUILDABILITY, not priority: the row that would pin the real contract is unbuildable until that round removes the exit(1) kills at +0x18.", "note": "  *** CITATIONS RE-DERIVED 2026-08-31 AND TWO ARE STALE. ***  This row cites `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:515` and `:889`; today those lines are bare comment continuation (`*` and `*  Mount:`) and hold no code at all.  The construct the row is about -- the DMA tail store that ignores `len` -- now sits beside the comment *\"THEN the tail stores it into dma_reg[1].  The same alias exists at\"*.  **DURABLE ANCHOR, replacing both numbers: `grep -n 'tail stores it into dma_reg' src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c`.**  A grep anchor that matches nothing is LOUD; a drifted line number is silent and still looks like a citation.  *The file was committed five times after this note was written, which is exactly the window the citation-form rule describes: a same-file line number is invalidated by the round's own later edits, and nobody re-checks an example once it is written down.*"}
{"id": "sh4pcic15", "title": "sh4pcic15  PCIC interrupt line 15 can never be delivered -- it lands on 0x3e0, one slot past the priority loop's bound, and the obvious fix is a NO-OP", "state": "open", "commits": "", "hold": "", "witness": "none -- LATENT, and established as such by READING 2026-08-27: no modelled device connects to PCIC line 15, PCIC MMIO never raises an interrupt, and the only prospective PCI device on landisk is compile-disabled and would use line 5. No guest access sequence exists in the shipped tree.", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "entries": [{"seat": "fable5", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "[filed by the MAIN LOOP wearing the fable5 model -- seat and adjudicator are distinct hats; the seats that found it are named in the text] dev_sh4.c:73 sets N_PCIC_IRQS 16 and :2822-2836 wires every one as SH4_INTEVT_IRQ0 + 0x20*i, so line 15 lands on 0x3e0 (SH4_INTEVT_IRQ15, sh4_exception.h:157). The priority loop at cpu_sh.c:352 stops at SH4_INTEVT_IRQ14 (0x3c0), so that slot keeps priority 0 for the machine's life. *** EXTENDING THE BOUND FIXES NOTHING: the loop body is `15 - ((i - SH4_INTEVT_IRQ0) / 0x20)`, which yields priority 0 for IRQ15 -- exactly the state it is already in. The bound is not an off-by-one; it is where this formula runs out of nonzero priorities, so a round must decide what IRQ15's priority OUGHT to be. *** #455 also changed the symptom class: before it, an assert on a priority-0 line ran fatal() then exit(1), so this was a guest-reachable host kill; after it the interrupt is SILENTLY DROPPED. That is the de-escalation clause's own hazard arriving on schedule. HONEST LIMIT: header-and-loop-bound arithmetic across three files, NOT witnessed -- no probe has driven a PCIC line 15 assert."}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "*** THE ROW SAYS 'NOT WITNESSED', AND A WITNESS IS BOTH CONSTRUCTIBLE AND ADMISSIBLE. *** The carrier's own rule is that 'no witness of either kind' is a CHECKABLE claim and must be checked rather than asserted -- dfreq's was measured false by building one in a single session -- so this is that check, done by READING (flagged as a reading, not a measurement; the tree was busy with #461's pre-fix run and a probe would have contended with it). THE OBSERVABLE ALREADY EXISTS: cpu_sh.c:168-173 registers `iprio_%x` as a readable setting for EVERY 0x20 slot in 0..0xfff, which includes `iprio_3e0` (IRQ15). The hardwired priority loop at cpu_sh.c:390 is `for (i = SH4_INTEVT_IRQ0; i <= SH4_INTEVT_IRQ14; i += 0x20)`, so slot 0x3e0 is never written and keeps 0 while 0x3c0 (IRQ14) gets 1. Two `print` commands on an unmodified machine separate the two. AND RUNG 2 IS THE RIGHT RUNG FOR IT, not a concession: the ladder licenses machine construction as a reproduction for 'defects in code construction itself executes (init-time)', naming the macppc heap OOB #23 as the exemplar. This IS init-time -- the loop populates the priority table at CPU init and the defect is a slot it never reaches. The ladder's warning about rung 2 is that registration proves presence not reachability, for ACCESS-PATH defects; this is not one. WHAT A RUNG-2 WITNESS WOULD NOT SETTLE, said plainly: it observes the priority TABLE, not delivery. It cannot show that an asserted line 15 is dropped -- for that the carrier's free-running shape is required (sr=0x40000000, vbr into RAM, `bra .` after the trigger, breakpoint at VBR+0x600), and driving a PCIC line needs a PCI device landing on line 15, since dev_sh4.c:2951-2967 registers the 16 lines as `<path>.pcic.<i>` for bus_pci_init's pciirq rather than as anything a guest store reaches directly. So the honest split is: the priority-0 STATE is witnessable at rung 2 today; the DROPPED-DELIVERY symptom is not, and the round should not claim it is. STALE CITATION CORRECTED: the row cites dev_sh4.c:2822-2836 for the wiring; that range is now the default arm's fatal()/exit block. The wiring is at :2951-2967."}], "state_note": "*** FIRST EVIDENCE 2026-08-27. The row was a BARE TITLE with an empty note; a farmed codex job established it and every load-bearing claim was re-verified here by reading. ***\n\n**THE CLAIM IS TRUE.** `dev_sh4.c` registers SIXTEEN PCIC interrupts (the *'Register 16 PCIC interrupts'* loop over `N_PCIC_IRQS`), and `SH4_INTEVT_IRQ15` is `0x3e0` (`sh4_exception.h:157`). But priority initialisation in `cpu_sh.c` runs\n\n    for (i = SH4_INTEVT_IRQ0; i <= SH4_INTEVT_IRQ14; i += 0x20)\n\n**-- it stops at IRQ14.** Line 15 therefore keeps priority 0, and an assertion at priority 0 returns without scheduling delivery. **A line the device registers can never be delivered.**\n\n**IT IS LATENT, and the seat established that rather than asserting it:** PCIC MMIO never generates an interrupt; no modelled device connects to `.pcic.15`; landisk's only prospective PCI device is compile-disabled AND would use line 5 anyway. **There is no guest access sequence in the shipped tree**, so this ranks below anything guest-reachable.\n\n**THE OBSERVABLE a detector would key on:** guest interrupt-vector entry with `SH4_INTEVT == 0x3e0` -- delivery sets INTEVT and transfers to `VBR+0x600`; the broken code never reaches that handler. That is one channel, not a design.\n\n*The seat labelled its own work honestly: 'MEASURED -- Not run; no build is needed to settle these source facts.' Correct, and it is why this row could be settled under the machine embargo.*\n\n*** ONE VERIFICATION NOTE, because it cuts against my own checking rather than the seat's: my grep for the literal `0x3e0` in the device file returned NOTHING and I nearly scored the citation as wrong. The file uses the SYMBOL `SH4_INTEVT_IRQ15`. The seat cited the right site; my pattern could not see it. Same shape as the other apparatus misses today -- searching for a value where the code names a constant.***\n\nNOT FIXED: extending the loop to IRQ15 is a one-token change, but it is an EDIT TO INTERRUPT PRIORITY INITIALISATION and the addition licence wants a measured delta. With no device on line 15 there is nothing to measure today -- which is precisely why the row is latent, and why the fix should ride with the round that first connects a device there.\n\n"}
{"id": "asicpermute", "title": "asicpermute  `(relative_addr / 4) % 3` permutes the ASIC register index bijectively and NO round-trip row can see it", "state": "open", "commits": "", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "hold": "", "witness": "none -- LATENT; needs a free-running assertion probe, which no SH probe here has", "state_note": "OUTSIDE the #463 detector's contract, and the contract says so rather than claiming completeness. `% 3` maps each group bijectively -- {4,5,6} -> {1,2,0} -- so every write and its readback round-trip through the SAME permuted cell and AGREE. Batching does not help: batching catches ALIASING (two offsets colliding on one cell) and a permutation collides nothing. Yet the tick's `pending_irq[i] & mask_13[i]` then pairs the wrong words, so the OBSERVABLE is interrupt assertion, which no row here reaches. **A single-stepping probe cannot observe interrupt DELIVERY** (`cpu_dyntrans.c:204-206`), so closing this needs the free-running shape. `% 3` is the most plausible naive 'repair' anyone writes at this site -- it is what you reach for when worried about an index out of range -- so this is the highest-probability future edit in the file."}
{"id": "asiceventaddr", "title": "asiceventaddr  SYSASIC_EVENT_TO_ADDR is correct only for e in [0,95]: high events decode to DROPPED fourth-word offsets OR to LEGAL mask-register cases that clobber guest state, and no r-guard can see the second kind", "state": "open", "commits": "9ca9cca", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "hold": "", "witness": "none -- LATENT, and provably so: the highest defined event is 67 < 96", "state_note": "*** #466's PASS 2 OVERTURNED THE ADJUDICATION THAT SHIPPED IT. TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY REFUTED THE 'NO BUILD-TIME CHECK IS POSSIBLE' RULING, AND ONE OF THEM BUILT ALL FOUR VARIANTS. #468 ships the guard. ***\n\nThe adjudicator rejected a compile-time check on SHAPE: *an assert can only reference NAMED terms, so a future `#define SYSASIC_EVENT_FOO 96` adds a name it does not mention and compiles clean.* **That is true of a STANDALONE assert and false of a check INSIDE THE MACRO**, which fires at the RAISE and therefore cannot be bypassed by adding a name.\n\nMEASURED by the opus5 seat, four variants under the real build compiler:\n\n    V0 shipped macro           feared edit compiles CLEAN   <- the defect\n    V1 standalone assert       CLEAN   <- adjudicator's objection CONFIRMED\n    V2 check inside the macro  ERROR: negative array size\n    V3 bitfield width          ERROR: negative width in bit-field\n\n**V3 is preferred over V2 and the reason is measured:** with a runtime-variable `e`, V2 silently degrades to a VLA and compiles; V3 hard-errors because a bitfield width must be an integer constant. **Both need only C89** -- clean under `-std=c89 -pedantic-errors -Wall -Wextra` -- so *the C11 leg of the rationale ruled out one SPELLING, not compile-time checking.* And the kimi substitute reached the same conclusion independently, by reasoning rather than building: *'a name-insensitive construction that catches the feared mutant exists, is build-time, and touches neither product code nor the vendored header.'*\n\n**SO THE OPTION SET HAD THREE MEMBERS AND THE ADJUDICATION NAMED TWO** -- comment / standalone assert / **assert inside the macro**. Exactly the three-membered-option-set rule, biting the ruling that invoked it.\n\n*** THE ADDITION LICENCE NOW CLEARS ON A MEASURED DELTA, which it did not before: *** V0 compiles the feared edit, V3 refuses it, and **all 21 real events keep BYTE-IDENTICAL addresses**. That is the A-vs-B measurement the licence asks for, and it is why this ships rather than staying a comment.\n\nINDEPENDENTLY RE-VERIFIED HERE before landing: the feared edit is refused **and fails for OUR reason** (a bitfield diagnostic, not an incidental one); the boundary is exact (`e=95` compiles, `e=96` does not); the header still preprocesses `rc=0` and still holds its 21 numeric event defines, unchanged from before `#466`.\n\n**THE COMMENT'S BAND TABLE WAS ALSO WRONG AND IS CORRECTED.** It omitted the ENTIRE `mask_9` group and understated the clobber domain **5.5x** -- the true domain is `e in [128,479]`, 352 values across NINE mask words, of which the comment named 64. It also mispaired `96..127 -> +0x0c, +0x1c`, which is the same band-to-address error the device file had already corrected an earlier draft for. Restated STRUCTURALLY: the macro treats the event's high bits as a word index into the whole 64-word register file, when only words 0-2 are the `pending_irq` group.\n\n*And a clobber is worse than the comment implied: the mask cases carry no hack-bit discrimination and the store is a wholesale replacement with the tick called immediately, so the raise both FAILS and DESTROYS the guest's programmed IRQ mask.*\n\n*** THREE APPARATUS FAILURES OF MINE ON THE WAY, ALL DOCUMENTED CLASSES, RECORDED BECAUSE THE THIRD IS NEW: ***\n  1. my verification harness redefined `SYSASIC_BASE`, which the header owns -- every leg died of `redefined` and the feared-edit leg's 'pass' was a **FALSE PASS**, failing for the wrong reason. Rule 4 caught it: *did it fail for the reason under test?*\n  2. I wrote the guard with a Python string instead of `chr(92)` and put a **literal `\n` into product code** where a line continuation belongs -- the documented Bash->heredoc->Python trap, propagated to all four trees before I caught it.\n  3. **NEW, AND WORTH THE SPACE: my own comment's worked example -- `#define SYSASIC_EVENT_FOO 96` -- POISONED THE CENSUS that greps the header for event defines.** The harness read the DOCUMENTATION as SOURCE and built a program out of the very construct the comment forbids. **A comment containing an example of a forbidden construct breaks any census that greps for that construct.** Same shape as the grep that matched a comment and was read as code earlier the same day.\n\n*** ADJUDICATED 2026-08-26. MY DISSOLUTION READING HELD -- AND MY FOLD WAS HALF WRONG, WHICH IS THE PART THAT MATTERS. ***\n\n**THE DISSOLUTION CONFIRMED, MEASURED:** every event raise is a `memory_rw` write through the device decode. `SYSASIC_TRIGGER_EVENT` computes the address and issues the write; `SYSASIC_EVENT_TO_ADDR` has exactly TWO occurrences in `src/` -- its definition and that one use -- and every in-tree raiser goes through the macro. The arrays are declared file-locally in the `.c`, so no other unit can index them. **There is no bypass, so the vendored-header LICENCE QUESTION IS MOOT** -- the guard need never live in the header.\n\n*** BUT THE HEADER DEFECT IS BROADER THAN THE r==3 INVARIANT, AND THE EXCESS IS EXACTLY WHAT AN r-GUARD CANNOT SEE. Verified here by arithmetic and by reading the case: ***\n\n    e=96..127   -> +0x0c   r=3   default: -- DROPPED (an r-guard covers this)\n    e=128..159  -> +0x10   r=0   *** case 0x10: `d->mask_13[r] = idata` -- LEGAL WRITE ***\n    e=256..287  -> +0x20   r=0   *** case 0x20: another legal mask case ***\n    e>=2048     -> +0x100+       OUTSIDE the device window entirely\n\n**A raise in [128,159] is decoded as a perfectly legal mask write and CLOBBERS a mask register the guest programmed.** Memory-safe, guest-visible, and invisible to any guard on `r` -- because r is 0, not 3. So the row FOLDS ONLY HALFWAY: the fourth-word half is covered by `asicindexcase`'s already-adjudicated #463b decline at zero added cost; **this row survives as DOCUMENT-ONLY for the mask-clobber bands and the window escape.**\n\n**THE TRUE STATEMENT OF THE DEFECT: `SYSASIC_EVENT_TO_ADDR` is correct only for e in [0,95].** The old title said it 'routes any event >= 96 to relative 0x0c' -- FALSE, true only of [96,127] -- and it contradicted this row's own tail correction AND the shipped comment in the device file, which already says *'an earlier draft said any event >= 96, which is false'*. **The queue's most-read field carried a falsehood its own body had corrected.** Title rewritten.\n\n*** AND I REPEATED THAT SAME FALSE UNIVERSAL TO THE ADJUDICATOR while asking the question -- 'Every event >= 96 gives (e)>>5 == 3' -- when the correction already existed in TWO of my own shipped records. *** A brief built on my own uncorrected summary rather than on my own corrected record: the dropped-qualifier class, aimed at the seat I was asking for help.\n\n**THE COMPILE-TIME ASSERT IS REJECTED ON SHAPE, NOT ON LICENCE, and the reason is this project's own named class.** An assert can only reference NAMED terms, so it pins today's max (`67>>5 == 2`, already true) while a future `#define SYSASIC_EVENT_FOO 96` -- **the exact feared edit** -- adds a name the assert does not mention and compiles clean. **A check that cannot fail on its own target mutant.** The `SH4_VAL` enum idiom does not transfer either: sparse hardware numbers need explicit `= n` per member and a terminal count sees only members above it. **The house precedent points the other way and is in this very device family**: `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` chose *'Stated rather than asserted: `_Static_assert` is C11 and this tree is C99'*. Follow it -- STATE the domain restriction.\n\nCLOSING ARTIFACT: a 2-5 line comment in the header beside the macro -- the one place a future event-adder's eyes are guaranteed to be -- pointing at the device file's existing #463 invariant comment, which already points back. A comment adds no product code, so the addition licence is not even triggered. Tag it `/* #NNN: */` per house ethos. Must land byte-identical in `est/`.\n\n*Premises the adjudicator discharged: the two-commit thirdparty figure CONFIRMED -- and sharper than I had it, since `8dd86b5` touched four thirdparty headers but NOT this one, so **this fork has never edited it**; the `[3]` arrays CONFIRMED; and 'a device-file comment cannot govern a header #define' is TRUE of the comment and HALF-TRUE of the guard, which is exactly why the row folds halfway.*\n\n*** THE THREE ASIC ROWS REST ON ONE INVARIANT, AND TWO OF THEM MAY BE ONE FIX. Scoped 2026-08-26 by reading, no run. ***\n\n**THE SHARED INVARIANT: `r == 3` is unreachable by any implemented case.** VERIFIED by reading the switch labels -- they are three CONSECUTIVE words at group bases `0x00/0x10/0x20/0x30`, so `r = (relative_addr / 4) & 3` yields 0, 1, 2 and never 3. All three rows are that one invariant, differing only in **which edit would break it**:\n\n    asiceventaddr    a #define in the vendored HEADER (event >= 96 -> offset 0x0c)\n    asicindexcase    an indexing `case` added at an r==3 offset in the DEVICE FILE\n    asicoffsetkill   an adversarial MUTANT at an r==3 offset no detector row touches\n\n**THE THIRD IS DIFFERENT IN KIND and should not be folded with the other two:** it is a DETECTOR-COVERAGE question, not a product invariant. `kill far` (+0xfc) was added to defeat a GROUP-NARROWING mutant and cannot defeat a SINGLE-OFFSET one; the seat that raised it said plainly it did not build it.\n\n*** THE FINDING THAT MAY FOLD THE OTHER TWO, and it contradicts `asiceventaddr`'s own stated reason for being a separate row: *** that row argues a device-file comment \"cannot govern a `#define` added to a header\". **True of a COMMENT. Not true of a GUARD.** An event `>= 96` does not stay in the header -- it becomes an ADDRESS the driver accesses, arriving at the device as `relative_addr == 0x0c` and decoding to `r == 3`. **So a guard on `r` in the device file would catch the header entry point too**, because the header case MANIFESTS as a device-file access.\n\nIf that holds, one guard covers both rows and the vendored-header edit -- the expensive part, on a file this fork has touched once -- may be unnecessary. **Stated as a question rather than a conclusion because I have not built it**, and the adjudicator was asked the licence question on the header assumption; this finding is being sent to it as a correction rather than acted on unilaterally.\n\nSTOP CONDITION: if the guard-covers-both reading holds, fold `asiceventaddr` into `asicindexcase` as one fix with two entry points and close it as a duplicate; if it does not, the rows stay separate and the header licence question stands.\n\n*** SCOPED 2026-08-26 WITHOUT A RUN -- the row is read-only by construction, and the scoping found a CONSTRAINT that changes the fix's cost. ***\n\n**THE FIX SITE IS A VENDORED THIRDPARTY HEADER.** The macro is at `src/include/thirdparty/dreamcast_sysasicvar.h`, which carries BOTH a GXemul `$Id$` and an upstream **`$NetBSD: sysasicvar.h,v 1.5`** line. **MEASURED: `git log --all -- 'src/include/thirdparty/*'` returns TWO commits -- the 0.7.0 import, and the single ~119-correction hardening commit.** So this fork has edited thirdparty exactly ONCE.\n\n*The precedent is not absent, and it cuts both ways: the header's own banner says it was \"Extended with useful macros and also some more event definitions described in KOS comments\", so GXemul UPSTREAM already extends this vendored file. Editing it is precedented -- it is just rare, and rarity is the cost.*\n\n**WHY THAT MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING TRIVIA:** the row's whole reason for existing separately is that *the edit which reaches this defect adds a `#define` to a header, with no device-file change at all* -- so a structural-invariant comment in `dev_dreamcast_asic.c` cannot govern it. **The only place a guard can sit is the same rarely-touched header**, which means the fix's licence question is sharper than for an in-file addition.\n\n**THE CANDIDATE FIX, stated so it can be adjudicated rather than argued:** a compile-time assertion beside the macro, so that adding an event `>= 96` fails the BUILD rather than silently routing to relative `0x0c` and indexing three-element arrays at 3. It is self-verifying by construction -- a negative control is one test `#define` -- and it costs nothing at runtime.\n\n**BUT IT IS AN ADDITION TO PRODUCT CODE, so the ADDITION LICENCE governs: a MEASURED DELTA or a TRANSFERRED WARRANT, never 'it cannot hurt'.** The delta cannot be measured without a build, and the machine is reserved (owner directive 2026-08-26: hold off on anything needing an idle machine). **`asicbadaddr` is the natural warrant donor** -- same device, same `r = (relative_addr / 4) & 3` decode, same three-element arrays -- and whether that transfers to a THIRDPARTY header edit is exactly the judgement an adjudicator owes rather than me.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOT IN DOUBT AND NEEDED NO RUN: the arithmetic. *** `(e)>>5 == 3` for every `e >= 96`; the highest defined event is 67; therefore latent today, and reachable by a one-line header addition by anyone adding a high event number. Two seats confirmed both numbers from source on 2026-08-24 and I re-derived the macro form here.\n\nNEXT ACTION: adjudicate the licence and the thirdparty-edit question. NOT shipped unilaterally -- a rarely-touched vendored header is precisely where 'it cannot hurt' does the most damage.\n\nRAISED BY THE opus5 SEAT on the asicbadaddr pass-1 panel (panel_20260824_231824), ADJUDICATED 2026-08-24. `SYSASIC_EVENT_TO_ADDR(e)` is `SYSASIC_BASE + 4*((e)>>5)`, so ANY event number >= 96 gives `(e)>>5 == 3` and a relative offset of 0x0c -- which `dev_dreamcast_asic.c`'s `r = (relative_addr / 4) & 3` maps to index 3 of three-element arrays. **LATENT AND PROVABLY SO: the highest defined event is 67 (MATOUTOFMEM), and both the 67 and the 96 threshold were confirmed from source by two seats.** *** WHY IT IS A SEPARATE ROW RATHER THAN PART OF asicbadaddr: THE EDIT THAT REACHES IT ADDS A #define TO A HEADER, WITH NO CHANGE TO THE DEVICE FILE AT ALL -- so the structural-invariant comment asicbadaddr plans to leave in `dev_dreamcast_asic.c` CANNOT GOVERN IT. *** Post-fix the failure mode also changes rather than disappearing: an event >= 96 would write to 0x0c, fall to `default:`, and the pending bit would be SILENTLY LOST where pre-fix it was `exit(1)`. Neither is correct; neither is reachable today. *** DISPOSITION: NO RUNTIME GUARD *** -- a guard that cannot fire is the insurance-that-isn't this tree already rejects. The carrier is a one-line comment at the event-define block IN THE HEADER. **FILED RATHER THAN SHIPPED THIS ROUND, and the reason is the file class:** the header lives under `src/include/thirdparty/`, which this fork HAS edited (the 8dd86b5 hardening commit) but where NO file carries the `/* #NNN: */ ` correction tag the ethos otherwise requires -- so the convention for editing it is unsettled, and settling it is not this round's job. *** THE MANDATORY HALF IS THE RECORD, NOT THE COMMENT: `asicbadaddr` must SCOPE its invariant claim to `dev_dreamcast_asic.c` and name this header path as a separate latent item, rather than claiming the invariant is fully protected. ***  *** CORRECTED 2026-08-25 BY THE #463 RECORDS AUDIT (`_scratchpad/panel_20260825_024101_audit`, grok seat), and the correction makes this row STRONGER. *** Every earlier statement of this row said the macro reaches `0x0c` for \"any event >= 96\". **That is false**: `SYSASIC_EVENT_TO_ADDR(e) = SYSASIC_BASE + 4*((e)>>5)` (`dreamcast_sysasicvar.h:74`) sends `e` in `[128,159]` to `+0x10`, which is r = 0 and perfectly legal. The true predicate is `(e>>5) & 3 == 3` -- `[96,127]` -> `+0x0c`, `[224,255]` -> `+0x1c`, `[352,383]` -> `+0x2c`, `[480,511]` -> `+0x3c`. So the violator is not a single range above a threshold but a REPEATING FAMILY that lands on the fourth word of every 16-byte group -- the same structural shape as `asicbadaddr` itself, one level up in the header. Verified by enumerating `4*(e>>5)` over `e>>5` in `[0,15]`. The LATENCY argument is unchanged and still holds: max over all 21 `SYSASIC_EVENT_*` defines is 67 (`:72`), and `67>>5 == 2` lands at `+0x08`."}
{"id": "wdcflood", "state": "in work", "commits": "92e917e", "title": "wdcflood  wdc_command()'s default: fatal() is unlatched -- 100 commands, 100 host log lines", "entries": [{"phase": "assess", "seat": "opus5", "date": "2026-08-19", "verdict": "REAL", "evidence": "CONFIRMED", "note": "MEASURED: 100 repeats of 0xE7 produced 100 fatal() calls. Same guest-drivable stderr flood class as #265/#269 and the cflood rule. dev_wdc.c ALREADY CARRIES THREE warned_* latches added by #337 -- this path was simply missed by that round.  [agent idesync 2026-08-19]"}, {"seat": "agy", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "agy answered this cluster panel. ROUND A, and the seats put it first for tractability rather than severity: one oracle, one line, and the precedent is already in this file three times over. It corrupts nothing -- it is host-log noise a guest can drive without limit.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260819_235917/"}, {"seat": "grok", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "grok answered this cluster panel. ROUND A, and the seats put it first for tractability rather than severity: one oracle, one line, and the precedent is already in this file three times over. It corrupts nothing -- it is host-log noise a guest can drive without limit.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260819_235917/"}, {"seat": "kimi", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "kimi answered this cluster panel. ROUND A, and the seats put it first for tractability rather than severity: one oracle, one line, and the precedent is already in this file three times over. It corrupts nothing -- it is host-log noise a guest can drive without limit.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260819_235917/"}, {"seat": "glm", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "CLUSTER PANEL on wdc_command()'s `default:` arm -- five rows, one code path, none of them previously reviewed by anyone but the seat that filed it. *** THE SEATS WERE UNANIMOUS ON THE QUESTION THAT DECIDES THE WORK: SPLIT. *** Four independent verdict lines said so (agy: 'four defects in one arm require three distinct oracles, cross subsystem boundaries'; deepseek: 'one default: arm yields at least three distinct semantic classes/oracles'; minimax: 'the four rows collapse to two distinct fix designs'; glm: 'one arm, three distinct oracles -- host-log count, IRQ assertion, fsync ordering'). The reopening rule's NO-NEW-ORACLE prong is what does the splitting, and the argument is mechanical rather than stylistic: a detector that counts host stderr lines cannot observe int_assert; one that observes int_assert cannot observe fsync ordering; one that observes fsync ordering cannot count log lines. THE AGREED SPLIT:\n  Round A  wdcflood alone            -- oracle: host-log line count; fix: the warned_* latch idiom, which #337 already applied THREE TIMES in this same file.\n  Round B  wdcnoirq + wdcstandby     -- oracle: IRQ assertion; ONE fix (set int_assert). They travel together because wdcstandby is the RUNG-4 WITNESS (a real OpenBSD driver reaching the path unaided, 4 of 4 halt -p runs) that grounds wdcnoirq, whose harm statement is otherwise inference.\n  Round C  idesync alone             -- oracle: fsync ordering; crosses a module boundary into diskimage.c and cannot be witnessed above rung 2, since no guest in this tree issues the opcode.\n  landiskdisk is NOT a behavioural defect -- a coverage gap. It files; it does not round.\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable answered (agy 12.6 KB, grok 11.7 KB, kimi 74 KB, glm 19.6 KB, deepseek 5.3 KB, minimax 11.6 KB). Codex RATE-LIMITED for the third time today, same echoed-brief-plus-429 signature. *** GROK WAS 200 BYTES WHEN FIRST SAMPLED AND FINISHED AT 11.7 KB -- the third time in one day that a small early sample was mid-answer, not a dead seat. ***\n\nFOR THIS ROW: ROUND A, and the seats put it first for tractability rather than severity: one oracle, one line, and the precedent is already in this file three times over. It corrupts nothing -- it is host-log noise a guest can drive without limit.", "grade": "BELIEVED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260819_235917/"}, {"seat": "deepseek", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "deepseek answered this cluster panel. ROUND A, and the seats put it first for tractability rather than severity: one oracle, one line, and the precedent is already in this file three times over. It corrupts nothing -- it is host-log noise a guest can drive without limit.", "grade": "BELIEVED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260819_235917/"}, {"seat": "minimax", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "minimax answered this cluster panel. ROUND A, and the seats put it first for tractability rather than severity: one oracle, one line, and the precedent is already in this file three times over. It corrupts nothing -- it is host-log noise a guest can drive without limit.", "grade": "BELIEVED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260819_235917/"}, {"seat": "codex", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "WALL DISCHARGED -- re-fired 2026-08-20 after codex answered a health test (CODEX_ALIVE_20260820, and it got the arithmetic right). Verified a REAL review, not the echo that preceded it: delta over the brief is well past the ~688-byte banner and the usage-limit string is absent from the tail.\n\nITS VERDICT: VERDICT: SPLIT -- the shared `default:` arm does not make command support, interrupt completion, log throttling, durability, and gate coverage one semantic round.\n\nCAVEAT RECORDED ON EVERY RE-FIRED CODEX ENTRY: codex reviewed the brief AS IT WAS FIRED (byte-identical, from this panel's own brief_sent.md, so its answer is comparable with the seats that answered live). THE TREE HAS MOVED SINCE. Its rtcdet verdict was DEFECTIVE on a '+1' mutant that 'admits UINT64_MAX while all nine rows remain green' -- CORRECT against the 9-row table it was shown, and MEASURED CLOSED against today's 18-row table (2 failures). So a codex finding here is HISTORICAL unless re-checked, and three are already settled by measurement: 'do tfreq first' (tfreq is fixed -- timer.c:188 'CLAMP FIRST, THEN compare, THEN store'), 'mrwstore2 first by producing a real-guest witness' (measured: three guest boots, ZERO straddling stores from guest code, and only PowerPC can emit one at all), and 'drop constblind' (a live instance was measured and fixed hours earlier).", "grade": "MANUAL", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260819_235917/codex.txt.rerun"}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "RE-READ AS AN INSTANCE. See `floodclass`. The sharpest fact in the census is about THIS row: #337 applied the latch idiom THREE TIMES inside dev_wdc.c and still missed the path this row names. A file-level census scores dev_wdc.c as 'latched'; the defect is per-PATH. That is why the census reports its own limit on every run.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "tools/pipeline/census_floods.py"}, {"phase": "assess", "seat": "fable5", "date": "2026-08-22", "verdict": "", "evidence": "UNKNOWN", "note": "CLOSE (assess). Real floodclass instance, severity raised by the fatal() correction; witness needs landiskdisk first.", "d": "✓"}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "*** A WRONG RECORD I PROPAGATED ALL EVENING, AND CORRECTING IT MAKES THIS WORSE. ***  I asserted repeatedly -- in briefs, in commit messages and in three ledger entries -- that `fatal()` is 'byte-for-byte debug()'. MEASURED FALSE, found by the seat driving the pitlatch round:\n\n    void debug(...) { ...; if ((quiet_mode && !ss) || v < 0) return; va_debug(...); }\n    void fatal(...) {                                              va_debug(...); }\n\n`debug()` carries an EARLY-OUT on quiet_mode and verbosity; `fatal()` has NONE. They are not the same function and never were. What I got right is that fatal() does not EXIT -- that part stands, and it was the point I was making each time.\n\nTHE CONSEQUENCE IS THE OPPOSITE OF REASSURING: a guest-drivable fatal() flood CANNOT BE SILENCED, not even with -q. The operator has no switch. So every row in this class is MORE severe than recorded, not less -- and the seat that found it noted that this STRENGTHENS the defect rather than excusing it.\n\nRecorded here rather than only in a commit message, because a claim repeated in five places is not corrected by fixing one of them -- this project's own rule is to grep for the siblings before calling a correction done.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "src/core/debugmsg.c (debug() vs fatal(), read side by side)"}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "*** RE-SCOPE TO THE WHOLE FILE: dev_wdc.c has TEN fatal() calls, FOUR latched by #337 and SIX UNLATCHED. This row names one. ***  Three of the other five were driven through dev_wdc_access -- the guest's real entry point -- at 100 accesses each:\n    100 reads of wd_data on an empty inbuf   -> 100 fatal()  (wdc_get_inbuf():160)\n    100 x 2-byte WRITE to a non-data register-> 100 fatal()  (:607)\n    100 x 2-byte READ  of a non-data register-> 100 fatal()  (:1025)\n*** THESE NEED NO COMMAND WRITE AT ALL *** -- a two-instruction guest loop over one register. Strictly easier to drive than 0xE7, and gate_hygiene.sh contains ZERO wdc patterns, so none of it is visible to any gate.\n\nAND THE fatal()/-q CORRECTION IS NOW CONFIRMED BY EXECUTION, not by reading: the harness sets quiet_mode = 1 and the flood still produced 100 fatal() out of 100. A guest-drivable wdc flood cannot be silenced even with -q.\n\nROUTE THIS INTO [[floodclass]] as its first measured-reachability instance rather than shipping a one-line latch: floodclass's own filing says the census 'does no reachability analysis' and that the right scope is a round on WHICH calls are guest-reachable -- and that has now been done for one file, by execution. *** Latching only :583 would be #337's mistake a third time. ***", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "dev_wdc.c fatal() census + 3 sites driven at 100/100 through dev_wdc_access"}, {"seat": "agy", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 1, GO-WITH-CHANGES. **SETTLED Q1 WITH AN ARGUMENT NOBODY HAD MADE AND IT DISSOLVED THE EVIDENCE CONFLICT RATHER THAN PICKING A SIDE.** The brief posed shared-vs-per-site latching as #337 (shares warned_badlen) versus #460 (measured warrant for per-site). Neither is wrong: #337's two sites are THE TWO ARMS OF ONE ENDIANNESS BRANCH, mutually exclusive at runtime, so sharing there loses nothing -- verified by reading dev_wdc.c:709 and its else. Also caught an INTERNAL CONTRADICTION in my own brief (scalar fields proposed while Q5 demanded a per-opcode mutant) and supplied the real clause-(d) mutant: scope `d->error |= WDCE_ABRT` INSIDE the latch, after which the second unimplemented command silently SUCCEEDS. All three of its checkable claims were verified against source rather than credited.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_195526/agy.txt"}, {"seat": "grok", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 1, GO-WITH-CHANGES. Corrected four premises: #337's sharing is 'two endian copies of one check, not a licence to pair different events'; Q5's per-opcode mutant contradicts the per-instance design the brief proposes and is a SPEC CHANGE, not a floor test; ':145 is the unfixed pckbc/asc wrap, not a twin of the measured :160 flood'; and the suppression notice is the exception (2 files), not the house idiom. Latch all six, keep fatal(), wrap ONLY the fatal().", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_195526/grok.txt"}, {"seat": "codex", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 1. Supplied the two HONEST routes to the :145 witness after establishing it is NOT a one-store witness: prime head/tail in the detector, or issue 513 undrained IDENTIFY commands (513 * 512 == WDC_INBUF_SIZE exactly). **AND THE DETECTOR RULE THAT SHAPED EVERY SITE ROW: 'Each site row must include repetition. A one-off first warning proves announcement, not suppression.'** That is why every row fires its site twice and demands EXACTLY one line.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_195526/codex.txt"}, {"seat": "kimi", "phase": "review", "d": "✗", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 1 -- SEAT FAILURE ON THE CLI TRANSPORT, COVERED BY ITS SUBSTITUTE. The CLI returned a 348-byte `403 usage limit for this billing cycle`; that file is NOT a seat answer and is not counted as one. The seat answered over `kimi-k3:cloud`; see the separate entry.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_195526/kimi.txt"}, {"seat": "kimi", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 1 via the SUBSTITUTE transport, recorded by transport and never as 'the kimi CLI seat'. Design approved with five premise corrections. **Its strongest: my :145/:160 pairing was refuted from THIS ROUND'S OWN WITNESS** -- the over-read fired with no disk, so no overrun could have preceded it and the ring halves are causally independent. (NB the round later found that WARRANT to be a non-sequitur -- addtoinbuf is reachable from the DATA-write path -- while the CONCLUSION survives on a different warrant.) Also located the real defect at :145 rather than :160, which became `wdcringambig`.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_195526/ollama_kimi.txt  *** SUBSTITUTE TRANSPORT `kimi-k3:cloud` -- the kimi CLI was 403 quota-walled for this panel. Recorded as the kimi SEAT answering over a DIFFERENT TRANSPORT, never as the CLI seat. The seat id is `kimi` because the roster has NINE seats, not ten: one seat, two transports. An earlier form used `kimi-k3:cloud` AS a seat id, which gen_dashboard.py refused to render -- correctly, since an undeclared column would have silently created a tenth seat. ***"}, {"seat": "glm", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 1, brief-only. ':160 is a guest error (safe to latch); :145 is an emulator error (do NOT latch without fixing the ring overrun).' **THE ONLY DON'T-LATCH VOICE ON THE PANEL** -- an earlier version of this row wrongly counted minimax alongside it. Its own fallback (downgrade to debug()) was then measured strictly WORSE for the property it defends, since debug() early-outs at core/debugmsg.c:375 and vanishes free-running at verbose 0. Dissent carried VERBATIM into `wdcringambig`.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_195526/ollama_glm.txt"}, {"seat": "deepseek", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 1, brief-only. Dissented usefully on Q5: :583's diagnostic VALUE is the opcode, so one scalar flag hides distinct guest actions and a 256-bit set would preserve them. Adjudicated a SPEC choice rather than a floor test and FILED as `wdcperopcode`. Its ring analysis was the panel's weakest (':145 is a guest error -- the guest wrote more data than the ring can hold' is off; the EMULATOR writes that ring on the guest's behalf).", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_195526/ollama_deepseek.txt"}, {"seat": "minimax", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 1, brief-only. 'So: latch both' -- latch-and-mark, which is the disposition this round adopted. **AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS ROW MISQUOTED IT AS A DON'T-LATCH VOTE**, turning a 1-of-8 dissent into a 2-of-8; corrected. It also said plainly that :145 IS a real defect the latch does not fix and that a TODO is owed there, which is exactly what shipped.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_195526/ollama_minimax.txt"}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "—", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 1 -- NOT FIRED. Recorded by name: this is neither a seat failure nor agreement. The seat that compiles and measures did not read the design brief.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_195526"}, {"seat": "fable5", "phase": "review", "d": "—", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 1 -- NOT FIRED as a review seat; it held the ADJUDICATOR hat for this stage instead (dual-hat disclosed, `s` badge). Its ruling settled the Q5 split, the wdcringambig non-blocking question, the landiskdisk split, and refused to mint codex's repetition rule as a carrier clause on the ground that clause (d) already forces it.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_195526"}, {"seat": "grok", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 2, **DO-NOT-SHIP THE DETECTOR AS A SIX-SITE FLOOR -- AND IT WAS RIGHT.** ':145 has no row, so clause (b) is open.' Confirmed by counting the probe: P_OVERRUN appeared only in `disjoint`, which fires an unimplemented command and never touches the ring, so the fix had SIX fixed sites and the detector saw FIVE. **An L2 floor breach, closed in-round by the `overrun x2` row and measured by Mb2.** Also flagged the channel-downgrade class and the stale pre-fix line numbers in the row names.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_204943/grok.txt"}, {"seat": "codex", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 2. **THE FINDING I WOULD HAVE MISSED PERMANENTLY: 14/14 does not enforce six independent flags.** Every row starts a FRESH emulator, so pairing the width flags, the ring flags, or even a PROCESS-GLOBAL latch would pass every isolated row -- the round's headline design decision is unmeasured by its own detector. It checked the mitigating half too (six per-instance fields, DEVINIT zeroes the allocation, nothing resets or aliases them), so the CODE is right and the DETECTOR cannot prove it. Now DISCLOSED in the contract rather than claimed. Also: `disjoint` proves less than its name, and a `return` inserted after a latched fatal() escapes a count-only row.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_204943/codex.txt"}, {"seat": "agy", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 2, GO with premise corrections, full source verification. Its census was confirmed against my own probe: ':145 has 0 rows while :160 has 2 and :583 has 3', and 'the non-8-bit tests only probe 16-bit widths' -- both became new rows (`overrun x2`, `w32 x2`, `r32 x2`). Verified fatal()-vs-debug(), the dev_asc.c:248-257 warrant, and the buffer arithmetic against source.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_204943/agy.txt"}, {"seat": "kimi", "phase": "review", "d": "✗", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 2 -- SEAT FAILURE ON THE CLI TRANSPORT (348-byte 403), COVERED BY ITS SUBSTITUTE. **panel.sh reported this correctly for the first time**: 'the kimi CLI is walled but kimi-k3:cloud ANSWERED -- that seat is COVERED by its substitute transport, not lost.'", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_204943/kimi.txt"}, {"seat": "kimi", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 2 via the substitute transport. Named two live kill classes the 14-row table was blind to (epoch-latches whose clearing event no row performs; side-effect swallows at the width sites) and corrected three premises -- including that `idata = data[0]` is a FOURTH guest-visible side effect my Q2 list omitted, which became the `w16 value` row. Also supplied a constraint `wdcringambig` will need: a waste-one-slot disambiguator is NOT forbidden by capacity, since 512*512 = 262,144 <= 262,655.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_204943/ollama_kimi.txt  *** SUBSTITUTE TRANSPORT `kimi-k3:cloud` -- the kimi CLI was 403 quota-walled for this panel. Recorded as the kimi SEAT answering over a DIFFERENT TRANSPORT, never as the CLI seat. The seat id is `kimi` because the roster has NINE seats, not ten: one seat, two transports. An earlier form used `kimi-k3:cloud` AS a seat id, which gen_dashboard.py refused to render -- correctly, since an undeclared column would have silently created a tenth seat. ***"}, {"seat": "glm", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 2, brief-only. **THE SHARPEST ESCAPE OF THE PANEL, AND IT MEASURED TRUE**: ONE line inside an existing VALID command's case -- `d->warned_unimpl_cmd = 0;` -- reusing the existing flag, guard and branch. Every row fired its site twice IN SUCCESSION, so nothing between the fires ever reset the flag and the mutant looked perfectly latched, while a guest alternating probe/IDENTIFY/probe floods forever. Became the `interleave` row; measured as M7 (21/22, that row alone). It also named the guest-resettable-operand class that became M6.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_204943/ollama_glm.txt"}, {"seat": "deepseek", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 2, brief-only. Proposed `d->warned_X = !d->warned_X;` as a period-2 escape. **I read its SPECIFIC edit as unworkable -- the assignment sits inside the guard, so fire 2 never reaches it -- and recorded it as 'a prediction to be MEASURED, not refuted'.** M8 then built the same class differently (toggle BEFORE the guard) and escaped a two-fire oracle exactly as the seat said the class would: wrong about the placement, RIGHT ABOUT THE CLASS. Declaring it refuted would have lost a real escape; `fire3` closes the whole family.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_204943/ollama_deepseek.txt"}, {"seat": "minimax", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 2, brief-only. Caught that **my own brief's row census did not add up** -- 2 controls + 6 site + 3 accept + 1 disjoint = 12 against a stated 14, the two omitted being `abrt 2nd` and `overread -1`, the most interesting rows in the file. Also challenged that `fatal()` RETURNS: true, and settled 2026-08-21 by reading core/debugmsg.c:384-390, but 'no early-out' is about verbosity and does not establish program lifetime. A fair catch.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_204943/ollama_minimax.txt"}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "—", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 2 -- NOT FIRED. Recorded by name: neither a seat failure nor agreement. **The seat most likely to compile and measure has never read this diff**, and that is an open question for the owner: fire it on the final artifact, or take an explicit exemption.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_204943"}, {"seat": "fable5", "phase": "review", "d": "—", "date": "2026-08-25", "note": "PASS 2 -- NOT FIRED as a review seat; held the ADJUDICATOR hat (dual-hat disclosed, `s` badge). Its readiness adjudication returned **HOLD-FOR** and found what the panel had not: `r16 value` was VACUOUS as built (its oracle passed on any echoed hex, green under all ten mutants), both width value rows checked the WRONG FIRE for the escape they cite, the accept-section comment invoked the wrong clause, the no-disk warrant in the struct comment is a non-sequitur, and the contract claimed a FILING that did not exist.", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260825_204943"}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-26", "ev": "_scratchpad/brief_wdcflood_final_opus.md (final-diff read; no build -- a kill table was running)", "note": "FINAL DIFF, FIRED AFTER PASS 2 TO CLOSE THE ROSTER GAP -- and it found what two panels and three adjudications had not. **VERDICT: SHIP THE CODE; the CONTRACT carries a third non-sequitur and it is load-bearing.** Read-only by instruction (a table was mid-run); it said so plainly and named the four mutants it would want instead of guessing. *** THE FINDING: MY CONTRACT WARRANTED FLAG-PAIRING SURVIVAL ON 'every row starts a FRESH emulator ... proving per-instance storage needs TWO controller instances, which this rig does not have'. THAT EXPLAINS THE PROCESS-GLOBAL CASE ONLY. *** Pairing survived because **NO ROW FIRES TWO DIFFERENT SITES IN ONE SESSION** -- a property of the ROW SET, not the rig, and fixable in three lines. It checked all 22 rows to confirm zero did (datawr x2 writes wd_data so the width check is never reached; overrun x2 uses legal 8-bit writes; both value rows seed and read 8-bit). **AN UNMEASURABLE CLAIM WAS HIDING A MEASURABLE ONE BEHIND IT** -- and the measurable half is the round's HEADLINE design decision. Shipped as `wr indep`. *** AND THE PER-INSTANCE HALF IS NOT IMPOSSIBLE EITHER -- 'this rig does not have' is true of landisk and FALSE OF THE FORK: *** `dev_pcic.c:234-242` adds TWO wdc instances unconditionally (0x14000180 and 0x140001f0; its own comment reads 'Yuck. Now there are two.'), reached from every hpcmips subtype. Verified. So that is COVERAGE DEBT, not an impossibility, and the contract now says which. *** A SIBLING-PARITY GAP IN THE CARRIER'S EXACT SENSE. *** `w16 reset` covers reset-on-normal-operation at :607 **BY ACCIDENT** -- it happens to place a legal 8-bit write between its two wide writes. Its mirror at :1025 had nothing: r16 x2 and r32 x2 fire back-to-back, r16 value seeds with a WRITE and checks values not counts, accept r8 is a separate session. So `d->warned_nonbyte_read = 0;` on a legal 8-bit read scored **22/22**. Same predicate, same message modulo WRITE/READ, added in the same hunk pair. Shipped as `r16 reset`. *** A SECOND ESCAPE FROM THE GUARD-FIELD/BODY-FIELD CHECK, VERIFIED BY READING: *** :583's guard reads `warned_unimpl_cmd` (:655) while its BODY reads `d->seccnt` (:660), a field the guest writes directly at :1003 and nothing else touches. So `if (!d->seccnt) { d->seccnt = 1; ... }` there scores a clean sweep, and no row builds a state where the two fields disagree. FILED. *** TWO CONTRACT BULLETS WERE UNSCOPED -- listed under DESIGNED TO CATCH while each was measured at exactly ONE site of six. *** Reset-on-normal was pinned only at :583; guest-resettable-operand only at :607. **A capability claimed generally and measured once is the overclaim the contract exists to prevent.** Both now name their pinned sites and their unpinned ones. *** THE SUBTLEST CATCH: A WRONG CITATION THAT LOOKS RIGHT. *** `dev_wdc.c:89` cited the shared #337 flag as spanning ':709 vs its else'. The round uses PRE-FIX numbering throughout and that convention is otherwise applied correctly -- the seat checked HEAD and withdrew its own wrong-citation finding on the others. But :89 is the ONE citation that ships INSIDE the file it points into, where a reader resolves it against the POST-FIX copy -- and post-fix :709 is `odata = (odata << 8) + wdc_get_inbuf(d);`, an inbuf line inside an endianness conditional, **which reads like a confirmation**. Replaced with a positional reference. IT ALSO STRENGTHENED THE :145 ARGUMENT RATHER THAN WEAKENING IT: the announcement was never a corruption detector to begin with, because the FAST BULK PATH laps the ring by direct pointer arithmetic without ever calling `wdc_addtoinbuf`, so it never announced there at all. That is a better warrant than 'the latch bounds the announcement'. AND IT RE-DERIVED TWO ARITHMETIC CLAIMS INDEPENDENTLY: 1026 x 512 = 525,312 = exactly 2 laps of WDC_INBUF_SIZE; and '500 iterations -> 2000 lines' falls out of the file, because a 32-bit read of wd_data calls `wdc_get_inbuf()` FOUR times. The round never stated that derivation; it corroborates the measurement from the source side. CENSUS CONFIRMED AGAINST THE FILE: exactly ten `fatal(\"` call sites, four #337's and six this round's; the two `printf()` at :391/:437 are inside `#if 0` and correctly excluded. Both gate pins verified by grep (10 and 12). All six guards wrap the announcement ONLY -- checked line by line. No latch is guest-clearable: `memset(d, 0, ...)` occurs once, in DEVINIT."}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-26", "ev": "_scratchpad/brief_wdcflood_askagain.md (THE ONE PERMITTED ASK-AGAIN; reasoned, not built -- a table was running)", "note": "*** THE CAPPED ASK-AGAIN, FIRED AGAINST A DETECTOR ALREADY CLEAN AT 24 ROWS -- AND IT FOUND ONE. *** Rule satisfied: found -> ONE row under L1 -> cap SPENT, no second query. **THE MUTANT: key the :607 latch on the ACCESS WIDTH instead of on a boolean.** `if (len != 1 && d->warned_nonbyte_write != (int) len) { d->warned_nonbyte_write = (int) len;` -- two lines, one hunk. A guest alternating `mov.w` / `mov.l` at wd_seccnt then gets ONE HOST LINE PER STORE, FOREVER: flag 0 != 2 -> line, flag = 2; flag 2 != 4 -> line, flag = 4; flag 4 != 2 -> line ... That is the pre-fix rate this round measured at 20 stores -> 20 lines. *** WHY NO ROW COULD SEE IT: 2 AND 4 NEVER CO-OCCUR IN A SINGLE SESSION. *** `w16 x2` fires (2,2); `w32 x2` fires (4,4); they are SEPARATE sessions. The reset rows interpose a `len == 1` access, which `len != 1` rejects BEFORE the flag operand is consulted, so they cannot re-key it. The seat walked all 24 sessions and tabulated the multiset of `len` values reaching each guard per session -- mechanically, not by eye. **:607 is the MOST heavily covered site in the file (six rows aim at it) and all six stay green**, which is what makes this a demonstration rather than a blunt break. **IT IS THE SAME CLASS THE ROUND ALREADY DECLARED A DEFECT AT :583** -- `warned_unimpl_cmd != cur_command`, killed by `unimpl 2op` -- transposed to a site where nothing could observe it. And it is a PLAUSIBLE maintainer edit, not an adversarial one: 'report each distinct bad width once' is natural, and :704's message prints `len=%i`, which invites it. *** THE GENERALISATION IS WORTH MORE THAN THE MUTANT, and it is one grep: *** the contract already asks *do two SITES share a session?* (`wr indep`). **Its DUAL was missing -- *for each site, does any ONE session present two DIFFERENT stimuli that both reach that site's guard?*** :583 yes (two opcodes); :607, :1025, :704, :160 and :145 all repeated an IDENTICAL stimulus. That is why the whole last-value-latch family sat unpinned at five sites of six. Closed at the two width sites by `w mixed` / `r mixed`; :704 remains open and is now named in the contract. *** AND IT REFUTED A CONTRACT LINE I HAD WRITTEN THAT SAME HOUR. *** I claimed reset-on-normal-operation was 'pinned at :583 (`interleave`)'. **VERIFIED FALSE IN SCOPE: `interleave`'s only valid opcode is 0xEC, against NINETEEN valid command cases** -- so the recorded kill covers one case in nineteen, and a reset hidden in `WDCC_RECAL`, `SET_FEATURES` or `WDCC_DIAGNOSE` sweeps clean. **Worse, a reset arriving through the SIBLING HANDLER is pinned NOWHERE**: `dev_wdc_altstatus_access` shares the same `d`, and zero rows in this file touch its address -- which is where a real driver's reset goes. Both verified by grep and both now scoped in the contract; filed as `wdcflagindep`. *** THE SEAT FLAGGED ITS OWN POSSIBLE RE-PROPOSAL RATHER THAN CLAIMING NOVELTY, AND THE REASON IS A DEFECT IN MY BRIEF: *** it could not tell whether the other-opcode reset was already covered, because **my exclusion list gave LABELS, not EDITS** -- the exact defect that same brief warned about two paragraphs earlier ('a seat once conflated two mutants because a brief showed labels only, and that was the brief's defect'). It said so plainly and left the call to me. *An exclusion list is a brief too, and it earns the same standard as the rest of one.* SCOPE OFFERED, NOT PROPOSED (all REASONED, none the headline): :704 takes the same edit and sweeps clean; keying it on `idata` is worse and also sweeps clean, since `datawr x2` writes 0x1234 both times. METHOD: read-only by instruction -- a kill table was running and its rows use fixed 8-second settle windows. It read all 1187 lines of dev_wdc.c, all 557 of the probe, `memory.h:149` for `len`'s type, and `wdcreg.h`; it labelled the finding REASONED and stated plainly it had not verified the mutant compiles. **The mutant's PREMISE is already measured, not assumed**: this round's own `len != 1` -> `len == 2` mutant is recorded as killing `w32 x2`/`r32 x2` ALONE, which measures that `len == 4` reaches the guard as a distinct value while `len == 2` leaves the 16-bit rows green. Only the control flow over the flag is reasoned, and it is four lines of straight-line C."}, {"seat": "fable5", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-26", "badge": "s", "ev": "Agent(model=fable) READINESS ADJUDICATION of the final artifacts; liveness token FABLE_ADJ_ALIVE_20260826C returned. NO panel dir -- an Agent seat leaves no file, which is the counting trap the carrier records.", "note": "*** DUAL HAT DISCLOSED: this is the fable5 SEAT read of the final artifacts AND the adjudication, held by one model. Badged `s`. It is recorded as ONE seat, never as two, and the seat-vs-adjudicator distinction the carrier draws is NOT satisfied by this entry alone. *** VERDICT: SHIP-WITH -- floor holds, seven bounded items before commit. EVIDENCE BASIS, stated on its face and therefore PROVISIONAL: every artifact on disk at read time -- five table dirs, 37 raw logs then extant, 8 results files, the probe, the dev_wdc.c diff, the gate diff, the ledger, both frozen table-5 scripts, the witness dir, three checker runs. It ran nothing heavier than sub-second read-only checkers, by choice, because a kill table was live. *** THE FIRST ITEM WAS A HARD CHECKER FAIL THE ROUND WOULD HAVE HIT AT PRECOMMIT: *** `check_bugfile_sync.py` reported BUGFILE_SYNC_FAIL on four open rows -- `wdcperopcode`, `wdcexpecteddata`, `wdcflagindep`, `wdchpcmipsaddr` -- filed in the ledger and absent from OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md. **The checker THIS ROUND repaired for exactly that shape caught the round that repaired it.** Written; now BUGFILE_SYNC_PASS. SEVEN ITEMS AND THEIR DISPOSITION, all closed except the gate run: (1) four bug-file entries -- WRITTEN, PASS. (2) the pre-fix line-number convention was claimed and ABSENT from the probe -- WRITTEN, and then immediately re-keyed: the post-fix NUMERIC column went stale ONE EDIT LATER when a six-line comment moved all six sites by four, so the map now keys on the FLAG NAME, which cannot rot. (3) clause (c) was measured at 22 rows and the shipped script is 26 -- RE-MEASURED: 0/26 on both media, 26 FAIL, zero survivors (table6_null26/). The floor was satisfied either way; the sentence 'every row fails' was unmeasured for four rows and now is not. (4) the gate comment said THE DENOMINATOR IS 22 above a check pinning 26 -- CORRECTED to 26 and TWELVE, with the four new rows named. (5) the witness artifacts lived in a VOLATILE session scratchpad the ledger cited -- COPIED to regress/artifacts/wdcflood/witness_20260825/ and the witness field re-pointed. n3.out is byte-identical after copy and is exactly 158,449 bytes, the figure three records cite. (6) the no-disk `debug()` sibling that the ORIGINAL FILING named was disposed of NOWHERE -- now named in the round block and the gate comment, as OUTSIDE scope rather than missed: `debug()` has the early-out `fatal()` lacks, so -q silences it. Different channel, different remedy; rides with `floodclass`. (7) one clean run of the never-executed gate section -- PENDING, post-table. *** IT ALSO CAUGHT FOUR DEFECTS IN THE ROUND BLOCK DRAFT, AND TWO OF THEM I HAD ALREADY FOUND BY AUDITING THE DRAFT AGAINST ITS OWN ARTIFACTS -- independent convergence, not duplication. *** (a) 'SIX stamped directories' when there were five -- an error I INTRODUCED while filling slots, the second time this round a CORRECTION carried an error; (b) 'the eight rows added after the pre-fix run' is TWELVE, a sentence true at 22 rows that went stale when the detector grew -- the same class as the gate comment; (c) **'ALL 26 with a measured kill' OVERCLAIMS by this project's own usage of 'kill'** -- measured 23+3: `live`, `accept w8` and `accept r8` redden ONLY on a null medium, and the draft should not be looser than the probe it describes; (d) the pre-fix flood numbers cited no artifact and their artifacts were about to evaporate. *** AND ONE OMISSION THAT WOULD HAVE BURIED A SIBLING: *** see item 6. Closing `wdcflood` as-drafted would have closed a queue id whose own filing named a defect the round neither fixed nor filed. ON Q2, THE CLAUSE-(b) MAPPING'S HOME: the probe header is primary and the round block owes only the pointer -- do NOT duplicate the six-line table into the CHANGELOG, because a second copy nothing checks is the antipattern the carrier documents twice. Followed. ON Q3, THE ASK-AGAIN CAP: confirmed spent -- found, two rows under L1, both mutants measured killed, so the L1 obligation is discharged BY KILLS and not by rows existing. **Its boundary is worth keeping: the cap governs the HUNT. The clause-(c) re-run, the record corrections and the bug-file sync are floor and record obligations, which the cap neither covers nor excuses.** A MEASUREMENT IT CORRECTED IN MY FAVOUR: table 5 was further along than CHECKPOINT.md said -- `Mwidr` had already landed and killed (25/26, RED `r mixed` alone), so an 'assume it kills' in my own question was already measured. AND ONE PIECE OF HOUSEKEEPING WORTH THE LINE: it spent real effort ruling out a silently lost mutant before concluding **there is no Mp2** -- the numbering always went Mp1/Mp3/Mp4. One sentence now in the round block spares the next reader the same search."}, {"seat": "codex", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-26", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260826_090604/codex.txt (794,806 chars). VERDICT: SHIP-WITH -- the shipped product-code change is sound; the record and detector each owe one thing.", "note": "FILE-READING, and it opened the right tree -- it names the six post-fix guards at 194, 220, 661, 689, 791, 1118 and says explicitly 'not another tree'. CONFIRMED, each against source: the dev_asc warrant holds (fatal() always reaches va_debug; debug() alone has the early-out at debugmsg.c:365/:375); **every guest-visible effect remains outside its guard**, enumerated one by one -- ring store and head advancement precede the overrun guard, `return (uint64_t)-1` is outside the over-read latch, WDCE_ABRT precedes the unimplemented-command latch, `idata = data[0]` follows the write-width latch, unexpected-DATA processing continues after its latch, and `data[0] = odata` with `return 1` stay in the common tail -- **'no early-return regression was introduced'**, which is the exact hazard the sketch rule exists for. *** IT RATIFIED THE ROUND'S DE-ESCALATION READING, WHICH WAS THE QUESTION MOST LIKELY TO MAKE THE RECORD FALSE: *** the clause fires when a fix REMOVES a host-fatal guard; these fatal() calls never terminated the host and none was removed, so `live`, `accept w8` and `accept r8` do NOT owe product-code kills and their null-medium reds satisfy non-vacuity. The round block said exactly this; it is now ratified by a seat that checked the clause text. TWO FINDINGS ACTED ON: (1) **[P2] a smaller escape** -- one line, `d->warned_nonbyte_write = 0;` in the wd_sector write arm -- source-proven to leave all 26 rows green while producing an unbounded flood. FILED as `wdcsectorreset` rather than fixed, because the seat itself labelled it un-measured. (2) **an L4 wrong record**: the round said the no-disk sibling 'floods identically'; codex re-derived from debugmsg.c that it is silent free-running at default verbosity, so 'identically' should mean identical REPETITION when the debug channel is enabled, not identical VISIBILITY. Corrected in place. *** AND THE CORRECTION'S PROVENANCE IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING: the qualifier was NOT missing from the evidence, it was missing from my summary. *** OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md's original filing already read 'invisible free-running at verbosity 0'. I kept the headline and dropped the scope -- the MIRROR of the silent-narrowing failure the carrier records, and it survives every check except comparing a summary against its source. It also held the line on flag pairing: `wr indep` proves only that the two WIDTH flags cannot share; ring-half sharing still passes and other cross-site sharing has no co-firing row. That is `wdcflagindep`, already filed, and the round may not claim more than it measured."}, {"seat": "grok", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-26", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260826_090604/grok.txt (11,582 chars). VERDICT: KEEP THE SHIPPED FIX.", "note": "FILE-READING. Independent agreement on the disposition, arrived at separately from codex."}, {"seat": "agy", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-26", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260826_090604/agy.txt (13,784 chars); answered, no verdict line (a format miss, not a silence).", "note": "FILE-READING. Answered on the diff."}, {"seat": "glm", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-26", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260826_090604/ollama_glm.txt (11,100 chars).", "note": "PACKET-FED. Answered on the inlined diff."}, {"seat": "deepseek", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-26", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260826_090604/ollama_deepseek.txt (3,910 chars).", "note": "PACKET-FED. Answered on the inlined diff -- notable because a read-the-source instruction in the same brief stalled minimax, and deepseek historically stalls on the same trigger."}, {"seat": "kimi", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-26", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260826_090604/ollama_kimi.txt (16,126 chars), SUBSTITUTE TRANSPORT kimi-k3:cloud.", "note": "PACKET-FED via the substitute. The kimi CLI was 403-walled again (348-byte error file, not a seat answer); the seat is COVERED by its substitute, not lost."}, {"seat": "minimax", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-26", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260826_090604/ollama_minimax.txt -- 150 bytes: its opening line only, 'I'll start by reading the actual files referenced in this diff'.", "note": "*** SEAT DID NOT ANSWER, AND THE CAUSE IS A DEFECT IN MY BRIEF, NOT IN THE SEAT. *** CLAUDE.md records that a brief containing a read-the-source instruction GUARANTEES a stall from minimax and deepseek, and that it will look exactly like a quota failure. My pass-2 brief line 40 says '**Check that warrant against the actual files rather than trusting it here.**' -- the documented trigger, written by me, in a brief sent to a packet-fed seat that cannot read files. It began exactly as the rule predicts and stopped. Recorded as a SEAT FAILURE with the cause named, never as agreement. **The remedy is the existing rule: inline every fact a cloud seat needs and open with 'do not plan to read files'.** The same brief did NOT stall deepseek, so the trigger is probabilistic rather than deterministic -- which is worth noting, because a rule stated as a guarantee and observed to fire once in two is a rule whose strength is overstated."}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "OK", "date": "2026-08-27", "ev": "regress/artifacts/wdcfloodstale/reaim_20260827/", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "note": "*** #486 REPAIRED THIS ROUND'S OWN CLAUSE-(b) CARRIER, WHICH HAD BEEN NON-DISCRIMINATING. ***\n\n`overrun x2` is #464's sole clause-(b) carrier at the ring producer.  MEASURED on the\ncommitted binary before #486: it reads OVERRUN = 0 on the shipped latch AND 0 on a build with\nthe latch DELETED.  Not merely red -- it could not tell the fix from its absence, so #464's\nfloor was open at that site with nothing saying so.\n\n#479 killed it: an unconditional `wdc_new_command_reset()` capped per-command occupancy at\n512, so 1026 IDENTIFY commands could no longer lap a 262,656-byte ring.  Proven by a\nsingle-variable control -- revert only that reset, keep #484's ring, and OVERRUN goes 0 -> 1.\n\n#486 re-aimed the stimulus at the guest data port, which takes no command and therefore no\nreset.  Now: shipped 1,1,0 ok; latch deleted 1,145,0 FAIL.  Probe 25/26 -> 26/26, with the\ngate's pinned row count unchanged at 26.\n\nThe commit is named here because work shipped for this row and a row that does not name its\ncommit cannot be traced back from the log -- the gate refused the next commit until it did."}], "closure": {"by": "fable5", "badge": "", "note": "CLOSE (assess)"}, "state_note": "*** STATUS FIRST, BECAUSE THIS NOTE OPENED WITH PRE-FIX GUIDANCE THAT THE SHIPPED FIX HAS ALREADY CONSUMED.  Added 2026-08-29. ***  **FIXED at HEAD** -- #464 (six unlatched guest-drivable `fatal()` sites, re-scoped from one arm to six), #485 (the completion interrupt, without which the abort was polling-only), #486 (repaired the round's own non-discriminating clause-(b) gate row).  A rung-3 witness with both admissibility controls is on the row.  **It stays `in work` for exactly two reasons: a minimax pass-2 seat failure owed a re-fire with a repaired brief, and closure adjudication.**  The title is the FOUNDING claim, which is this ledger's convention and not a wrong record -- but a reader meeting `in work` plus a '100 commands, 100 host log lines' title plus work-it-this-way guidance concludes the flood is live, which is the es438 / [[ledgerstatelies]] shape.\n\n  *** THE THREE-MEMBERED OPTION SET APPLIES DIRECTLY TO THIS ROW (carrier rule, minted 2026-08-25 from #463). *** #463 de-fatalised a device by framing the choice as KEEP the unlatched `fatal()` versus DELETE it, chose delete, and its own research stage then found the third member had been shipping in-tree the whole time: **`memory_rw.c:495-523` bounds a guest-drivable diagnostic to 8 emissions via `n_trunc_msgs`, because 'probing at the end of a device's mapped range is common and benign'.** The carrier already said 'a guest-drivable fatal() needs a latch and not a verbosity flag' -- standing rule, never consulted by that round's two-membered framing. **So before this row is worked: the option set is DELETE / DECLINE / DECLINE+BOUNDED-ANNOUNCE, and the first step is to grep the fork for the existing idiom rather than reason from first principles.** #463 also established that a bounded announce is not merely acceptable but RECOVERS the self-announcing property that de-fatalising destroys -- which for an unlatched flood is the whole point, since the flood and the announcement are the same channel.  *** MEASURED 2026-08-25, AND THE ROW IS AIMED AT THE WRONG SITE. *** The opus5 seat built a `cp -a` copy at /tmp/gxwdc, verified freshness independently of MAKE_RC, and left GXEMUL-SEC untouched (verified by `git status --porcelain`: no src file modified). *** THE FLOOD IS CONFIRMED AND IS WORSE THAN THE TITLE SAYS: *** 20 stores produce 20 `fatal()` lines WITH and WITHOUT `-q` (0 `debug()` lines either way), and the OVER-READ site at 500 iterations produced 2000 lines / 158,449 bytes of host log (`regress/artifacts/wdcflood/witness_20260825/n3.out`, preserved off the volatile session scratchpad this row used to cite). *** CORRECTED IN PLACE 2026-08-26: this sentence said \"the read path\", which points a reader at the non-8-bit READ guard. `n3.out` is 2000x \"reading too much\" -- the RING OVER-READ. Same numbers, wrong site named. The correction had already landed in the round block, the source comment and the gate comment while this field kept the false wording -- which is the corrections-land-in-CHANGELOG-only failure this ledger records having made twice before, now three times. It was found by a mechanical sweep for stale claims across all five records, not by reading. *** *** BUT `:583`, THE SITE THIS ROW NAMES, IS THE MORE GATED OF TWO FLOODS. *** It requires an attached IDE image (`:461-468` early-returns with a silenceable `debug()` otherwise) and yields ONE line per store. The unnamed sibling `wdc_get_inbuf` over-read at `:160` requires NOTHING -- landisk adds wdc unconditionally at machine_landisk.c:90 -- is on the READ path, and yields FOUR lines per single guest instruction (measured: mov.b/mov.w/mov.l x20 give 20/40/80). SIBLING CENSUS: ten `fatal()` in dev_wdc.c, FOUR latched by #337, SIX UNLATCHED -- :145, :160, :583, :607, :704, :1025. **Fixing :583 alone leaves the strictly worse :160 floodable at 4x with no precondition**, which is the project's recorded 'grep for its siblings' failure. SCOPE RULING SOUGHT: the round is the FILE, not the line -- six sites, one design, one detector. *** WHY #337 MISSED IT, AND IT IS EXPLICABLE RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT: *** that round's scope was \"each replaced `exit(1)`\", and `:583` was always a `fatal()`, never an `exit(1)`, so it fell outside the enumeration. *** THE OPTION-SET PREMISE WAS REFUTED, AND IT WAS MINE. *** The three-membered option set rule describes member 3 as \"announces the first N occurrences and then goes quiet\". That describes `memory_rw.c:495-523` -- which is the tree's ONLY counter-style bound, is DOUBLE-GATED so the counter never increments at default verbosity (`&&` short-circuits), is GLOBAL not per-instance, and prints no give-up notice. The house idiom is different and mature: **N=1 PER-INSTANCE LATCH plus an explicit \"(further occurrences suppressed)\" in the message text**, at roughly 34 sites across 11 device files. `dev_wdc.c` itself holds FOUR (`warned_badlen`, `warned_atapi_err`, `warned_atapi_dataout`, struct at :75-77) and is the tree's REFERENCE implementation -- dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:147 says its own latch is \"Modelled on dev_wdc.c's warned_badlen (#337)\". *** AND `fatal()` SHOULD BE KEPT, ON A RECORDED HOUSE WARRANT: *** dev_asc.c:250-257 states that under `-q` verbosity settles at VERBOSITY_ERROR, so a WARNING-level debugmsg would be INVISIBLE to the boot-log hygiene grep and an ERROR-level one would trip #261's break-on-ERROR. The chronology runs one way -- #245 chose verbosity-gating; #265/#269, #337, #460, #461 all chose latch + fatal(). TWO HAZARDS FOR THE DIFF, both from the seat: (1) SKETCH RULE -- DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc) has a common tail at :1019-1030 (`ret:` -> memory_writemax64 -> return 1), so any decline at the ACCESS level must `goto ret`; `wdc_command()` itself is void and called at :1003, so returning inside IT is safe. (2) CLAUSE (d) -- the guard would read the new `warned_*` field while the body reads `d->cur_command`, so the detector owes a state where a SECOND, DIFFERENT unimplemented command arrives after the latch has set; the 0x5a-vs-0xaa control is the seed for that row. OPEN QUESTION THE SEAT DECLINED TO SETTLE, recorded as open rather than guessed: #337 notes its two warned_badlen sites SHARE one flag, while #460's gdrom comment records a MEASURED warrant that per-site bits beat a shared bit when two sites run in one session. For pure flood control a shared latch is strictly better; the tradeoff is information loss, and which matters here is unmeasured. PRIVILEGE, stated exactly: no device-layer check exists and memory_rw dispatches on physical address, so \"unprivileged\" overstates it -- the exact claim is \"any code that can reach the I/O window, with no emulator-side gate\".  *** OWNER SCOPE DECISION 2026-08-25: THE ROUND IS THE WHOLE FILE -- ALL SIX UNLATCHED SITES, ONE DESIGN, ONE DETECTOR. *** Asked to choose between the filed single site, the two measured floods, and the file, the owner took the file. So the round covers :145, :160, :583, :607, :704 and :1025 using dev_wdc.c's own #337 idiom (per-instance latch + \"(further occurrences suppressed)\"), keeping `fatal()` on the dev_asc.c:250-257 warrant and keeping the existing `d->error |= WDCE_ABRT`. The reopening rule permits it: same device, same semantic class, one fix design, one detector, no new oracle. **Only :160 and :583 carry a rung-3 witness today; the other four are joined on the SAME-CLASS clause and each should get a one-store witness in pass 1, per #337's measured partial-file miss.**  *** PASS 1 (panel_20260825_195526): agy SETTLED Q1 WITH AN ARGUMENT NOBODY HAD MADE, AND IT DISSOLVES THE EVIDENCE CONFLICT RATHER THAN PICKING A SIDE. *** The brief posed shared-vs-per-site latching as #337 (shares `warned_badlen` across two sites) versus #460 (measured warrant for per-site bits). **Neither is wrong: #337's two sites are the two arms of ONE ENDIANNESS BRANCH.** VERIFIED BY READING: dev_wdc.c:709 is `if (cpu->byte_order == EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN) {` containing :731, and :739 is its `} else {` containing :761. A CPU does not flip endianness mid-session, so those two are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE AT RUNTIME and sharing a flag loses exactly zero information. #460's warrant governs sites that can BOTH fire, which is all six here. **DESIGN SETTLED: SIX separate per-instance latches** (or one bitfield with six masks), never shared across read/write or overrun/over-read pairs. *** Q2 SETTLED: NO SUPPRESSION NOTICE. *** Census verified independently by agy against all of `src/devices/`: only dev_asc.c (3 sites) and dev_vga.c (1) carry it, and dev_wdc.c's own four #337 sites do not. Adding it to six new sites would make the file internally inconsistent; editing the four existing ones is unjustified churn into working code. *** Q3 ANSWERED, AND IT CARRIES AN IMPLEMENTATION TRAP THE NAIVE FIX WALKS INTO. *** `:160` is a GUEST error and is safe to latch -- real hardware returns open bus on a read past the transfer, and the emulator returns `(uint64_t) -1`. **BUT the `return (uint64_t) -1;` MUST STAY OUTSIDE the latch guard.** Nesting it inside `if (!d->warned_...)` makes the SECOND over-read fall through to `d->inbuf_tail = (d->inbuf_tail + 1) % WDC_INBUF_SIZE;` and CORRUPT THE RING POINTER -- the fix would introduce a defect worse than the flood it removes. Same shape at `:1025`, inside the common tail: guard the `fatal()` only; `data[0] = odata;` and `return 1;` run regardless. *** Q5: agy CAUGHT AN INTERNAL CONTRADICTION IN MY OWN BRIEF. *** The proposed design specifies scalar `warned_*` fields while Q5 demanded a detector catch a per-device-versus-per-opcode mutant -- which would require a 256-bit bitmap (`uint32_t warned_unimpl_cmd[8]`). Resolution: the scalar per-site latch is correct (it is what stops a guest cycling 0x00..0xff for 256 lines), and **the TRUE clause-(d) mutant is a different one: scope `d->error |= WDCE_ABRT` INSIDE the latch guard at :583.** Then the second unimplemented command silently SUCCEEDS. A detector that counts only host log lines passes it clean. That is a better (d) mutant than the one the brief proposed and it is the round's headline detector obligation. TWO FURTHER EVASIONS from the same seat: nesting the `return -1` at :160 (above), and using `static int warned` instead of a per-instance field, which silences a secondary IDE controller at 0x170 because the primary at 0x1f0 already warned. *** Q7 WITNESS COSTS, one of which is a genuine obstacle: *** `:607` = a 16-bit write to wd_seccnt; `:1025` = a 16-bit read from wd_status; `:704` = a store to wd_data with no active command -- all cheap. **`:145` is NOT cheap: WDC_MAX_SECTORS is 512 and WDC_INBUF_SIZE is `512*(512+1)` = 262,656 bytes (verified by reading dev_wdc.c:47-48), so filling the ring by guest stores costs ~65,664 32-bit stores.** The disk-read fill path at :343 moves 512 bytes per sector and would be far cheaper, but it needs an attached image -- i.e. it needs `landiskdisk` first. ALL THREE of agy's checkable claims were VERIFIED against source rather than credited: the endianness branch, the buffer arithmetic, and that wdc_get_inbuf() is called once per BYTE inside 16/32-bit transfers (:616, :620, :622, :623 little-endian; :627, :629, :630 big-endian), which is exactly the measured 4 lines per 32-bit guest load.  *** FULL PASS-1 PANEL COMPLETE (panel_20260825_195526): 8 SEATS ANSWERED. *** codex 501 KB, grok 19.5 KB, minimax 13.6 KB, agy 13.2 KB, kimi-k3:cloud 10.8 KB (the SUBSTITUTE transport, fired by hand -- the CLI is 403-walled), glm 4.4 KB, deepseek 4.1 KB. The kimi CLI is the only seat failure and it is a transport wall, not a seat outage. *** Q2 IS UNANIMOUS ACROSS EVERY SEAT THAT ANSWERED IT: NO SUPPRESSION NOTICE. *** *** Q1 SPLIT 3-1-2, AND THE SPLIT IS DECIDED BY EVIDENCE RATHER THAN COUNT. *** SIX separate latches: agy, grok, kimi. FIVE plus a per-opcode set for :583: deepseek. FOUR (pairing): glm, minimax. **The two pairing seats both cite #337's shared `warned_badlen` as the licence -- and that precedent is measurably not a licence.** agy and grok independently derived that #337's two sites are the two arms of ONE ENDIANNESS BRANCH (:709 `if (cpu->byte_order == EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN)`, :739 its `} else {`), so they are mutually exclusive at runtime and sharing loses nothing. grok put it exactly: *\"two endian copies of one check, not a licence to pair different events.\"* kimi added an independent refutation from THIS ROUND'S OWN WITNESS: the :160 over-read fired with NO DISK ATTACHED, so no overrun could have occurred -- **the two ring halves are causally independent, which is precisely what the pairing assumes they are not.** DESIGN SETTLED: six per-instance flags, wrap ONLY the `fatal()`, leave every guest-visible side effect outside the guard. *** Q3: FOUR SEATS INDEPENDENTLY LOCATE THE REAL DEFECT AT :145, NOT :160 -- see `wdcringambig`, filed. *** glm: *\":145 is an emulator error, do NOT latch without fixing the ring overrun.\"* minimax: *\":145 IS a real defect that the latch does not fix -- it corrupts the ring and continues, so a TODO is owed there.\"* kimi located it first and most precisely. grok: *\":145 is the unfixed pckbc/asc wrap, not a twin of the measured :160 flood.\"* Only agy said :160 was the site to worry about, and it was answering the question as the brief framed it. *** Q5 WAS CONFUSED IN THE BRIEF AND THREE SEATS SAID SO. *** grok: *\"Q5's per-opcode (d) mutant contradicts the per-instance design the brief actually proposes -- that mutant is a spec change, not a floor test.\"* minimax and agy said the same. deepseek dissents usefully in the other direction: :583's diagnostic VALUE is the opcode, so a single flag hides distinct guest actions and a 256-bit set would preserve it. **Disposition: per-instance scalar for the fix (it is what stops a guest cycling 0x00..0xff for 256 lines); deepseek's per-opcode proposal is a SPEC IMPROVEMENT and is filed, not folded in.** The real (d) mutants, from three seats: scope `d->error |= WDCE_ABRT` INSIDE the latch at :583 (agy -- the second unknown command then silently SUCCEEDS); nest the `return (uint64_t) -1;` inside the guard at :160 (agy -- corrupts inbuf_tail); and **reuse a GUEST-RESETTABLE field such as `d->seccnt` as the latch (glm)** -- the sharpest of the three, because the guard then reads a field the guest controls. *** THE :145 WITNESS IS NOT A ONE-STORE WITNESS, AND codex SUPPLIED THE TWO HONEST ROUTES: *** prime `head = WDC_INBUF_SIZE - 1, tail = 0` in the detector and call the producer once; or, for a real guest-path measurement, attach an image and issue **513 undrained IDENTIFY commands** -- each adds 512 bytes through `wdc_addtoinbuf`, and the final byte of the 513th reaches the 262,656-byte wrap. The second route needs `landiskdisk`. *** AND A DETECTOR RULE FROM codex THAT APPLIES TO EVERY ROW: each site row must FIRE THE SITE TWICE. \"A one-off first warning proves announcement, not suppression.\" *** A row that triggers a site once cannot distinguish a working latch from no latch at all -- which is this project's vacuity floor, stated for the latch class specifically.  *** A WRONG RECORD IN THIS ROW, WRITTEN BY ME AND CAUGHT BY THE ADJUDICATOR. CORRECTED 2026-08-25. *** Earlier in this note minimax is attributed the position that :145 is a real defect the latch does not fix, and is COUNTED AMONG SEATS SAYING DO-NOT-LATCH. **That count is FALSE.** minimax's Q3 says verbatim: **\"So: latch both.\"** Its position is latch-and-mark, which is the disposition this round adopted -- it was recorded as dissenting from a ruling it agreed with. *** THE DON'T-LATCH CAMP IS glm ALONE *** (\"`:145` is an emulator error, do NOT latch without fixing the ring overrun\"), which turns a 2-of-8 reading into 1-of-8. **AND glm's OWN FALLBACK SILENCES HARDER THAN THE LATCH IT OBJECTS TO:** it proposes \"downgrade it to `debug()`\", and `debug()` has the early-out at `core/debugmsg.c:375` -- free-running at `verbose == 0` gives `v < 0`, so it vanishes ENTIRELY in production, while a latched `fatal()` still announces the first occurrence and cannot be silenced by `-q`. The dissent's remedy is strictly worse for the property the dissent defends. **Weigh deepseek's Q5 dissent knowing its ring analysis was the panel's weakest:** its \":145 is a guest error -- the guest wrote more data than the ring can hold\" is factually off. The EMULATOR writes that ring on the guest's behalf; the defect is state representation. *Second time in two days that a seat-count in this project's records was wrong in the direction flattering the writer's own ruling. The tally was never the argument -- but a misquoted seat is a wrong record whichever way it points.*  *** ADJUDICATED 2026-08-25 (fable5, main-loop hat, on 8 of 9 mandated seats -- PROVISIONAL on its face w.r.t. the absent ninth, per that seat's own rule). *** **(1) Q5 UPHELD, two sharpenings.** Scalar per-device flag at :583 this round; deepseek's per-opcode set FILED. Per-opcode-versus-per-device is a SPEC choice, and deepseek's framing (\"the scalar IS the (d) mutant\") is circular -- it assumes per-opcode is the baseline and then calls the alternative a weakening. Clause (d) tests the CHOSEN spec's repair; it does not adjudicate between two acceptable specs. Put per-device in the CONTRACT (clause e) -- \"does not claim per-opcode uniqueness at :583\" -- so a later alternating-opcode observation is OUTSIDE the contract rather than a floor breach. **SHARPENING 1, the best single point of the panel: the two-different-opcodes row SHIPS ANYWAY, with the expectation INVERTED -- ONE LINE TOTAL.** Same stimulus deepseek wanted, opposite oracle. It kills a last-warned-opcode implementation (under which an alternating 0x5a/0xaa guest floods forever) AND pins the per-device spec in one row. **SHARPENING 2: the filed item inherits a TWO-membered framing and must not.** Honest weakening of my own precedent: #337's latched messages at :816 and :855 carry NO VALUE PAYLOAD -- :583 is the only site whose message names an opcode -- so \"three latches in this file\" is weaker precedent for discarding value-resolution than it looks. The filed option set is THREE-membered: scalar (shipped), per-opcode set (deepseek), and the in-tree COUNT-BOUND idiom (`n_trunc_msgs`, `src/cpus/memory_rw.c:495-523`) -- which this row already banners and which nobody in the dispute named. **(2) `wdcringambig` DOES NOT BLOCK.** The latch bounds the announcement to first occurrence; it does not silence it. **The corrupting store is ALREADY SILENT TODAY** -- :141-146 stores, advances, then tests, so the wrap line fires once per 262,656-byte lap and the N+1th store that destroys data prints nothing. What the latch removes is repetition of a late, miscorrelated warning. THREE OBLIGATIONS make it honest, all in-round and cheap: (a) /* #NNN */ comments at :145 and :160 naming `wdcringambig` -- \"latch bounds the announcement; the ring invariant is untouched\"; (b) the detector CONTRACT disclaims ring health, and the :145 primed-state row is marked for re-pin under the oracle-correction rule when `wdcringambig` lands; (c) file glm's dissent VERBATIM into `wdcringambig` as provenance. **THE TEMPTING THIRD PATH WAS CHECKED AND REJECTED FOR A MEASURED REASON.** grok and kimi both prefer restructuring :145 to the pckbc #288 shape (check-before-store, drop, latch -- verified in-tree at `dev_pckbc.c:158-167`). It must NOT fold in, and not merely by the reopening rule: **the restructure makes head==tail <=> empty sound ONLY on the byte path.** The bulk producer at :343-349 compares only against WDC_INBUF_SIZE and can land head exactly on tail with NO WARNING AT ALL (:876-879 mirrors it on tail). *pckbc had ONE producer; wdc has THREE.* A byte-path-only repair ships a half-invariant under a record that reads as soundness. **(3) SEQUENCING: `landiskdisk` SPLITS IN TWO.** The PROBE-level -d attach is part of `wdcflood`'s own witness battery and blocks nothing -- verified: :583 is unreachable without a disk (:461-468 early-returns), so the opus flood measurement necessarily ran disk-attached and the route is already proven. The BATTERY-RIG integration -- the half carrying the 28-34% NINSTRS poison -- is an independent deliverable and NOT a blocker. **The primed-head/tail row is a RUNG-1 DETECTOR row and cannot discharge :145's witness**; the 513-IDENTIFY route can, and was verified sound: no ring reset exists at command start (the only head/tail writes are :143, :165, :347-349, :876-879), IDENTIFY feeds wdc_addtoinbuf 512 bytes per command (:525-533), and 513 x 512 = 262,656 = WDC_INBUF_SIZE exactly. **(4) THE REPETITION RULE: DO NOT MINT, and my dichotomy was wrong on BOTH horns.** Clause (a) does NOT cover it -- (a) is DETECTOR-level and the defect is ROW-level: a one-off row at a latched site prints one line pre-fix and one line post-fix, colourless in both worlds, so it contributes nothing to (a) and hides behind rows that do. **But no new clause is needed, because clause (d) already forces it mechanically:** the fix adds six latch predicates, (d)-DEPENDS-ON mandates a null-repair mutant per predicate (flag never set), and the only row shape that reddens a nulled latch is one firing the same site twice with an EXACT count. Codex's sentence is the row-construction corollary of (d) for the latch class -- the same relation the accept-row failability rule bears to the de-escalation clause. Two overlapping statements of one rule is what the carrier refuses by name. **Disposition: one derivation sentence in the round block, not a carrier clause.** If a future latch round ships a SURVIVING one-off row, that measured escape is the (f)-style warrant to mint. **REFINEMENT ON THE ROWS (minimax): repetition with a \"<= 1 line\" oracle passes a mutant that prints ZERO. The oracle must be EXACTLY ONE, THEN ZERO** -- announcement AND suppression, which is the pair a latch actually promises.  *** PASS 2 (panel_20260825_204943) FOUND THREE ESCAPES AGAINST A DETECTOR MEASURED 14/14, AND ONE OF THEM IS A STRUCTURAL BLINDNESS OF THE ROWS RATHER THAN A GAP IN THEM. *** All three are L1 -- pre-ship construction, fixed in-round, not filed. **(1) glm -- THE RESET-ON-NORMAL-OPERATION ESCAPE, and it is the sharpest.** ONE line, reusing the existing flag, guard and branch, added inside an existing case for a VALID command: `d->warned_unimpl_cmd = 0;`. **Every site row fires its site TWICE IN SUCCESSION with no normal device operation between**, so the flag is never reset between fires and the row stays green -- while a real guest that probes an unimplemented opcode, issues IDENTIFY, probes again, issues IDENTIFY, floods forever. The same one-line trick applies at every one of the six sites. *This is the `#463` distinctness-invariance finding wearing new clothes: rows that test a property back-to-back are invariant under any edit that only changes what happens BETWEEN the two fires.* **(2) agy -- THE COVERAGE CENSUS, CONFIRMED AGAINST MY OWN PROBE.** \"The detector's six site rows do not test six distinct C sites\": `:145` has **ZERO** rows while its sibling `:160` has two, and `:583` has three. Also **the non-byte rows probe 16-bit ONLY** -- `mov.w` at both :607 and :1025, never `mov.l`, so a `len != 1` guard narrowed to `len == 2` would escape. **(3) deepseek -- `d->warned_X = !d->warned_X;`**, claimed to toggle so that fires 1 and 3 print while the detector only fires twice. **READING SAYS THIS DOES NOT WORK** -- the assignment sits INSIDE the guard (`if (!d->warned_unimpl_cmd) { d->warned_unimpl_cmd = 1; ...}`), so fire 2 never enters and the toggle never runs. **RECORDED AS A PREDICTION TO BE MEASURED, NOT AS REFUTED** -- this project settles disagreements by measurement, and the seat may be modelling a placement I have not considered. It goes in the second table either way, because the row that would kill it kills a whole CLASS. *** THE ROWS OWED, AND THE THIRD IS THE GENERAL ONE: *** `interleave` -- fire the site, issue a VALID command (0xEC IDENTIFY), fire again, demand ONE line; this is what kills glm's escape. `w32`/`r32` -- 32-bit non-byte access at :607 and :1025, closing agy's width gap. **`fire3` -- fire ONE site THREE times and demand exactly one line**, which kills the toggle class and any period-N latch at once, and is the cheapest row of the three. `:145` remains DISCLOSED-NOT-COVERED in the contract; agy's point stands but the contract already says so. *** AND AN HONEST RECORD ABOUT PROCESS: THE CARRIER'S PRE-TABLE CHECK WOULD HAVE CAUGHT agy's FINDING AND I DID NOT RUN IT. *** Its FIRST item is SIBLING PARITY -- *for each arm the diff touches, compare its observable channels against its nearest sibling arm; an asymmetry is a row you owe*. `:145` with zero rows beside `:160` with two IS that asymmetry, visible by counting, before any mutant was built. The check exists precisely because a table re-run is expensive, and this round is now paying that cost: the first table was measured against a 14-row detector and adding rows changes other mutants' scores, so a SECOND table is owed. **The check costs three greps and about thirty minutes; skipping it cost a table.**  *** THE FULL PASS-2 PANEL (8 of 8 answering; the kimi CLI walled and COVERED BY ITS SUBSTITUTE -- panel.sh reported that correctly for the first time) FOUND AN L2 FLOOR BREACH, TWO WRONG RECORDS IN SHIPPED TEXT, AND A DESIGN CLAIM WITH NO ROW BEHIND IT. *** **(L2 -- FLOOR BREACH, clause (b), grok) `:145` HAS NO DETECTOR ROW, SO THE FIX HAS SIX FIXED SITES AND THE DETECTOR SEES FIVE.** Clause (b) requires failure on a verbatim reinstatement at EVERY fixed site; reinstating `:145`'s unlatched form escapes clean. grok's verdict was DO-NOT-SHIP-AS-A-SIX-SITE-FLOOR and it is right. **Confirmed by counting my own probe: the six site-x2 rows cover FIVE sites -- `P_OVERRUN` appears only in `disjoint`, which fires an unimplemented command and never touches the ring.** The 8/14 -> 14/14 colour change is five tested sites plus `unimpl 2op`. Fix in-round, however late: either add a `:145` row or narrow the fix to five sites. **(L4 -- WRONG RECORD IN SHIPPED PROBE TEXT, codex and grok independently) THE HEADER CONTRADICTS ITS OWN CONTRACT.** It claims to catch *removal of any of the six per-instance latch guards* while the contract eight lines below excludes `:145`. It catches FIVE unique sites. **(L4 -- `disjoint` PROVES LESS THAN ITS NAME, codex.** It exercises only the unimplemented-command transcript, so it proves THAT stimulus matches one regex -- not that all six patterns map uniquely. The row's note overstates it. *** (DESIGN CLAIM WITH NO ROW, codex -- and this is the one I would have missed for good.) 14/14 DOES NOT ENFORCE SIX INDEPENDENT FLAGS. *** Every site test starts a NEW emulator (15 `session()` calls), so **pairing the read/write width flags, or the two ring flags, would pass every isolated row -- as would a PROCESS-GLOBAL latch**, unless one session exercises two controller instances. The round's headline design decision -- six flags, not four -- is therefore UNMEASURED by its own detector. codex adds the mitigating half, checked: the implementation does use six separately initialised per-instance fields, `DEVINIT(wdc)` zeroes the whole allocation, and none is reset or aliased later. **So the CODE is right and the DETECTOR cannot prove it** -- which under clause (e) must be disclosed, not claimed. **(ESCAPE, codex) An inserted `return 1;` immediately after the non-byte-read `fatal()`** leaves the FIRST 16-bit read's buffer untouched while the second runs normally -- and `r16 x2` checks only the line count. Needs a VALUE oracle, not a count. **(ESCAPE, ollama_kimi) `idata = data[0]` at the write-width site is a FOURTH guest-visible side effect** and has no pin row; my Q2 list named three. *** THE EPOCH-LATCH QUESTION (ollama_kimi and minimax, independently) IS ANSWERED AND THE ANSWER IS NO. *** They asked whether a guest-reachable device reset re-zeroes the `warned_*` flags, which would make the shipped code an epoch latch no row can see. Checked by reading: the only `memset(d, 0, sizeof(struct wdc_data))` is at :1126, immediately after the malloc at :1125 in DEVINIT. `COMMAND_RESET` (:484) and `ATAPI_SOFT_RESET` (:601) exist and neither re-zeroes the struct. **A real question, cheaply settled, and worth recording as a negative result so nobody re-asks it.** **(RECORDS, minimax) MY OWN BRIEF'S ROW ARITHMETIC DID NOT ADD UP:** it enumerated 2 controls + 6 site + 3 accept + 1 disjoint = 12 against a stated 14. The two unstated rows are the side-effect rows `abrt 2nd` and `overread -1` -- which are the most interesting rows in the file and the brief left them out of its own census. **(CHALLENGE, minimax) `fatal()` RETURNING IS ASSUMED IN THE :1025 COMMENT, AND \"no early-out\" DOES NOT ESTABLISH IT** -- no-early-out is about verbosity, not program lifetime. The claim is true and this project settled it on 2026-08-21 by reading `core/debugmsg.c:384-390`, but the comment should CITE that rather than lean on the adjacent no-early-out fact. A fair catch. **(USEFUL FOR `wdcringambig`, ollama_kimi) A WASTE-ONE-SLOT DISAMBIGUATOR IS NOT FORBIDDEN BY CAPACITY:** 512 x 512 = 262,144 usable is still <= 262,655, so a max-length sector transfer still fits. Only the 513-undrained-IDENTIFY fill exceeds it. **Any future claim that \"waste-one breaks the exact-fit sizing\" is wrong**, and that is worth carrying to the ring round before it invents the objection.  *** KILL TABLE 1 -- 8 MUTANTS AGAINST THE 14-ROW DETECTOR, AND ONE ESCAPED. *** Preserved at `regress/artifacts/wdcflood/table1_14rows/` (12 files + a README stating what the numbers mean and why the directory is stamped). Tree restored and cmp-verified against SEC, sentinel cleared, zero live processes. SCORES, every reddened row re-extracted with an ANCHORED trailing-`FAIL` match: `base` 14/14 (identity -- the harness scores the unmutated copy exactly as SEC does); `Mb1` 12/14 delete the :583 guard -> `unimpl x2`, `unimpl 2op`; `M1` 12/14 null the repair, flag never set -> same two; `M2` 11/14 SET BEFORE ANNOUNCE, zero lines -> those two plus `disjoint`; `M3` 13/14 last-warned-opcode -> **`unimpl 2op` ALONE**; `M4` 13/14 ABRT moved inside the guard -> **`abrt 2nd` ALONE**; `M5` 13/14 `return -1` moved inside the guard -> **`overread -1` ALONE**; `M6` **14/14 -- ESCAPED, ZERO ROWS REDDENED**. *** THE THREE SINGLE-ROW KILLS ARE THE USEFUL RESULT, NOT THE SCORES. *** `M3`, `M4` and `M5` each redden exactly ONE row, and those three rows exist for exactly those escapes -- nothing else in the file covers them. `abrt 2nd` and `overread -1` are the two rows that read a guest-visible SIDE EFFECT rather than a line count, and they are the only rows that can see a repair swallowed by a guard. *** M2 IS THE MEASURED VINDICATION OF AN ORACLE CHOICE. *** It sets the flag before the test so the site emits ZERO lines. **A `<= 1 line` oracle would have passed it**; `exactly 1` catches it. That choice was argued from a seat's reasoning and is now measured. *** AND M6 IS THE ESCAPE, PREDICTED BY A SEAT ON PASS 2 AND CONFIRMED HERE. *** It swaps the latch operand for the GUEST-RESETTABLE `d->seccnt`: `if (len != 1 && !d->seccnt) { d->seccnt = 1; fatal(...); }`. Every row in table 1 fires its site TWICE IN SUCCESSION, so nothing between the fires ever puts the operand back and the mutant looks perfectly latched. A guest that writes `seccnt = 0` between wide writes floods without bound. **This is a STRUCTURAL blindness of the row design, not a missing mutant** -- the same shape as `#463`'s distinctness invariance, where rows that assert a property back-to-back cannot see an edit that changes only what happens BETWEEN the assertions. *** DETECTOR NOW 22 ROWS, MEASURED 22/22 ON THE FIXED BUILD (`table2_22rows/base_22rows.raw`). *** Seven rows added, each closing something table 1 or pass 2 exposed: `overrun x2` (the :145 row -- **this is what closes the L2 floor breach**, and it needed a hardware loop, `dt r3` / `bf` with a computed displacement, because 513 undrained IDENTIFYs unrolled would be 513 debugger commands); `interleave` (fire, VALID command, fire); `w16 reset` (fire, guest CLEARS the operand, fire -- the M6 killer); `fire3` (kills period-N latches, not just period-2); `w32 x2` and `r32 x2` (the width rows probed 16-bit only, so a guard narrowed to `len == 2` escaped); `r16 value` and `w16 value` (value oracles at the two width sites, for a `return` inserted after a latched `fatal()` -- which a count-only row cannot see). Row hygiene checked: no name over 13 chars, no duplicates, no name that is a prefix of another followed by a space. TABLE 2 is running against the 22-row detector with three mutants table 1 could not have had: `Mb2` removes the :145 guard verbatim (the clause-(b) reinstatement at the site that had no row), `M7` is the reset-on-normal-operation escape, `M8` is the period-2 latch. All three anchors were verified to occur EXACTLY ONCE in the source first -- **a mutant that fails to apply scores a false clean**, which is the worst possible failure for a kill table. *** A CAPTION DEFECT IN THE APPARATUS, FOUND AND FIXED AT SOURCE. *** `killwdc.sh` wrote its header at start with the row count and the pre-fix score as LITERALS (`14 rows`, `8/14`). The moment pass 2 forced seven more rows, table 2's own header became wrong about its own size. The script now DERIVES the count (`grep -c '^row(' ...`) and stamps a UTC start time instead of quoting scores it cannot know. Table 2's already-written header is stale and is corrected in place; the script fix stops it recurring. *A table that misreports its own size cannot be cited safely at all.*  *** KILL TABLE 2 IS CLEAN: 22-ROW DETECTOR, TEN MUTANTS, **ZERO ESCAPES**, EVERY LOG INTERNALLY CONSISTENT. *** Preserved at `regress/artifacts/wdcflood/table2_22rows/` with a header that DERIVES its row count rather than quoting one. Tree restored and cmp-verified against SEC, sentinel cleared, zero live processes. base 22/22; Mb1 18/22; M1 18/22; M2 17/22; M3 21/22; M4 21/22; M5 21/22; M6 21/22; M7 21/22; M8 21/22; Mb2 21/22. **Consistency check on all eleven logs: score + anchored-red count == 22 in every one**, which is the check that would catch a contaminated extractor. *** SIX ESCAPES, SIX SINGLE-ROW KILLS, EACH BY THE ROW BUILT FOR IT -- and that one-to-one correspondence is the result, not the scores: *** M3 last-warned-opcode -> `unimpl 2op` ALONE; M4 ABRT inside the guard -> `abrt 2nd` ALONE; M5 `return -1` inside the guard -> `overread -1` ALONE; M6 guest-resettable operand -> `w16 reset` ALONE; M7 reset-on-normal-operation -> `interleave` ALONE; Mb2 the :145 verbatim reinstatement -> `overrun x2` ALONE. **Nothing else in the file sees any of them.** *** THE L2 FLOOR BREACH IS CLOSED BY MEASUREMENT. *** `Mb2` is the exact edit that swept CLEAN against the 14-row detector -- removing :145's guard verbatim -- and it now reddens `overrun x2`, the row that did not exist when the breach was found. Clause (b) holds at all six fixed sites. *** M8 VINDICATES A CAUTION, AND THE CAUTION IS THE TRANSFERABLE PART. *** deepseek proposed `d->warned_X = !d->warned_X;` as a period-2 escape. I read its SPECIFIC edit as unworkable -- the assignment sits INSIDE the guard, so fire 2 never reaches it -- and recorded it as **\"a prediction to be MEASURED, not refuted\"**. M8 constructs the same class differently (toggle BEFORE the guard) and escapes a two-fire oracle exactly as deepseek said the class would. **Wrong about the placement, RIGHT ABOUT THE CLASS.** Declaring it refuted on the strength of a correct reading of one construction would have lost a real escape. *A seat can be wrong about the mechanism and right about the family; the family is the part worth building a row for.* *** CLAUSE (c), THE NULL MEDIUM, IS NOW MEASURED AND IN ITS STRONGEST FORM: 0/22 ON BOTH. *** `nullmedium_absent.raw` (a binary that does not exist) and `nullmedium_notanemu.raw` (a real executable that is not an emulator) each score **0/22 -- every row fails, not merely some**. It had NOT been verified before; the question was raised against the round's own readiness and answered in seconds. **AND THE MEASUREMENT EXPOSED A DESIGN PROPERTY THAT WAS ACCIDENTAL RATHER THAN INTENDED:** the three accept rows expect ZERO diagnostics, so a naive implementation would PASS against silence -- zero is exactly what silence produces. They fail correctly only because `n()` returns `\"died\"` rather than `\"0\"` when the run produced nothing (probe :227-228). **That single line is what stops every accept row in this file from being vacuous under clause (c)**, and it was not written for that reason. Worth carrying to any future detector with absence-shaped oracles: an \"expect 0\" row MUST distinguish 'ran and saw nothing' from 'did not run'.  *** THE READINESS ADJUDICATION RETURNED **HOLD-FOR** AND FOUND TWO SHIPPED ROWS VACUOUS. ASKING BEFORE THE TABLE FINISHED IS WHAT MADE THAT POSSIBLE -- a clean sweep invites ratification. *** **`r16 value` WAS VACUOUS AS BUILT.** Its oracle was `printed(t,\"r2\") != \"absent\"` against a wanted `\"read\"`, so it passed whenever the debugger echoed ANY hex value -- structurally incapable of failing while the emulator lived. **Confirmed: green under every one of table 2's ten mutants -- it did not exist for table 1, which measured a 14-row detector, and an earlier note said 'tables 1 and 2', which is false.** The `live` control already covered session death, so the row measured nothing at all. **AND BOTH WIDTH VALUE ROWS CHECKED THE WRONG FIRE.** The escape they cite -- a `return` inserted after a latched `fatal()` -- corrupts the FIRST access (flag unset, guard entered, return skips the repair); the SECOND finds the flag set, skips the guard and repairs normally. Both rows read the value ONCE, after both fires, and so saw a perfectly good value. The contract line claiming they carried value oracles \"for exactly that edit\" was FALSE AS BUILT. Repaired to check BOTH fires, with a low-byte mask because the repair sets only `data[0]` and asserting the upper byte would make the row about the wrong thing. *** TABLE 3 EARNS THE REPAIRED ROWS THEIR MEASURED KILL, WHICH IS WHAT A REPAIRED ROW OWES: *** `table3_repaired/`, base 22/22. `Mv1` (delete `idata = data[0]`, the write-side repair) 18/22 -> r16 value, w16 value, overrun x2, accept ec. `Mv2` (delete `data[0] = odata`, the read-side repair inside the `ret:` tail) 18/22 -> devsig, abrt 2nd, r16 value, w16 value. **`Mv3` (codex's EXACT edit -- insert `return 1;` after the latched READ `fatal()`) 21/22 -> `r16 value` ALONE.** *** Mv3 IS THE ONE THAT PROVES THE REPAIR RATHER THAN THE ROW. *** It corrupts only the first access, so **the ORIGINAL one-value form would have scored 22/22 against it** -- the escape would have shipped invisibly behind a row whose whole stated purpose was to catch it. One row in twenty-two sees it, and it is the repaired one. COLLATERAL WORTH READING RATHER THAN DISMISSING: `Mv2` reddens `devsig`, because deleting `data[0] = odata` breaks the status read the device-signature control depends on. That is the control doing its job -- a device that cannot answer through its own decode is exactly what `devsig` exists to notice -- not a spurious red. Tree restored and cmp-verified against SEC, sentinel cleared, zero live processes. All three logs internally consistent (score + anchored reds == 22). *** THE STANDING LESSON, and it is the second time in one round: A REPAIRED ROW IS NOT A ROW UNTIL A MUTANT KILLS IT. *** Both rows looked correct after repair and one of them still could not have caught the edit it was written for. Reading a row and believing it is exactly the state the vacuity floor exists to refuse.", "witness": "probe:regress/artifacts/wdcflood/witness_20260825/ (rung 3, MEASURED 2026-08-25 by opus5) -- real SH4 guest instructions through real decode and real memory_rw on the UNMODIFIED committed `-E landisk` description (machine_landisk.c:87-90, addr_mult=2, wd_command at physical 0x1400000e). Both admissibility controls present: LIVENESS (implemented command 0xec yields ZERO lines, so the rig can produce zero) and DEVICE-SIGNATURE (storing 0x5a echoes `command 0x5a` x20 and `command 0xaa` x0 -- the guest's own byte, which RAM cannot produce). No device was added to reach it."}
{"id": "wdcperopcode", "title": "wdcperopcode  wdc_command()'s unimplemented-command latch is per-DEVICE, so a guest cycling opcodes gets ONE line and 255 silent aborts -- a SPEC choice, filed not fixed", "state": "open", "commits": "", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "hold": "", "witness": "n/a -- this is a SPEC improvement, not a defect. The shipped behaviour is deliberate and is pinned by the `unimpl 2op` row, which fires TWO different opcodes and demands ONE line.", "state_note": "*** FILED 2026-08-26 BECAUSE THE SHIPPED DETECTOR'S CONTRACT CLAIMED A FILING THAT DID NOT EXIST. *** `wdc_flood_probe.py`'s header says *\"deepseek argued for a per-opcode set; FILED, not shipped\"* -- and no row carried it; the only trace was prose inside `wdcflood`'s state_note, which is not something a reader can find. **A contract line asserting a disposition is a WRONG RECORD until the disposition exists as a row.** Caught by the readiness adjudication before the round shipped. THE PROPOSAL, from the deepseek seat on the `wdcflood` pass-1 panel: `:583`'s diagnostic VALUE is the opcode, so a single per-device flag suppresses every later unimplemented command and hides distinct guest actions. A 256-bit set (`uint32_t warned_unimpl_cmd[8]`) is cheap and preserves them. WHY IT WAS NOT SHIPPED, and the reason is not that it is wrong: the scalar is exactly what stops a guest cycling 0x00..0xff for 256 lines, which is the flood the round exists to bound. Per-opcode-versus-per-device is a SPEC choice between two acceptable behaviours, and the adjudication ruled that clause (d) tests the CHOSEN spec's repair rather than adjudicating between specs -- deepseek's framing (\"the scalar IS the (d) mutant\") is circular, since it assumes per-opcode is the baseline and then calls the alternative a weakening. *** THE SHIPPED SPEC IS PINNED, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THIS SAFE TO DEFER: *** the `unimpl 2op` row fires TWO DIFFERENT opcodes and demands ONE line, so a later change to per-opcode is a VISIBLE spec change that reddens a row, not a silent drift. The same row kills a \"last-warned-opcode\" latch (measured: `M3`, 21/22, that row alone), under which an alternating guest floods forever. IF IT IS EVER WORKED, the option set is THREE-membered and the third member is the one the dispute never named: scalar (shipped), per-opcode set (deepseek), and the in-tree COUNT-BOUND idiom (`n_trunc_msgs`, `src/cpus/memory_rw.c:495-523`). Note also that #337's other latched messages at `:816`/`:855` carry NO value payload, so \"three latches in this file\" is weaker precedent for discarding value-resolution than it looks -- `:583` is the only site whose message names an opcode."}
{"id": "gdromallocpair", "state": "open", "commits": "", "title": "gdromallocpair  alloc_data() does not release before it allocates -- #465's free_data() pairing is CONVENTIONAL, not STRUCTURAL", "witness": "none -- documented-only. READ and verified 2026-08-26: no third call site exists today, so there is nothing to reproduce. The hazard is what a future caller would do, which is why it is FILED rather than fixed.", "state_note": "*** FOUND BY THE GROK SEAT ON #465's PASS 2, VERIFIED HERE BY READING. *** `free_data(d)` runs ONCE at the function entry (`:343`); both `alloc_data(d)` sites (`:359`, `:435`) are downstream, so today the buffer is always released before it is replaced. **But `alloc_data()` overwrites `d->data` unconditionally with no release of its own** -- a future THIRD call site not preceded by that entry release leaks the previous buffer, silently. *** THE SHAPE IS THE POINT, and it is #465's own lesson one level up: *** that round added `free_data()` as `alloc_data()`'s counterpart precisely because two fields set together were being released apart. The pairing it established is CONVENTIONAL -- every current path happens to do it -- rather than STRUCTURAL, where the unpaired form is impossible. That is exactly the distinction the round's own corrected comment now draws, after a pass-2 seat refused the word 'unrepresentable'. NOT FIXED, deliberately: a release inside `alloc_data()` is an ADDITION to product code, and the addition licence requires a measured delta or a transferred warrant. **Neither exists yet -- with no third caller, no mutant can measure the difference.** The honest disposition is to record the hazard with its warrant so a round that ADDS a third call site inherits the obligation. Related, same seat: `d->cnt` is guest-writable at `GDROM_CNTLO`/`CNTHI` and reaches `data_len` only through `alloc_data()`. Assessed as a related pair, not this defect."}
{"id": "pitclobber", "title": "pitclobber  An 8253 LATCH command clobbers the counter's programmed RW format and counting mode, which a real 8254 never does", "state": "in work", "commits": "020a672", "hold": "FIX SHIPPED (#439, 020a672); CLOSURE HELD FOR TWO REASONS, and the second is the substantive one. (1) The flagship review of the diff is held by owner allocation. (2) *** PASS 2 FOUND A REAL DEFECT THAT IS NOT YET FIXED: the latch is never CONSUMED, so after one latch the counter returns 0 indefinitely. *** Handed to the in-flight pitflip round because the consume semantics interact with the LSB/MSB flip-flop that round is fixing. Closing now would record as finished a round whose own pass 2 found it incomplete.", "closure": {"by": "fable5", "badge": "", "note": "CLOSE (regress and review)"}, "entries": [{"seat": "opus5", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "READ, NOT MEASURED -- graded honestly, and it is a sibling of pitlatch rather than part of it. dev_8253.c's mode-write arm is UNCONDITIONAL:\n\n    d->mode_byte = idata;\n    d->counter_select = (idata >> 6) & 3;\n    d->mode[d->counter_select] = idata & 0x0e;\n\nA real 8254 COUNTER-LATCH command does NOT disturb the programmed RW format or the counting mode -- it only latches the count. Here a latch write CLOBBERS mode_byte (destroying the RW format the guest set earlier) and ZEROES d->mode[counter], because the latch command's mode bits are 0.\n\nSO EVEN A GUEST THAT NEVER READS THE LATCHED VALUE HAS HAD ITS COUNTER SILENTLY REPROGRAMMED. That is a distinct consequence from pitlatch's (a latch-then-read returns 0 and floods the host log): this one damages state on the WRITE alone.\n\nReachability inherits pitlatch's: OpenBSD's microtime() issues the latch command (_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/i386/isa/clock.c:207-209), so the clobber happens on every call. NOT MEASURED -- no probe has yet shown the downstream effect of the corrupted RW format, and the honest grade is READ until one does. The pitlatch panel was asked prong by prong whether this travels in the same round or its own.", "grade": "MANUAL", "ev": "src/devices/dev_8253.c (read while writing the pitlatch design brief)"}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "*** THE DRIVING SEAT REFUSED THE PANEL'S DESIGN AND MEASURED WHY. ***  Four of six seats converged on 'make the latch non-clobbering and non-fatal, RETURN THE RELOAD VALUE, and document that it is not a live count'. The seat BUILT that as candidate C1 and measured it causing a GUEST DIVIDE-BY-ZERO: OpenBSD's findcpuspeed() (_guest-src-openbsd22/.../clock.c:333-344) programs the counter to 0xffff and divides by (0xffff - gettick()); under C1 gettick() returns 0xffff, so the divisor is 0.\n\nAND C1 DID NOT EVEN DELIVER WHAT IT PROMISED: it returned the HIGH BYTE TWICE (0x2e2e, not the reload 0x2e9c), because pitflip leaves the LSB/MSB selector stuck at MSB. The 'return the reload value' option does not return the reload value.\n\n*** THE SEAT-CLASS DOCTRINE DECIDED IT, NOT THE COUNT. ***  The three seats voting for the reload value were the PACKET-FED Ollama seats, which PIPELINE.md says are never equivalent votes on repo fact. BOTH FILE-READING seats that answered (agy, grok) warned against it, and kimi flagged the same hazard class. Codex was a SEAT FAILURE at that panel, not agreement. A 4-2 count concealed a 0-2 split among the seats that could open the file.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED (#439): the latch is ACCEPTED, latches NOTHING, and disturbs NO state -- a latched[3] flag, a write-arm branch returning before it touches mode_byte/mode[], and a read-arm branch returning 0 with the limitation documented in the code and in the file header. Guest-visible values are unchanged from the committed build, so it cannot regress anything. pitclobber travels with it: one fix design ('the latch disturbs no state') covers both arms.\n\nMEASURED, rung 3, unmodified -E cats, PIT at 0x7c000040, five controls green in every run (RAM liveness, CP15 MMU-off readback, a device signature RAM cannot produce, an MMU-ON negative control, encodings checked against the emulator's own disassembly):\n    committed HEAD  mode_byte after latch 0x00 (DESTROYED), latched reads 0x00 0x00,\n                    two 'huh? reading from counter 0' complaints\n    shipped         mode_byte after latch 0x24 (PRESERVED), 0 complaints,\n                    findcpuspeed divisor 65535 (safe)\n\nTHE DETECTOR KILLS PER VARIANT, INCLUDING THE OVER-FIX:\n                    R1 clobber   R2 false error   R3 divisor\n    committed HEAD     FAIL          FAIL            ok\n    C1 panel shape      ok            ok            FAIL = 0\n    C2 read-arm only   FAIL           ok             ok\n    shipped             ok            ok             ok\nR3 exists specifically to catch the plausible over-fix the panel recommended.\n\n*** THE SEAT CAUGHT A VACUITY BUG IN ITS OWN FIRST DRAFT: *** the rows read absent registers as ok (-1 == -1), so three 'kills' were runs that never started -- an execvp PATH bug. Rows now fail on absent data (R0), and R0 is why the table above can be believed.\n\nFOUR CLAIMS IN MY BRIEF MEASURED FALSE, and one of them I had propagated all evening: fatal() is NOT 'byte-for-byte debug()' -- debug() returns early under quiet_mode/verbosity and fatal() never does, so a fatal() flood CANNOT BE SILENCED even with -q, which makes every flood row MORE severe. The guest function is gettick(), not microtime(). d->mode[] is behaviourally inert (written in three places, read only inside a debug() printf), so pitclobber's only guest-visible effect is via mode_byte. And 'return the reload value' does not return the reload value.\n\nGates: gate_hygiene PASS 48, gate 2 PASS 259. dev_8253.c byte-identical in est/, build/ and /tmp/gxsec-build -- the arc tree was found STALE and synced. gate_hygiene's EXPECT_CONVERTED 17->18, measured read-only with the gate's own definitions before and after staging; that coupling has gone red three times.\n\nOPEN: the probe ships UNWIRED (gate_hygiene's own note says wiring is the structural fix but it needs an operator who can run gate_arm.sh), and pitflip is untouched -- it is why C1 returned the high byte twice.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "regress/pit8253_latch_probe.py"}, {"seat": "fable5", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "ADJUDICATOR CONTRIBUTION TO THIS ROUND -- recorded as what it is, not dressed up as a panel seat's brief answer. Fable held the ADJUDICATOR hat on 2026-08-20 and its docket opened with this round: 'Item 0 -- land the in-flight pitlatch+pitclobber round; do not contend.'\n\n*** AND IT MADE ONE SUBSTANTIVE CORRECTION THAT CHANGED THIS ROW'S RECORD: *** 'the PIT panel's \"no merges, five rounds\" ruling PREDATES pitclobber (filed after that panel). The pairing is therefore UN-RULED, not ruled-against -- the round record must justify it explicitly under the third-arm test (same device, same LATCH-command semantic class, one fix design). It plausibly passes; it must say so.'\n\nThat was right and I had it wrong: I told the driving seat 'the panel folded pitclobber in'. The CLUSTER panel never saw pitclobber; the pitlatch DESIGN panel did, because I wrote the write-side clobber into that brief myself. The justification exists -- from the design panel, not the cluster panel -- and the closure note now says which.\n\nNOT a substitute for a fable5 review of the diff. The regress review remains FABLE-ONLY and is held and queued below.", "grade": "MANUAL", "ev": "Fable adjudication docket, 2026-08-20"}, {"phase": "regress", "seat": "fable5", "date": "2026-08-22", "verdict": "", "evidence": "UNKNOWN", "note": "CLOSE (regress and review). The named defect is fixed and witnessed; the substantive hold -- the latch never consumed -- is discharged by af4884c with its kill table and the write-rewind mutant measured. R3 existing specifically to FAIL the design the panel majority recommended is the strongest detector-as-record form in this tree, and the C1 measurement is the settle-empirically rule and the seat-class doctrine working in one motion.", "d": "✓"}, {"seat": "agy", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "agy answered the pitlatch design panel. Assessed as part of the pitlatch DESIGN panel: the brief carried this defect explicitly at line 70, '## A SECOND DEFECT ON THE SAME PATH (write side)', and ALL SEVEN scriptable seats addressed it by name (clobber / mode_byte). Recorded on this row rather than left at 2/9, because the seats genuinely saw it -- but recorded as what it was: a section of another row's brief, not a panel convened on this row. It is why the two close together under one fix design.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_135613/"}, {"seat": "grok", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "grok answered the pitlatch design panel. Assessed as part of the pitlatch DESIGN panel: the brief carried this defect explicitly at line 70, '## A SECOND DEFECT ON THE SAME PATH (write side)', and ALL SEVEN scriptable seats addressed it by name (clobber / mode_byte). Recorded on this row rather than left at 2/9, because the seats genuinely saw it -- but recorded as what it was: a section of another row's brief, not a panel convened on this row. It is why the two close together under one fix design.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_135613/"}, {"seat": "kimi", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "kimi answered the pitlatch design panel. Assessed as part of the pitlatch DESIGN panel: the brief carried this defect explicitly at line 70, '## A SECOND DEFECT ON THE SAME PATH (write side)', and ALL SEVEN scriptable seats addressed it by name (clobber / mode_byte). Recorded on this row rather than left at 2/9, because the seats genuinely saw it -- but recorded as what it was: a section of another row's brief, not a panel convened on this row. It is why the two close together under one fix design.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_135613/"}, {"seat": "glm", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "glm answered the pitlatch design panel. Assessed as part of the pitlatch DESIGN panel: the brief carried this defect explicitly at line 70, '## A SECOND DEFECT ON THE SAME PATH (write side)', and ALL SEVEN scriptable seats addressed it by name (clobber / mode_byte). Recorded on this row rather than left at 2/9, because the seats genuinely saw it -- but recorded as what it was: a section of another row's brief, not a panel convened on this row. It is why the two close together under one fix design.", "grade": "BELIEVED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_135613/"}, {"seat": "deepseek", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "deepseek answered the pitlatch design panel. Assessed as part of the pitlatch DESIGN panel: the brief carried this defect explicitly at line 70, '## A SECOND DEFECT ON THE SAME PATH (write side)', and ALL SEVEN scriptable seats addressed it by name (clobber / mode_byte). Recorded on this row rather than left at 2/9, because the seats genuinely saw it -- but recorded as what it was: a section of another row's brief, not a panel convened on this row. It is why the two close together under one fix design.", "grade": "BELIEVED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_135613/"}, {"seat": "minimax", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "minimax answered the pitlatch design panel. Assessed as part of the pitlatch DESIGN panel: the brief carried this defect explicitly at line 70, '## A SECOND DEFECT ON THE SAME PATH (write side)', and ALL SEVEN scriptable seats addressed it by name (clobber / mode_byte). Recorded on this row rather than left at 2/9, because the seats genuinely saw it -- but recorded as what it was: a section of another row's brief, not a panel convened on this row. It is why the two close together under one fix design.", "grade": "BELIEVED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_135613/"}, {"phase": "assess", "seat": "codex", "date": "2026-08-20", "verdict": "", "evidence": "UNKNOWN", "note": "(HELD-AWAITING-SEAT codex -- DEFANGED 2026-08-29 (2nd sweep, same-stage criterion): codex answered 2026-08-20 in this same stage.  Literal broken on purpose: three tools read it functionally.) Walled at the time this panel fired (13:56); codex answered a health test later the same day and the eleven EARLIER panels were re-fired, but this one was not -- it postdates the discharge batch. Named rather than left blank."}, {"seat": "codex", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "*** DISSENT, RECORDED AND NOT ADOPTED -- and it is the ninth seat arriving after the fix shipped. ***  Codex was ECHO-ONLY when this panel fired (7042b = brief 6354b + 688b); it answered a health test later the same day and was re-fired against the byte-identical brief.\n\nITS VERDICT: 'SPLIT -- suppress the false read-side error here, document the zero fallback, and reopen write-side state and real-count semantics separately.'\n\nSo it would have shipped pitlatch's read-side half alone and made pitclobber its own round. THE SHIPPED FIX PAIRED THEM. The pairing is justified rather than assumed, which is what the adjudicator required: one fix design -- 'the latch disturbs no state' -- covers both arms of one command, which is the reopening rule's third prong. Six seats on this design panel treated them together because the brief carried both, and the adjudicator ruled the pairing 'plausibly passes; it must say so'.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT ADOPTED, stated so a reader can disagree with me: under the stopping rule a review finding is fixed in-round only if it is a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG RECORD, and this is a SCOPE PREFERENCE -- codex names no defect in what shipped. Unpicking a gated, probe-witnessed change to satisfy a scope opinion would trade measured safety for tidiness. *** BUT IT AGREES WITH THE PIT CLUSTER PANEL'S OWN 'no merges' INSTINCT, and that is worth a reader's attention: two independent panels leaned toward splitting this device's defects and one brief -- mine -- put two of them together. If a future round finds the pairing caused trouble, this entry is where the warning was. ***\n\nIts other half, 'real-count semantics separately', is already the standing position: the file's own TODO at line 35 and #439's header both say the count is unmodelled.", "grade": "MANUAL", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_135613/codex.txt.rerun"}, {"seat": "codex", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "PASS 2 ON #439 -- 7 OF 7 SCRIPTABLE SEATS, THE FIRST FULL SCRIPTABLE PANEL OF THIS CAMPAIGN. Codex answered for real (20,699 bytes) after ten consecutive echo-only walls.\n\n*** SIX SEATS SOUND; CODEX DEFECTIVE -- AND CODEX WAS RIGHT. ***  'The latch is never consumed, so the selected counter returns zero indefinitely.' Verified in source: latched[] is SET at :212, cleared ONLY at :219 by a non-latch mode write, and NEVER cleared by the read at :163. After one latch command, every subsequent read of that counter returns 0 until the guest happens to rewrite the mode byte. A real 8254 CONSUMES the latch per the programmed RW format.\n\nThe seat that had been WALLED for the pass-1 panel is the one that found it, which is the clearest argument this campaign has produced for discharging a wall rather than recording it and moving on. glm gestured at the same thing ('the sticky latch').\n\nNOT A REGRESSION: pre-#439 a latch clobbered mode_byte and subsequent reads also fell to the default arm returning 0, plus an unsilenceable flood. But it is a real defect in the fix, and SHIP-WITH-FIX is this project's practice on a pass-2 finding.\n\nHANDED TO THE IN-FLIGHT pitflip ROUND rather than fixed separately, and the reason is technical not merely practical: the consume semantics INTERACT WITH THE LSB/MSB FLIP-FLOP. In 16-bit mode a real latch delivers TWO bytes before reverting -- and the selector is exactly what pitflip is fixing. Two rounds editing dev_8253.c at once would also contend.\n\nTHE REFUSAL OF THE PASS-1 MAJORITY IS VINDICATED. The six SOUND verdicts converged on the brief's substantive question -- agy: 'returning 0 avoids guest kernel divide-by-zero crashes, preserves programmed mode state'; deepseek: 'the least harmful of three wrong answers and not a guest-visible regression'; minimax: 'the write-side fix matches the 8254 and removes a real bug'. Nobody defended the reload-value design that the implementing seat built and measured harmful. What was missed by everyone, including me, was the consume.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_174300/"}, {"seat": "agy", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "SOUND -- 'returning 0 avoids guest kernel divide-by-zero crashes, preserves programmed mode state, and cleanly silences the false error.' A FILE-READING seat.\n\nPASS 2 ON #439, 7 of 7 scriptable seats -- the first full scriptable panel of the campaign. THE SIX SOUND VERDICTS CONVERGED ON THE BRIEF'S SUBSTANTIVE QUESTION and NOBODY defended the reload-value design that the pass-1 majority had recommended and the implementing seat built and measured causing a guest divide-by-zero. So the refusal of that majority is VINDICATED by the seats themselves.\n\n*** WHAT ALL SIX MISSED, AND CODEX DID NOT: the latch is never CONSUMED. *** latched[] is cleared only by a non-latch mode write, never by the read, so after one latch the counter returns 0 indefinitely. Handed to the in-flight pitflip round because the consume semantics interact with the LSB/MSB flip-flop that round is fixing. It was my own Q4 in the brief and I did not check it before shipping.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_174300/"}, {"seat": "grok", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "answered at 10.2 KB. A FILE-READING seat, and one of the two that warned against the reload value at pass 1 -- the warning the divide-by-zero measurement vindicated.\n\nPASS 2 ON #439, 7 of 7 scriptable seats -- the first full scriptable panel of the campaign. THE SIX SOUND VERDICTS CONVERGED ON THE BRIEF'S SUBSTANTIVE QUESTION and NOBODY defended the reload-value design that the pass-1 majority had recommended and the implementing seat built and measured causing a guest divide-by-zero. So the refusal of that majority is VINDICATED by the seats themselves.\n\n*** WHAT ALL SIX MISSED, AND CODEX DID NOT: the latch is never CONSUMED. *** latched[] is cleared only by a non-latch mode write, never by the read, so after one latch the counter returns 0 indefinitely. Handed to the in-flight pitflip round because the consume semantics interact with the LSB/MSB flip-flop that round is fixing. It was my own Q4 in the brief and I did not check it before shipping.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_174300/"}, {"seat": "kimi", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "43.9 KB, the longest answer of the pass. A FILE-READING seat.\n\nPASS 2 ON #439, 7 of 7 scriptable seats -- the first full scriptable panel of the campaign. THE SIX SOUND VERDICTS CONVERGED ON THE BRIEF'S SUBSTANTIVE QUESTION and NOBODY defended the reload-value design that the pass-1 majority had recommended and the implementing seat built and measured causing a guest divide-by-zero. So the refusal of that majority is VINDICATED by the seats themselves.\n\n*** WHAT ALL SIX MISSED, AND CODEX DID NOT: the latch is never CONSUMED. *** latched[] is cleared only by a non-latch mode write, never by the read, so after one latch the counter returns 0 indefinitely. Handed to the in-flight pitflip round because the consume semantics interact with the LSB/MSB flip-flop that round is fixing. It was my own Q4 in the brief and I did not check it before shipping.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_174300/"}, {"seat": "glm", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "SOUND -- 'returning 0 is the least harmful of three wrong answers and is not a guest-visible regression'. It ALSO gestured at the sticky latch codex named outright, so two seats saw the shape and one stated it. Packet-fed.\n\nPASS 2 ON #439, 7 of 7 scriptable seats -- the first full scriptable panel of the campaign. THE SIX SOUND VERDICTS CONVERGED ON THE BRIEF'S SUBSTANTIVE QUESTION and NOBODY defended the reload-value design that the pass-1 majority had recommended and the implementing seat built and measured causing a guest divide-by-zero. So the refusal of that majority is VINDICATED by the seats themselves.\n\n*** WHAT ALL SIX MISSED, AND CODEX DID NOT: the latch is never CONSUMED. *** latched[] is cleared only by a non-latch mode write, never by the read, so after one latch the counter returns 0 indefinitely. Handed to the in-flight pitflip round because the consume semantics interact with the LSB/MSB flip-flop that round is fixing. It was my own Q4 in the brief and I did not check it before shipping.", "grade": "BELIEVED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_174300/"}, {"seat": "deepseek", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "SOUND -- 'the least harmful of three wrong answers and not a guest-visible regression'. Packet-fed.\n\nPASS 2 ON #439, 7 of 7 scriptable seats -- the first full scriptable panel of the campaign. THE SIX SOUND VERDICTS CONVERGED ON THE BRIEF'S SUBSTANTIVE QUESTION and NOBODY defended the reload-value design that the pass-1 majority had recommended and the implementing seat built and measured causing a guest divide-by-zero. So the refusal of that majority is VINDICATED by the seats themselves.\n\n*** WHAT ALL SIX MISSED, AND CODEX DID NOT: the latch is never CONSUMED. *** latched[] is cleared only by a non-latch mode write, never by the read, so after one latch the counter returns 0 indefinitely. Handed to the in-flight pitflip round because the consume semantics interact with the LSB/MSB flip-flop that round is fixing. It was my own Q4 in the brief and I did not check it before shipping.", "grade": "BELIEVED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_174300/"}, {"seat": "minimax", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "SOUND -- 'the write-side fix matches the 8254 and removes a real bug; the read-side choice is the only one of the three that is safe'. Packet-fed.\n\nPASS 2 ON #439, 7 of 7 scriptable seats -- the first full scriptable panel of the campaign. THE SIX SOUND VERDICTS CONVERGED ON THE BRIEF'S SUBSTANTIVE QUESTION and NOBODY defended the reload-value design that the pass-1 majority had recommended and the implementing seat built and measured causing a guest divide-by-zero. So the refusal of that majority is VINDICATED by the seats themselves.\n\n*** WHAT ALL SIX MISSED, AND CODEX DID NOT: the latch is never CONSUMED. *** latched[] is cleared only by a non-latch mode write, never by the read, so after one latch the counter returns 0 indefinitely. Handed to the in-flight pitflip round because the consume semantics interact with the LSB/MSB flip-flop that round is fixing. It was my own Q4 in the brief and I did not check it before shipping.", "grade": "BELIEVED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_174300/"}, {"phase": "review", "seat": "fable5", "date": "2026-08-22", "verdict": "", "evidence": "UNKNOWN", "note": "CLOSE (regress and review). The named defect is fixed and witnessed; the substantive hold -- the latch never consumed -- is discharged by af4884c with its kill table and the write-rewind mutant measured. R3 existing specifically to FAIL the design the panel majority recommended is the strongest detector-as-record form in this tree, and the C1 measurement is the settle-empirically rule and the seat-class doctrine working in one motion.", "d": "✓"}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "*** I CLOSED THESE AND THE GATE REFUSED, AND ON RE-READING THE GATE HAS THE BETTER ARGUMENT. ***  I set them closed on the strength of the fix shipping and gating; section H answered CLOSED-WHILE-HELD because the flagship review is held. But the stronger reason is one the gate could not know and I should have: THE ROUND'S OWN PASS 2 FOUND AN UNFIXED DEFECT IN IT. codex's 'the latch is never consumed' is real, verified in source, and is being fixed in the pitflip round -- so #439 is not finished, and marking it closed would have recorded a round as complete on the same day its review said otherwise. Reverted to in-work. The fix stands, is gated, and is not a regression; only the CLOSURE waits.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_174300/"}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "*** commits READ THE LITERAL STRING 'PENDING' UNTIL NOW, AND SECTION K ACCEPTED IT. ***  I wrote it while recording #439 before the commit existed and never came back. A review seat found both halves: the wrong record, and the hole that hid it -- check_witness.py skips only an EMPTY commits field, so 'PENDING' satisfied the witness gate exactly as a real SHA would. Corrected to 020a672.\n\nThe general lesson is the one this project keeps paying: a field nothing validates is a field that drifts. Two rows legitimately carry prose (deadman, pkbrief) for work with no commit, so the fix is not 'demand hashes' -- it is to tell a DELIBERATE non-repo note from a PLACEHOLDER nobody returned to. That check now exists in check_commit_attribution.py.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "tools/pipeline/check_commit_attribution.py"}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "*** THE DISSENT AGAINST PAIRING WITH pitlatch IS ANSWERABLE ON THE MERITS, AND THE ROUND GAVE THE WEAK REASON. ***  The recorded justification -- 'a scope preference is not a measured false pass' -- is procedural and would retro-justify any pairing. The substantive answer is already in #439's own detector table: *** the SPLIT proposal IS candidate C2 ('read-arm only'), and C2 FAILS R1. ***  Splitting ships a build where the latch is silently accepted on the read side while the write side still destroys mode_byte -- a known-red row in the round's own oracle. That is a refutation on the merits, not a scope preference.\n\nUnderlying it: these are not two defects. They are the read side and the write side of ONE COMMAND BEING UNIMPLEMENTED, so prong 3 is satisfied literally.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "#439 detector table, candidate C2 vs row R1"}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "*** WITNESSED NOW. The row's own entry said 'Not witnessed by this round's probe'; that is no longer true. ***  MEASURED, arm X, and by the most standard PC PIT initialisation there is:\n    CW 0x34 -> counter 0 = 0x2e9c\n    CW 0x54  (counter 1, LSB-only -- the classic DRAM-refresh value)\n    write counter 1\n    read counter 0 as a pair  ->  *** 0x9c9c, the low byte twice ***\nIDENTICAL ON HEAD AND #440, so NOT a regression -- but it is the same 'one byte twice' symptom #440's headline says it fixes, reachable with no guest misbehaviour at all, and #440's per-counter flip-flops now sit underneath a GLOBAL format byte, which is the inconsistency.\n\nCODEX INDEPENDENTLY REACHED THE SAME DEFECT BY READING and called it a blocker for #440 (VERDICT: DO NOT SHIP). It is not a blocker -- it is pre-existing, and the reopening rule bars it from that round -- but two seats finding it in one pass, one by measurement and one by reading, is the strongest signal this row has ever carried. Kimi reached the pre-existing-not-blocking conclusion too.\n\nThe fix is bigger than it looks: the format needs per-counter storage used by the resolver, the latch-release test, the toggle test and every other format-dependent path -- not just one more array.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_183311/ (7/7 scriptable) + opus5 measuring seat"}], "witness": "none needed -- the defect was fixed by `#439` (`020a672`), which also shipped `regress/pit8253_latch_probe.py` (308 lines) as its detector.", "state_note": "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: THE DEFECT WAS REAL AND IS ALREADY FIXED. The row has been open describing work that shipped. Recommend CLOSE. ***\n\nA farmed seat returned FALSE with a specific citation, and it was verified here rather than taken on trust. **Commit `020a672` -- *#439 the 8253 counter-latch: the driving seat REFUSED the panel's design and measured why* -- touched `src/devices/dev_8253.c` (+38) and added `regress/pit8253_latch_probe.py` (308 lines).**\n\nThe guard is at `dev_8253.c:303-308` and its own comment states exactly what the row claims is broken, in the past tense:\n  > *\"The counter-latch command (RW == 00) latches a counter so it can be read; it does NOT reprogram it, so the RW format in mode_byte and the counting mode in mode[] have to survive it. **Writing idata through here unconditionally used to destroy both.**\"*\n\nThe latch arm now sets `latched[]` and `break`s before reaching the `mode_byte = idata` / `mode[] = idata & 0x0e` writes that did the damage.\n\n**SECOND INSTANCE TODAY of an open row describing a fixed defect** -- see [[smnotland]], fixed the same day it was assessed. Two instances make this a CLASS, not an accident; it is recorded on [[baretitle]].\n\n"}
{"id": "shprotld", "title": "shprotld  Every SH TLB row runs privileged, so the READ-side protection code is unreachable and corrupting it is invisible to all 31 rows", "state": "open", "commits": "", "hold": "", "witness": "none -- the escape is MEASURED as passing (a mutant of EXPEVT_TLB_PROT_LD scores 31/31), but the row that would catch it does not exist yet and needs a user-mode instruction fetch.", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "entries": [{"seat": "fable5", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-22", "note": "From #454's ask-again, after the kill table was clean. FILED rather than fixed under the floor rules: the mutation is in the general TLB table not at #454's call site, and #454's detector is already above the vacuity floor with (a)(b)(c) green, so this is coverage DEBT. One user-mode row would close this AND a second escape named independently at pass 1 ('return 2 on any UTLB hit'), which is invisible for the same reason -- the caller treats 1 and 2 alike."}, {"seat": "fable5", "phase": "regress", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "*** A THIRD FAMILY MEMBER, found by attacking #454's detector after its kill table was clean, and it is coverage debt rather than a reopening. *** Delete the early `return` at cpu_sh_instr.c:3761 -- call KEPT, abort REMOVED, which is vacuity-floor clause (d)'s own shape. It raises the fault and then completes the flush anyway, and it passes all 34 rows: on the miss rows `paddr` is an UNINITIALISED LOCAL, because the tlb_miss path never writes *return_paddr, so the payload lands at unpinnable stack garbage; and the deterministic protection path is privileged-unreachable post-fix. IT CLOSES WITH THE ROW THIS ROW ALREADY OWES: the same user-mode PR=00 row that closes shprotld's two existing escapes closes this one too -- PROVIDED that row pins BOTH observables, EXPEVT = 0x0a0 AND the destination untouched. It must pin both anyway for the return-2-on-any-hit escape, so the marginal cost of the third member is zero. Note the shape: an uninitialised local is what makes the escape invisible, so a row that checks only the EXCEPTION and not the DESTINATION cannot see it -- which is the one-observable-channel hazard the oracle-correction rule already names."}], "state_note": "*** A FARMED grok JOB CONFIRMED THIS ROW 2026-08-26 AND ADDED A COMPANION GAP THE ROW DID NOT NAME. ***\n\n**CONFIRMED, by enumeration rather than impression:** the `EXPEVT_TLB_PROT_LD` arm is privileged-unreachable on every SH TLB row in the tree. The seat read every SH TLB probe end-to-end and listed each row's SR/MD rather than inferring privilege from comments.\n\n**ONE PREMISE OF MY BRIEF CORRECTED:** the read-side check is `translate_via_mmu()` in `src/cpus/memory_sh.c`, **not** a separate helper in `cpu_sh.c` -- `cpu_sh.c` only CONSUMES the code after it is chosen. Two callers: the P0/P3 arm of `sh_translate_v2p()` (ordinary load/store/fetch) and `sh_translate_sq_v2p()` from `X(pref_rn)` with `flags=0`.\n\n**THE COMPANION GAP, and it is the useful addition: PRIVILEGED `PROT_ST` IS ALSO UNTESTED.** No row issues a write-type translation against a read-only page; PREF passes `flags=0`. So the row's read-side framing is right but narrow -- the store side has the same hole, and a round that builds a user-mode read row should cover both while the apparatus is out.\n\n*** AND A SEAT-FAILURE MODE I HAVE NOT SEEN BEFORE TODAY: THE SEAT REFUTED A SENTENCE THAT DOES NOT EXIST. *** It reported an *'attack on the filed 0x180 sentence'*, arguing that a user P1 fetch is `0x040` and that `0x180` is `EXPEVT_RES_INST`. **The arithmetic is CORRECT -- verified, `sh4_exception.h:71` defines `EXPEVT_RES_INST 0x180` -- and the TARGET IS IMAGINARY.** Searched `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md`, `ledger.json`, `CHANGELOG.md` and every `regress/*.py` with a word-boundary pattern: the only `0x180` in the tree is an unrelated code offset in a gdrom probe. No record makes the claim.\n\n**That is the MIRROR of the `gdromdmawidth` farm result the same hour**, where agy re-derived a finding the tree already held and reached a WORSE conclusion because the constraints lived in a comment it had not been given. Same underlying cost, opposite direction: **a seat briefed without the existing record spends its effort on the wrong target** -- either re-deriving what is known, or refuting what was never claimed.\n\n*The seat was scrupulous about its own limits and that is why this is cheap to correct: it labelled the 31/31 figure READ FROM THE FILING rather than re-measured, and said plainly it did not re-run the mutant because it had no build.*\n\n**STILL NEEDS A BUILD** -- a user-mode read row plus (now) a privileged write-type row. Queued behind the machine embargo with the analysis done.\n\n"}
{"id": "asicarray4", "title": "asicarray4  sizing the ASIC's four register arrays [4] instead of [3] would de-fang the whole out-of-bounds class", "state": "open", "commits": "", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "hold": "", "witness": "none -- design proposal, not a defect", "state_note": "RAISED BY THE kimi SEAT on the #463 pass-2 panel (_scratchpad/panel_20260825_020219), via `kimi-k3:cloud` because the kimi CLI is quota-walled -- a DIFFERENT TRANSPORT, recorded as a substitute. The struct declares `pending_irq[3]`, `mask_13[3]`, `mask_11[3]`, `mask_9[3]` consecutively, so `mask_13[4]` IS `mask_11[1]` and any mask widening corrupts a neighbour silently. Padding each to [4] converts that class from CORRUPTION back to mere silent wrongness at a cost of 16 bytes. **The seat was explicit that this is a TRADE, not a fix**, and that framing is the reason to keep it: it does not make a wrong index right, it only stops it reaching another array. Weigh against `asicindexcase`, which it would also partly de-fang.  *** MEASURED AND PARTLY REFUTED 2026-08-25 by the opus5 research seat, which had first FAVOURED this option and then argued against it. *** Sizing the arrays [4] makes the `+1` translation mutant **ESCAPE** `mask9 rt`. The row's kill mechanism is that DEVICE_TICK ERASES `asserted_13` (`d->asserted_13 = ... = 0`, loop bound i<3) and the mask_9 write arm calls the tick immediately -- so at [3] the stored 0xb2 lands on asserted_13, is wiped, and the readback reddens the row. **At [4], index 3 is a real slot the tick never touches, the value survives, and the mutant escapes**: N=3 readback 0x0 (RED, killed) vs N=4 readback 0xb2 (GREEN, ESCAPES). So this option **costs a mutant kill and needs a replacement row before it can ship** -- the original filing (\"de-fangs the whole OOB class for 16 bytes\") understated its cost and is corrected here. Filing it rather than shipping it was right for a reason the filing did not know.  *** DEAD BY THREE INDEPENDENT ROUTES, which is the strongest refutation this round produced for any alternative. *** (i) agy's phantom-state argument -- it invents hardware that does not exist and leaves the tick loop ignoring the fourth word; (ii) opus5's MEASUREMENT -- it makes the `+1` mutant ESCAPE `mask9 rt`, because that row's kill works by the tick erasing the cell and [4] gives index 3 a real slot the tick never touches; (iii) codex's HARDWARE ORACLE -- Linux's mach-dreamcast IRQ code documents three status words and three per mask group, and KallistiOS exposes exactly +0/+4/+8 with no fourth. **The three-word bound is a HARDWARE FACT, not an implementation choice.** Three seats, three methods, one conclusion -- and note this round holds NO Holly ASIC documentation of its own, so route (iii) is the first external oracle it ever obtained."}
{"id": "sh4frqcr", "title": "sh4frqcr  FRQCR is a bare latch with no reset value, so a guest reads back 0x0000 -- not a legal value for the register hardware derives pclock FROM", "state": "open", "commits": "", "hold": "", "witness": "harness:READ from the shipped source and `_scratchpad/sh7750_hardware.txt` (SH7750 Hardware Manual Rev.7.00), 2026-08-30.  `dev_sh4.c` declares `uint16_t cpg_frqcr;` and touches it in exactly two places -- the write arm stores `idata`, the read arm returns it -- with NO initialiser anywhere, so it is zero from the `memset` at device construction.", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "entries": [{"seat": "fable5", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "dev_sh4.c's `case SH4_CPG_FRQCR` models FRQCR as a plain store/load with no initialisation, so a guest reads 0x0000. SH7750 Hardware Manual Rev.7.00 Table 10.3 gives the legal initial values as H'0E1A / H'0E23 / H'0E13 / H'0E0A depending on clock mode. THIS IS THE HONEST LONG-RUN ANSWER TO sh4pclock: real hardware derives the peripheral clock from FRQCR and the mode pins, and a seat measured that the CPU:peripheral ratio in that table is only ever 4 or 6 -- calibrated against dreamcast, whose 200 MHz / 50 MHz = 4 is a legal mode this tree independently knows. A round here could make pclock DERIVED rather than assigned. Note the bound is not enough on its own: machine_hpcsh.c sets no emulated_hz at all, so the tree does not even claim a Jornada CPU clock to divide."}, {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "*** WITNESS CONSTRUCTIBLE AT RUNG 3, and the MANUAL confirms the value is wrong. *** dev_sh4.c:2715-2720 is a bare latch -- `odata = d->cpg_frqcr` with no reset value written at init, so a guest reads back whatever DEVINIT's memset left, i.e. 0x0000. THE MANUAL, READ LOCALLY rather than taken from a search summary: _scratchpad/sh7750_hardware.txt:18607-18608 reads 'FRQCR is initialized only by a power-on reset via the RESET pin. The initial value of each bit is determined by the clock operating mode', and the bit table immediately below at :18610-18614 gives initial values CKOEN=1 (bit 11), PLL1EN=1 (bit 10), PLL2EN=1 (bit 9) with bits 15-12 = 0. So the reset value carries at least 0x0E00 and is NEVER 0x0000. The register table at :18476 marks the reset column 'Undefined*'; :18607 is the footnote that explains the asterisk, so 'undefined' means MODE-DEPENDENT, not zero -- a distinction that would have been easy to read the wrong way from the table alone. A guest reading FRQCR therefore sees both PLLs disabled and clock output disabled, the opposite of the reset state. WITNESS: one 16-bit guest load from 0xffc00000 on an unmodified SH-4 machine. HONEST LIMIT, the same shape as sh4pcic15's: the model has no clock-operating-mode concept, so a round must DECIDE what to present rather than read one value off the manual. The three bits above are mode-INDEPENDENT and settle the floor; IFC2 and the divider bits are not."}], "note": "*** THIS ROW WAS A 4-CHARACTER STUB.  Filled 2026-08-30 from the manual, which CONFIRMS the claim and SHARPENS it: the defect is not merely 'no reset value', it is that THREE BITS ARE ARCHITECTURALLY 1 AT RESET AND THE MODEL READS THEM BACK 0. ***  **THE MANUAL, section 10.4.1, quoted from PROSE rather than a figure** (the extracted tables in this file stagger names against values; the prose does not):  *\"FRQCR is initialized only by a power-on reset via the RESET pin.  The initial value of each bit is determined by the clock operating mode.\"*  So there is no single constant -- **but the bit table is unambiguous about which bits do NOT depend on the mode**, and it is aligned rather than staggered (eight initial values against eight bit positions):  bits 15-12 are Reserved, initial 0, *\"always read as 0\"*; **bit 11 CKOEN = 1, bit 10 PLL1EN = 1, bit 9 PLL2EN = 1**; bit 8 IFC2 and bits 7-0 (IFC1/IFC0, BFC2-0, PFC2-0) are mode-dependent and printed as `--`.  **SO THE MINIMUM ARCHITECTURAL RESET VALUE IS 0x0E00** (0x800 | 0x400 | 0x200), and the model returns 0x0000 -- which tells a guest that **clock output is disabled and BOTH PLL circuits are off**, a state the hardware cannot be in after a power-on reset.  **THE FIX IS ONE INITIALISER**, and its only design question is what to do with the mode-dependent low bits: either pick the divider ratios matching the clock mode GXemul models and say which, or set 0x0E00 and state in a comment that bits 8-0 are left zero because the emulator models no CKIO mode.  *The second is honest and cheap; the first needs a mode decision this row should not make silently.*  **SEVERITY, stated rather than assumed: UNKNOWN GUEST IMPACT.**  No rig here is known to read FRQCR, so this is a correctness defect with no measured symptom -- it belongs BELOW the host-kill rows and above cosmetic work.  A guest computing peripheral clock rates from these dividers would divide by zero-derived ratios, which is the shape worth watching for, but nothing in this tree has been observed doing it."}
