NOTE *** FOUND 2026-08-25 WHILE VERIFYING A PREMISE IN THE `gdrompionull` PASS-1 BRIEF, NOT WHILE LOOKING FOR IT. *** The brief's Q4 asked, almost as an afterthought, whether `d->cur_data_offset` is reset anywhere -- because the round had reasoned about `data_len` throughout and barely looked at the guard's OTHER operand. It is reset (`:327`, beside the NULL). But reading the guard to check that showed something else. *** THE GUARD CHECKS ONE BYTE AND THE BODY READS TWO. *** Verified by reading, three lines:   (1) `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:611` -- `if (d->cur_data_offset < d->data_len) {`   (2) `:612-613` -- `odata = d->data[d->cur_data_offset ++];` then `odata |= (d->data[d->cur_data_offset ++] << 8);` -- **two increments, one bounds test**   (3) `alloc_data` `:309` -- `malloc(d->data_len)`, **exactly `data_len` bytes, no padding, no rounding**. So at `cur_data_offset == data_len - 1` the guard PASSES, the first read is the last valid byte, and **the second read is `d->data[data_len]` -- one byte past the allocation**. *** REACHABILITY, STATED AS WHAT IS READ AND WHAT IS OWED. *** READ: `data_len = d->cnt` (`:307`), and `d->cnt` is GUEST-WRITABLE A BYTE AT A TIME -- `:704` sets the low byte from a CNTLO store and `:712` the high byte from CNTHI, so an ODD count is expressible. The two subsequent adjustments do not remove oddness: `:409-410` only replaces `cnt` when it is ZERO, and `:486-487` only rewrites the single value 65536. **OWED: a measurement that an odd `cnt` actually survives to `alloc_data` on a path a guest can drive.** The existing `gdrom_pio_null_probe.py` already stages commands through these registers and could answer it with two extra stores; I have NOT run it, and this row does not claim the defect is reachable until that runs. *** WHY THIS IS A DIFFERENT DEFECT FROM `gdrompionull` AND MUST NOT BE FOLDED INTO IT. *** That row is `data` NULL while `data_len` lies -- a state-invariant violation whose fix is one assignment in the tail. This is an OFF-BY