3043:   "state_note": "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE, and THE ROW COVERS TWO DEFECTS OF DIFFERENT SEVERITY -- which is the shape that buries one when the other closes. Split before working it. ***\n\n**HALF 1 -- LIVE.** A small `.iso` (El Torito sized, ~1.44 MB) is typed `DISKIMAGE_FLOPPY` while `is_a_cdrom == 1`. The conjunction is the defect: the guest's SCSI/IDE probes miss it, so **the CD-ROM is not on the bus at all**. The host banner shows it (`FLOPPY CD-ROM ... 1440 KB`) which is why it reads as cosmetic and is not.\n\n**HALF 2 -- LATENT.** The `d:` prefix does not behave like `s:`/`i:` here. Not guest-visible; a prefix asymmetry. **It should NOT ride along as a second guest defect.**\n\n*** A FIX OWES THE ORACLE-CORRECTION ORDER, AND THE ROW THAT MAKES IT SO WAS VERIFIED HERE. *** `regress/diff_diskimage_parse.c:293` ships\n  `check(\"[RECORD] bare 1.44M *.iso is FLOPPY-typed (filed defect)\", ...)`\nand `regress/gate_offline.sh:1693-1694` gates on that record being present. **The shipped detector PINS TODAY'S WRONG TYPE on purpose**, and `:290` says so in its own comment (*'becomes a FLOPPY CD-ROM, doubly unreachable. Filed separately'*).\n\n> So the sequence is fixed by rule: **RE-PIN THE ROW FIRST, measure the committed build RED under the inverted expectation, THEN change the typing heuristic.** Doing it the other way round is observationally identical to fitting the test to the binary.\n\n**Candidate fix and its warrant, both already in the tree:** the heuristic gains `&& !d->is_a_cdrom` (a one-liner already written down in `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md`), and the transferred warrant is the existing `&& !prefix_d` guard on `.iso`/`.cdr` at `diskimage.c:1852`. `#418` already moved `d:`'s type assignment after the recalc, which does NOT rescue a small ISO -- worth knowing, because it looks like it should.\n\n"
3852:   "state_note": "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: the MECHANISM is TRUE, the SEVERITY IS NIL, and the tree already says so 600 lines from where the seat was looking. ***\n\n**True as stated:** the `SH4_TSTR` arm (`dev_sh4.c:1929-1948`) starts a timer on a write with no `timer_hz` guard anywhere in it.\n\n**But that state was already measured and recorded as harmless**, in this file, by the round that placed the guard (`:2062-2064`):\n  > *\"A seat measured the third option (store idata, skip the prescaler switch, leave timer_hz at 0) as SAFE -- no division by zero, no UNF, no pending interrupt.\"*\n\n**And it is safe STRUCTURALLY, not incidentally:** `timer_hz` is the NUMERATOR at `:481`, `step = (uint32_t)(d->timer_hz[i] / SH4_PSEUDO_TIMER_HZ)`, so a zero can only make `step` zero -- it can never be a divisor. `:2071-2073` adds that `timer_hz[]` is already 0.0 from DEVINIT's memset, and those four prescaler cases are its only writes in the file.\n\n**DISPOSITION: assessed, NOT a defect.** A started timer at 0 Hz that does not advance is the CORRECT behaviour, not a symptom -- so a detector keyed on TCNT0 failing to advance would pin the right answer as though it were a bug.\n\n*The farmed seat confirmed the mechanism and could not have known the disposition: the assessment lives ~600 lines away in the same file. This is why a seat answer is verified locally before it is recorded, never transcribed.*\n\n"
4429:     "note": "FLAGSHIP ADJUDICATION, queue item 7, batched. Badge `s` disclosed: same-model review of the main loop's records, not a two-family check. Re-verified every code claim by grep/read; boots and mutant builds attributed to the measure seat rather than re-run.\n* *** THE FINDING IS A FLAW IN THE LADDER IT WROTE THIS MORNING: THE RUNG ORDER AND THE PRIORITY ORDER DIVERGE. *** b120r's witness is rung 4 (an instrumented boot) while armbdt's is rung 3 (a probe), so the ladder's clause 'order items by the strongest witness that exists TODAY' puts b120r FIRST. That is wrong for scheduling: b120r's measured guest-visible symptom is NIL (told-98 and told-35 give byte-identical transcripts) while armbdt CORRUPTS GUEST DATA. RUNG MEASURES REACHABILITY-PROOF STRENGTH, NOT HARM. The carrier clause is corrected.\nCONFIRM close-as-design, BUT THE WITNESS WORDING WAS STALE AND IS CORRECTED. The defect is real as a SHAPE -- reload_timer_value always calls timer_add(freq,...) regardless of the FREERUN/PERIODIC bit, and DEVICE_TICK(footbridge)'s own comment admits 'I thought I had solved this, but it didn't quite work. TODO.'\n* *** 'No witness of either kind' IS STALE and must not read as 'unreachable'. *** regress/footbridge_sites_probe.py is COMMITTED and drives TIMER_1_LOAD through real guest instructions on an unmodified CATS, and the MIPS-compare shape is boot-travelled on every pmax/arc boot. The path IS witnessable. What is absent is a SYMPTOM, because #427's clamp removed the harmful half. Precise classification: REACHABLE, SYMPTOMLESS-BY-CONSTRUCTION -- correctly filed as design, not round-eligible. Disposition unchanged."
4900:     "note": "MEMORY / DEVICE-ACCESS CLUSTER TRIAGE: six rows, each filed by ONE seat and never reviewed. Brief generated mechanically from the ledger.\n\n*** THE CLEAREST DROP OF THE CAMPAIGN: `mrwgrind`, FOUR SEATS (agy, glm, deepseek, minimax). ***  minimax's reason is the one that decides it -- 'unreachable' -- and unlike the constblind drop this one does NOT rest on a claim inside the row that everyone read back to me. It rests on the shape of the code path.\n\nWHAT TO DO FIRST -- three-way split:\n  mrwstore2  glm and minimax, and their reason is the strongest severity claim in the packet: 'real guest-reachable DATA CORRUPTION via double device write' on a straddling store whose tail faults. Under this project's ordering rule -- severity and reachability, never rung -- that outranks the others on its face.\n  ns16550    agy, for IMMEDIATE fix. A guest-writable divisor dividing by zero is UB, though the row itself concedes it is 'not in the shipped path'.\n  pcchalt    deepseek, and agy would schedule it as a DETECTOR round rather than a fix -- which is the right category: #431's deleted PPC page-crossing halt has no detector, so the question is whether its removal can be noticed, not whether to remove it again.\n\nALSO DROPPED: `devexit`, two seats (glm, deepseek). Note the tension worth recording -- devexit is the GENERAL question behind correction #438, which fixed one instance (a guest store to luna88k's INT_ST_MASK ended the emulator process), and this project already shipped census_exits.py counting 398 exit sites with 120 inside device handlers. A drop here means 'the general row is not the unit of work', NOT 'the exits are fine'.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable. Codex RATE-LIMITED, eighth panel today.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: KEPT. No seat prioritised it and none argued to drop it. It is an AUDIT row -- device handlers that ignore the access length -- and audits are the shape most likely to go stale, as constblind demonstrated tonight when a seven-differential audit was quoted against a thirteen-differential tree.",
5351:     "note": "MEMORY / DEVICE-ACCESS CLUSTER TRIAGE: six rows, each filed by ONE seat and never reviewed. Brief generated mechanically from the ledger.\n\n*** THE CLEAREST DROP OF THE CAMPAIGN: `mrwgrind`, FOUR SEATS (agy, glm, deepseek, minimax). ***  minimax's reason is the one that decides it -- 'unreachable' -- and unlike the constblind drop this one does NOT rest on a claim inside the row that everyone read back to me. It rests on the shape of the code path.\n\nWHAT TO DO FIRST -- three-way split:\n  mrwstore2  glm and minimax, and their reason is the strongest severity claim in the packet: 'real guest-reachable DATA CORRUPTION via double device write' on a straddling store whose tail faults. Under this project's ordering rule -- severity and reachability, never rung -- that outranks the others on its face.\n  ns16550    agy, for IMMEDIATE fix. A guest-writable divisor dividing by zero is UB, though the row itself concedes it is 'not in the shipped path'.\n  pcchalt    deepseek, and agy would schedule it as a DETECTOR round rather than a fix -- which is the right category: #431's deleted PPC page-crossing halt has no detector, so the question is whether its removal can be noticed, not whether to remove it again.\n\nALSO DROPPED: `devexit`, two seats (glm, deepseek). Note the tension worth recording -- devexit is the GENERAL question behind correction #438, which fixed one instance (a guest store to luna88k's INT_ST_MASK ended the emulator process), and this project already shipped census_exits.py counting 398 exit sites with 120 inside device handlers. A drop here means 'the general row is not the unit of work', NOT 'the exits are fine'.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable. Codex RATE-LIMITED, eighth panel today.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: DROP RECOMMENDED -- two seats. RECORD THE TENSION: this is the GENERAL row behind #438, which fixed one real instance, and census_exits.py counted 398 exit sites with 120 inside device handlers. The drop means 'the general row is not the unit of work', never 'the exits are fine'. If it closes, the census must stay as the standing answer. NOT EXECUTED: codex-held.",
6506:     "note": "TIMER-DOMAIN TRIAGE PANEL: eight rows, each filed by ONE seat and never reviewed. The brief was generated MECHANICALLY FROM THE LEDGER so every inlined fact is the filing seat's own text rather than my transcription of it -- adopted after this session shipped six wrong records in one round by retyping claims.\n\n*** NO CONSENSUS ON WHAT TO DO FIRST, AND THE SPLIT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN VOTED: ***\n  i8253zero first  -- agy ('a document-settled functional bug that FREEZES EMULATED GUEST CLOCKS') and glm ('the only clean wrong-record -- datasheet vs code -- in the packet').\n  fbpending first  -- deepseek, and kimi leaning the same way.\n  capgap first     -- minimax (correct R4's wrong record, then raise reject/drop/lag to policy).\nThe two seats that named i8253zero include the FILE-READING seat, and its reason is the only one in the packet that names a guest-visible symptom (a frozen clock) rather than a records or design concern. That is the ordering rule this project uses -- severity and reachability, never rung, never vote -- so i8253zero is the recorded lead.\n\n*** THE DROPS ARE WHERE THE PANEL AGREED, and drops are the point of a triage brief: ***\n  fbextrate  DROP -- THREE seats (agy, deepseek, minimax). agy's reason is the sharpest: 'an ungrounded heuristic under an acknowledged hardware unknown'.\n  sigunsafe  DROP as a ROUND -- two seats (agy, deepseek). agy: 'theoretical UB with zero observable guest harm'.\nNeither drop is executed here. Both rows are codex-held, and this session already had one closure refused as CLOSED-WHILE-HELD for exactly this reason: 'unanimous among the seats that ANSWERED' is how a six-seat verdict gets reported as a nine-seat one.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable (agy 14.6 KB, grok 14.3 KB, kimi 62.7 KB, glm 15.0 KB, deepseek 5.7 KB, minimax 9.9 KB). Codex RATE-LIMITED, fifth panel today.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: THIRD. One seat would do it first, framing it as correcting R4's wrong record and then raising reject/drop/lag to a POLICY question -- which is the honest shape, since the header already concedes the domain admits 32x more than the cap can serve and calls closing that gap a policy question filed rather than answered.",
6610:     "note": "TIMER-DOMAIN TRIAGE PANEL: eight rows, each filed by ONE seat and never reviewed. The brief was generated MECHANICALLY FROM THE LEDGER so every inlined fact is the filing seat's own text rather than my transcription of it -- adopted after this session shipped six wrong records in one round by retyping claims.\n\n*** NO CONSENSUS ON WHAT TO DO FIRST, AND THE SPLIT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN VOTED: ***\n  i8253zero first  -- agy ('a document-settled functional bug that FREEZES EMULATED GUEST CLOCKS') and glm ('the only clean wrong-record -- datasheet vs code -- in the packet').\n  fbpending first  -- deepseek, and kimi leaning the same way.\n  capgap first     -- minimax (correct R4's wrong record, then raise reject/drop/lag to policy).\nThe two seats that named i8253zero include the FILE-READING seat, and its reason is the only one in the packet that names a guest-visible symptom (a frozen clock) rather than a records or design concern. That is the ordering rule this project uses -- severity and reachability, never rung, never vote -- so i8253zero is the recorded lead.\n\n*** THE DROPS ARE WHERE THE PANEL AGREED, and drops are the point of a triage brief: ***\n  fbextrate  DROP -- THREE seats (agy, deepseek, minimax). agy's reason is the sharpest: 'an ungrounded heuristic under an acknowledged hardware unknown'.\n  sigunsafe  DROP as a ROUND -- two seats (agy, deepseek). agy: 'theoretical UB with zero observable guest harm'.\nNeither drop is executed here. Both rows are codex-held, and this session already had one closure refused as CLOSED-WHILE-HELD for exactly this reason: 'unanimous among the seats that ANSWERED' is how a six-seat verdict gets reported as a nine-seat one.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable (agy 14.6 KB, grok 14.3 KB, kimi 62.7 KB, glm 15.0 KB, deepseek 5.7 KB, minimax 9.9 KB). Codex RATE-LIMITED, fifth panel today.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: KEEP, no seat prioritised it. Closely related to capgap -- both concern the catch-up budget -- but the brief