6714:     "note": "TIMER-DOMAIN TRIAGE PANEL: eight rows, each filed by ONE seat and never reviewed. The brief was generated MECHANICALLY FROM THE LEDGER so every inlined fact is the filing seat's own text rather than my transcription of it -- adopted after this session shipped six wrong records in one round by retyping claims.\n\n*** NO CONSENSUS ON WHAT TO DO FIRST, AND THE SPLIT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN VOTED: ***\n  i8253zero first  -- agy ('a document-settled functional bug that FREEZES EMULATED GUEST CLOCKS') and glm ('the only clean wrong-record -- datasheet vs code -- in the packet').\n  fbpending first  -- deepseek, and kimi leaning the same way.\n  capgap first     -- minimax (correct R4's wrong record, then raise reject/drop/lag to policy).\nThe two seats that named i8253zero include the FILE-READING seat, and its reason is the only one in the packet that names a guest-visible symptom (a frozen clock) rather than a records or design concern. That is the ordering rule this project uses -- severity and reachability, never rung, never vote -- so i8253zero is the recorded lead.\n\n*** THE DROPS ARE WHERE THE PANEL AGREED, and drops are the point of a triage brief: ***\n  fbextrate  DROP -- THREE seats (agy, deepseek, minimax). agy's reason is the sharpest: 'an ungrounded heuristic under an acknowledged hardware unknown'.\n  sigunsafe  DROP as a ROUND -- two seats (agy, deepseek). agy: 'theoretical UB with zero observable guest harm'.\nNeither drop is executed here. Both rows are codex-held, and this session already had one closure refused as CLOSED-WHILE-HELD for exactly this reason: 'unanimous among the seats that ANSWERED' is how a six-seat verdict gets reported as a nine-seat one.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable (agy 14.6 KB, grok 14.3 KB, kimi 62.7 KB, glm 15.0 KB, deepseek 5.7 KB, minimax 9.9 KB). Codex RATE-LIMITED, fifth panel today.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: KEEP. Related to rtcflood and wdcflood; all three are now re-read under the `floodclass` row, which measured the class at 72 device files carrying fatal() with no latch idiom at all. Fixing these one at a time is what #337 already tried inside a single file, and it still missed a path.",
6827:     "note": "TIMER-DOMAIN TRIAGE PANEL: eight rows, each filed by ONE seat and never reviewed. The brief was generated MECHANICALLY FROM THE LEDGER so every inlined fact is the filing seat's own text rather than my transcription of it -- adopted after this session shipped six wrong records in one round by retyping claims.\n\n*** NO CONSENSUS ON WHAT TO DO FIRST, AND THE SPLIT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN VOTED: ***\n  i8253zero first  -- agy ('a document-settled functional bug that FREEZES EMULATED GUEST CLOCKS') and glm ('the only clean wrong-record -- datasheet vs code -- in the packet').\n  fbpending first  -- deepseek, and kimi leaning the same way.\n  capgap first     -- minimax (correct R4's wrong record, then raise reject/drop/lag to policy).\nThe two seats that named i8253zero include the FILE-READING seat, and its reason is the only one in the packet that names a guest-visible symptom (a frozen clock) rather than a records or design concern. That is the ordering rule this project uses -- severity and reachability, never rung, never vote -- so i8253zero is the recorded lead.\n\n*** THE DROPS ARE WHERE THE PANEL AGREED, and drops are the point of a triage brief: ***\n  fbextrate  DROP -- THREE seats (agy, deepseek, minimax). agy's reason is the sharpest: 'an ungrounded heuristic under an acknowledged hardware unknown'.\n  sigunsafe  DROP as a ROUND -- two seats (agy, deepseek). agy: 'theoretical UB with zero observable guest harm'.\nNeither drop is executed here. Both rows are codex-held, and this session already had one closure refused as CLOSED-WHILE-HELD for exactly this reason: 'unanimous among the seats that ANSWERED' is how a six-seat verdict gets reported as a nine-seat one.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable (agy 14.6 KB, grok 14.3 KB, kimi 62.7 KB, glm 15.0 KB, deepseek 5.7 KB, minimax 9.9 KB). Codex RATE-LIMITED, fifth panel today.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: DROP RECOMMENDED as a round -- two seats. 'Theoretical UB with zero observable guest harm' (agy). NOT EXECUTED: the row is codex-held, and a drop is a closure.",
7087:     "note": "MEMORY / DEVICE-ACCESS CLUSTER TRIAGE: six rows, each filed by ONE seat and never reviewed. Brief generated mechanically from the ledger.\n\n*** THE CLEAREST DROP OF THE CAMPAIGN: `mrwgrind`, FOUR SEATS (agy, glm, deepseek, minimax). ***  minimax's reason is the one that decides it -- 'unreachable' -- and unlike the constblind drop this one does NOT rest on a claim inside the row that everyone read back to me. It rests on the shape of the code path.\n\nWHAT TO DO FIRST -- three-way split:\n  mrwstore2  glm and minimax, and their reason is the strongest severity claim in the packet: 'real guest-reachable DATA CORRUPTION via double device write' on a straddling store whose tail faults. Under this project's ordering rule -- severity and reachability, never rung -- that outranks the others on its face.\n  ns16550    agy, for IMMEDIATE fix. A guest-writable divisor dividing by zero is UB, though the row itself concedes it is 'not in the shipped path'.\n  pcchalt    deepseek, and agy would schedule it as a DETECTOR round rather than a fix -- which is the right category: #431's deleted PPC page-crossing halt has no detector, so the question is whether its removal can be noticed, not whether to remove it again.\n\nALSO DROPPED: `devexit`, two seats (glm, deepseek). Note the tension worth recording -- devexit is the GENERAL question behind correction #438, which fixed one instance (a guest store to luna88k's INT_ST_MASK ended the emulator process), and this project already shipped census_exits.py counting 398 exit sites with 120 inside device handlers. A drop here means 'the general row is not the unit of work', NOT 'the exits are fine'.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable. Codex RATE-LIMITED, eighth panel today.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: *** DROP -- FOUR SEATS, and it is the cleanest drop of the campaign. ***  A length near SIZE_MAX grinding rather than being declined is unreachable, and the verdict rests on the code path rather than on a claim inside the row. NOT EXECUTED: codex-held, and a drop is a closure.",
7143:   "hold": "*** THE OLD BLOCKING REASON IS REFUTED AND WAS AN L4 -- corrected 2026-08-26. *** It read 'the fix may itself DIVERGE from real hardware'. **A fix cannot diverge, because the behaviour is PERMITTED and never REQUIRED.** Four PowerPC manuals are now local in `_scratchpad/` (OEA Book III v2.01, VEA Book II v2.01, MPCFPE32B Rev 2 -- the 32-bit PEM covering the PPC750 this row's witness uses -- and the 2005 32/64-bit PEM), fetched and extracted with the same pdftotext used for ddi0100i.txt. PEM 3.2.2 / Book II 2.1: a misaligned store is not atomic, so it falls in the 'any other load or store instruction MAY be partially executed and then aborted... and then re-executed' bucket; 6.1.4 says 'some bytes just before the boundary may have been updated'. **So an emulator that never partially executes is conformant under every one of them -- the INTERSECTION of both readings, not a divergence from either.** *** AND THE MANUALS DISAGREE ON THE CASE THIS ROW IS ACTUALLY ABOUT, recorded as an honest UNKNOWN rather than settled by the stronger document: *** Book III v2.01 4.2.4.1 says that for an access whose first byte is Caching-Inhibited AND Guarded -- exactly the MMIO-head shape here -- a DSI means 'the portion of the storage operand that is in Caching Inhibited and Guarded storage is NOT accessed', i.e. the device write must not happen. The PEM contains no such rule and its 6.1.4 explicitly permits the head bytes to have been updated. Whether a real PPC750 suppresses the device write is NOT established. THE REASONS THAT SURVIVE and on which this row stays filed: REACHABILITY (zero straddling stores measured across three real boots) and SEVERITY (a duplicated device write -- guest-visible, no host kill, no memory-safety consequence; both PowerPC and ARM put the burden on software). *** SCOPE CORRECTION THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE BLOCKER: 'memory_rw is the choke point' is FALSE FOR THE CLASS. *** stmw/stswi/lmw/lswi and ARM's bdt_store each loop and return on the first failing element, so they have the identical partial-completion-across-a-fault property at the INSTRUCTION level, entirely outside memory_rw -- and PEM 6.1.4's first bullet is about exactly those. **A memory_rw fix would be a partial fix that reads as a complete one**, which is the argument for filing this properly rather than shipping a round a future reader mistakes for closure. TWO SIDE-EFFECT CAUTIONS FROM A PRIOR SEAT MEASURED ABSENT IN THIS TREE: FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS returns before every SPR write and before ppc_exception(), and there is NO R/C writeback on PPC at all -- so the recorded warning about PPC603 software reload describes hazards that do not exist here. FILED SEPARATELY, all from the same reading: `X(stvx)` does a raw 16-byte memory_rw with no `EA & ~0xF` mask (a second straddling producer, and a divergence in its own right); diff_memory_rw.c has a coverage hole exactly at this row's shape (device head + FAULTING tail); and memory_ppc.c:302-305 is an unlatched guest-drivable fatal(), one per translation fault -- the floodclass shape #464 just closed six of.",
7164:     "note": "agy answered this panel. LEAD CANDIDATE on SEVERITY, from two seats: 'real guest-reachable DATA CORRUPTION via double device write' when a straddling store's tail faults. This project orders by severity and reachability, never by rung, so a corruption claim outranks the alternatives on its face -- but the claim itself has not been re-measured here and should be, since memory_rw has been corrected twice recently (#47, #430/#431) and the surrounding code has moved.",
